response.text:
{
  "releases": [
    {
      "date": "2026-08-19",
      "title": "Bank of Kestria holds policy rate at four per cent",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank of Kestria today maintained its policy interest rate at 4.00 per cent. The decision balances slower price growth against continuing pressure from service costs. Annual consumer price inflation fell to 2.8 per cent in July, from 3.1 per cent in June, but remains above the Bank's 2 per cent target."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Household spending was broadly unchanged during the second quarter. Business surveys showed weaker demand for manufactured goods in Amberford and steadier activity among transport firms in Kestrel Bay. Wage growth eased, although employers continued to report difficulty filling some skilled posts."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank judged that the current rate remains appropriate while these pressures work through the economy. Further decisions will depend on evidence about inflation, wages and domestic demand. Today's decision does not change the Bank's operating arrangements for supplying short-term funds to regulated banks."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-25",
      "title": "New payment tables show wider use of instant transfers",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Instant transfers accounted for 38 per cent of electronic account payments in July, according to payment statistics published today by the Bank of Kestria. This compares with 31 per cent a year earlier. The tables cover domestic payments in Kestrian crowns and exclude cash withdrawals and transfers between a bank's own accounts."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The average instant transfer was KRN 246. Payments below KRN 100 made up just over half of transactions, reflecting their use for small purchases and shared household expenses. Providers reported that 99.3 per cent of transfers reached the receiving account within ten seconds."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank has added separate figures for rejected and returned payments to help users understand service performance. These categories do not by themselves indicate fraud or a system failure. The accompanying notes explain how providers count transactions and how revisions are recorded. All figures are available without charge."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-07",
      "title": "Bank opens consultation on clearer payment outage notices",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank of Kestria today opened a consultation on proposed rules for informing customers about payment service outages. The proposals would require regulated providers to publish an initial notice within 20 minutes of confirming a significant interruption and to update it at least once an hour."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Notices would need to explain which services are affected, whether customers should retry a payment and what alternatives remain available. Providers would also have to publish a closing notice when normal service resumes. The proposed rules would apply to both planned maintenance and unexpected failures, with different notice requirements for each."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The consultation draws on the Bank's review of 17 interruptions reported during the first half of this year. In six cases, public messages did not clearly identify the affected service. The Bank invites comments from payment providers, businesses and individual customers. The proposals are open for comment and are not yet binding."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-08",
      "title": "Household debt review finds uneven pressure across income groups",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Most households continued to meet their loan payments during the second quarter, but pressure increased among borrowers with smaller financial reserves, the Bank of Kestria said today. Its household debt review found that 2.4 per cent of outstanding housing loans were more than 90 days overdue, compared with 2.2 per cent in the previous quarter."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The share of overdue unsecured loans rose from 4.6 to 4.9 per cent. Borrowers reporting reduced working hours accounted for much of the increase. The review combines regulated lenders' returns with an anonymous household survey covering Saltmarsh, Ostara, Amberford, Kestrel Bay and Val-Brume."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank found no evidence of a broad withdrawal of household credit. However, lenders should assess whether repayment plans remain affordable when income falls. The review's survey estimates carry a margin of uncertainty, and differences between cities should not be treated as rankings of local financial health."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-25",
      "title": "Bank reduces policy interest rate as price pressures ease",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank of Kestria today reduced its policy interest rate by 0.25 percentage points to 3.75 per cent. Annual consumer price inflation slowed to 2.4 per cent in August, from 2.8 per cent in July. Lower goods inflation and a further easing in wage growth supported the decision."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Recent evidence suggests that domestic demand remains subdued. Retail sales volumes declined slightly over the three months to August, while businesses reported limited plans to expand their workforce. Service prices are still rising faster than the overall price index, and the Bank remains attentive to that pressure."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The reduction provides a modest adjustment as inflation moves closer to the 2 per cent target. It does not establish a fixed path for interest rates. The Bank will assess each decision using the information available at the time, including household spending, labour costs and businesses' pricing decisions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-28",
      "title": "Settlement system completes recovery exercise from Amberford backup site",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank of Kestria has completed a recovery exercise for the system that settles large payments between regulated banks. During the exercise on 27 September, staff transferred test operations from Saltmarsh to the Bank's backup site in Amberford. The exercise used simulated payment instructions and did not move customer funds."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "All 14 participating institutions connected successfully to the backup service. Processing resumed after 43 minutes, within the Bank's internal recovery limit of 60 minutes. The test included the loss of a primary communications link and a check that completed instructions could not be processed twice."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Observers identified two areas for improvement: clearer contact arrangements during staff changes and faster confirmation that every participant had received the recovery message. Neither affected the completion of the exercise. The Bank has assigned corrective actions and will track them through its operational risk programme. Normal settlement services remained available throughout."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-05",
      "title": "Financial stability review highlights commercial property refinancing risks",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Kestria's banking system has sufficient capital to absorb substantial losses, but commercial property lending warrants close attention, according to the Bank of Kestria's financial stability review published today. Regulated banks reported an aggregate core capital ratio of 15.6 per cent at the end of June, compared with 15.2 per cent a year earlier."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The review estimates that KRN 8.7 billion of commercial property loans require refinancing within twelve months. Office buildings in Saltmarsh and warehouse developments around Kestrel Bay account for a large share. Lower rental income could make refinancing harder even following September's reduction in the policy rate."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "In the Bank's adverse scenario, property values fall by 25 per cent and unemployment rises sharply. Aggregate bank capital remains above minimum requirements, although outcomes vary between lenders. The scenario is a resilience test, not a forecast. The Bank has asked lenders to strengthen records on lease expiry dates and borrower cash flows."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-13",
      "title": "Bank publishes French payment guidance for Val-Brume businesses",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank of Kestria today published a French-language guide to payment services for businesses in Val-Brume and the surrounding French-speaking region. The guide explains settlement times, payment references, refunds and the steps a business can take when money does not arrive as expected."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The publication follows six local workshops attended by 186 business owners and bookkeepers. Participants most often asked how to distinguish a pending payment from a completed transfer and how to reconcile several customer payments against one invoice. Worked examples use fictional transactions in Kestrian crowns."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The guide also explains the Bank's role in regulating payment systems and the separate responsibility of providers to resolve individual customer complaints. It includes a one-page checklist for recording a payment problem before contacting a provider. Both French and English editions are available free of charge in accessible digital formats, with printed copies supplied through the Bank's public information service."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-14",
      "title": "Quarterly survey shows inflation expectations moving closer to target",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Households expect prices to rise by 2.6 per cent over the next year, according to the median response in the Bank of Kestria's quarterly expectations survey. This is down from 2.9 per cent in the previous survey. Expectations for annual inflation three years ahead remained at 2.2 per cent."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The survey collected responses from 2,400 adults between 21 September and 2 October. Respondents in all five surveyed cities reported lower expectations for food price increases. Views on housing costs were more varied, with tenants generally expecting larger increases than households that owned their homes outright."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The collection period included the policy rate decision announced on 25 September, so the results cannot isolate its effect on public expectations. Survey responses describe what people expect, rather than measuring actual price changes. The Bank considers this evidence alongside observed inflation, wage growth and spending when assessing price pressures."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "title": "Payment providers directed to improve duplicate transfer controls",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Bank of Kestria has directed three regulated payment providers to improve controls that prevent duplicate transfers after a connection failure. A supervisory review found that some systems could accept a repeated instruction without reliably checking whether the original payment had already been completed."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The review examined 11 providers and identified 428 duplicate transfers, worth KRN 312,600, during July to September. All affected customers have been repaid. The Bank found that the weaknesses arose within the three providers' own processing systems; the central settlement system recorded the instructions it received correctly."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The directions require stronger transaction checks, clearer records of repeated instructions and independent confirmation that the changes work. The Bank is monitoring compliance directly with each provider. These technical requirements are separate from the consultation opened on 7 September about public outage notices. Customers who suspect a duplicate payment should contact their provider with the transaction references."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "releases": [
    {
      "date": "2026-08-18",
      "title": "Commission opens consultation on clearer reporting by listed companies",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission today opened a consultation on proposed changes to annual reporting rules for listed companies. The proposals would require clearer explanations of cash shortages, major customer losses and changes to accounting estimates. Responses must reach the Commission by 29 September 2026."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A review of 48 annual reports found that 17 companies used broad statements that gave investors little information about their main risks. Nine reports described available funding without explaining when loans fell due. The proposed rules would require companies to connect these figures with their plans for meeting payments."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The consultation also asks whether smaller issuers should receive additional guidance and a longer preparation period. Investors, companies, auditors and other interested parties may submit written comments. The consultation paper is available in English and French. The Commission has not decided whether to adopt the proposals and will publish a summary of responses."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-25",
      "title": "Amberford bond issuer ordered to correct missing debt disclosures",
      "author": "Dorian Pellac",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission has directed Amberford Heatworks to correct its latest half-year report after the bond issuer omitted information about a loan condition. The condition allows a lender to demand repayment if the company's cash reserves fall below KRN 6 million."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The company reported cash reserves of KRN 7.4 million at 30 June but did not explain the condition or the consequences of breaching it. The Commission considers this information necessary for investors assessing the company's ability to meet payments on its KRN 42 million bond issue."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Amberford Heatworks must publish a corrected report by 8 September and explain the changes in a separate market notice. The direction does not require early repayment of the bonds. No financial penalty has been imposed. The Commission will review the corrected report and consider whether further action is needed. Bond issuers remain responsible for explaining significant borrowing conditions in plain language."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-07",
      "title": "Commission publishes findings from review of market announcement timing",
      "author": "Selin Arvane",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission has completed a review of how quickly listed companies publish information that could affect their securities prices. Staff examined 63 announcements issued between January and June, including notices about cancelled contracts, financing agreements and significant changes to expected earnings."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "In 11 cases, companies could not provide a clear record of when senior staff first received the information. Five companies relied on a single person to approve announcements, with no cover during absences. The Commission has written to those companies requesting improvements to their procedures."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The findings do not establish that all 11 companies disclosed information late. However, incomplete records make it harder to demonstrate compliance. Companies should record key decisions, identify an alternative approver and keep disclosure arrangements under review. A briefing note published today includes practical examples. The Commission will assess the responses before deciding whether any individual case requires further investigation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-14",
      "title": "Kestrel Bay broker fined for trading ahead of client orders",
      "author": "Dorian Pellac",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission has imposed a KRN 780,000 penalty on Kestrel Bay broker Velmora Securities for using confidential client order information when trading for its own account. The firm admitted the breaches and agreed to appoint an independent reviewer of its trading controls."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Between February and May, a trading desk bought shares before executing 23 large client purchase orders. It then sold those shares after the client purchases had pushed prices higher. The conduct generated KRN 164,000 in profits. The firm must return that amount to affected clients in addition to paying the penalty."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The investigation found that staff could view pending client orders while placing trades for the firm's account, without effective checks. Velmora Securities has restricted that access. Its independent reviewer must assess the changes and report to the Commission within 90 days. The firm must also provide clients with a written explanation of how individual repayment amounts were calculated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-25",
      "title": "Commission reminds issuers of approaching annual reporting consultation deadline",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission is reminding interested parties that its consultation on clearer annual reporting closes on 29 September. The consultation, opened on 18 August, covers explanations of funding risks, major customer losses and changes to accounting estimates in listed company reports."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Commission has received 26 submissions so far, including responses from individual investors and smaller listed companies. Several respondents have asked how much detail would be expected when a company depends on one large customer. The consultation paper includes questions on this issue, and further views are welcome."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Respondents may answer any of the questions and do not need to comment on the whole paper. Submissions can be made in English or French. Anyone requesting confidential treatment should identify the relevant passages and explain why confidentiality is needed. The Commission will consider the evidence before deciding its next steps. Existing reporting requirements continue to apply while the proposals are under review."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-30",
      "title": "Corrected Amberford Heatworks report satisfies Commission disclosure direction",
      "author": "Selin Arvane",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission has completed its review of the corrected half-year report published by Amberford Heatworks on 8 September. The revised report satisfies the disclosure direction announced on 25 August concerning a loan condition linked to the company's minimum cash reserves."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The corrected report explains the KRN 6 million cash threshold, how the lender measures compliance and when repayment could be demanded. It also sets out the company's cash position at 30 June and the steps management would consider if reserves approached the threshold. The company published the required separate notice explaining the corrections."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Commission is closing its review of compliance with this direction. This does not amount to an assessment of the company's financial strength or a recommendation to buy its bonds. The case underlines the need to explain borrowing conditions alongside headline debt figures. Issuers should check that investors can understand both when obligations fall due and what could cause payment dates to change."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-06",
      "title": "Ostara workshop will explain bond disclosure duties to smaller issuers",
      "author": "Lucien Marcelet",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission will hold a free workshop in Ostara on 22 October for smaller companies that issue bonds to the public. The session will explain continuing disclosure duties, including how to report borrowing conditions, missed payments and material changes in available funding."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "There are 60 places available, with registration closing on 19 October unless the session fills earlier. Finance staff, company secretaries and directors may attend. Commission staff will use fictional examples involving a KRN 15 million bond issue and a loan approaching its repayment date. Attendance does not satisfy any formal reporting requirement."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Participants may submit general questions when registering. Staff cannot give advance approval to company announcements or discuss confidential supervisory cases at the workshop. Written materials will be available in English and French, and a French-language online session will be arranged separately. The Commission will publish the presentation materials so that issuers unable to attend can use the same guidance."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-13",
      "title": "Commission seeks court order over suspected false revenue statements",
      "author": "Dorian Pellac",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission has applied to the securities court in Saltmarsh for an order requiring Ordelune Storage to preserve accounting records and related correspondence. The application forms part of an investigation into suspected false revenue statements in the listed company's latest annual report."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Commission is examining whether KRN 18.6 million recorded as storage service revenue was supported by completed work. Its application concerns records held by the company and two former executives. The Commission has asked for an expedited hearing because some relevant electronic records are subject to routine deletion."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The allegations have not been determined by the court. The application does not seek a finding of liability or a financial penalty at this stage. Ordelune Storage and the former executives will have an opportunity to respond. The Commission will publish a further update when the court decides the application. Investors should consult the company's market announcements for its statements on the matter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-16",
      "title": "Annual reporting consultation closes with forty three written submissions",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission received 43 written submissions to its consultation on clearer annual reporting, which closed on 29 September. Non-confidential responses are now available in the consultation register. Staff are reviewing the evidence, and no decision has been made on changes to the rules."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The submissions came from 16 listed companies, 12 individual investors, seven audit practices and eight other respondents. Common topics included the cost of preparing additional explanations, the treatment of commercially sensitive customer information and the time smaller companies would need to update reporting procedures."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Several investors requested clearer links between borrowing figures and repayment dates. Some companies favoured practical guidance alongside any new requirements. The Commission will assess these views together rather than treating the number of responses supporting a proposal as a vote. A response paper will explain the conclusions and any proposed implementation arrangements. Companies should continue to prepare reports under the existing rules."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-19",
      "title": "Val-Brume review finds gaps in French bond disclosure documents",
      "author": "Lucien Marcelet",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Securities Commission has asked six bond issuers to correct French disclosure documents following a review in Val-Brume. Staff examined 28 documents made available to investors in the French-speaking region and compared key payment terms with the corresponding English versions."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Four documents omitted wording about an issuer's right to repay bonds early. Two others gave an incorrect deadline for bondholders to submit payment instructions. The affected issues have a combined face value of KRN 96 million. The review did not identify any payment already missed because of these errors."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The six issuers must publish corrected documents and notify affected holders by 30 October. The Commission has also requested evidence of checks by staff with suitable language and financial knowledge. Issuers remain responsible for the accuracy of documents prepared by outside translators. Investors who believe a discrepancy has affected a payment should contact the issuer and may also report the matter to the Commission."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "title": "Court orders preservation of records in Ordelune Storage investigation",
      "author": "Dorian Pellac",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The securities court in Saltmarsh today ordered Ordelune Storage and two former executives to preserve records relevant to the Kestria Securities Commission's investigation into suspected false revenue statements. The order follows the Commission's application announced on 13 October and takes effect immediately."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The order covers accounting entries, customer contracts, invoices and related correspondence concerning KRN 18.6 million in reported storage service revenue. Those subject to it must suspend routine deletion of relevant electronic material and retain paper records. They must provide written confirmation of the preservation steps taken within seven days."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The court has not decided whether the revenue statements were false or whether any person breached securities law. The Commission's investigation continues, and the company and former executives retain the right to respond to any allegations. Preserving the records will allow the evidence to be examined. The Commission will announce any further public proceedings when they are filed."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "releases": [
    {
      "date": "2026-08-18",
      "title": "Annual consumer price inflation eases to 3.2 per cent",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "author_role": "Head of Prices Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Consumer prices in Kestria rose by 3.2 per cent in the year to July, down from 3.5 per cent in June. Prices increased by 0.2 per cent between June and July. The annual rate measures how much the price of a representative basket of goods and services has changed over twelve months."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Slower growth in food prices made the largest contribution to the easing. Food prices were 4.1 per cent higher than a year earlier, compared with 4.8 per cent in June. Fresh vegetable prices fell during July, particularly in markets surveyed in Amberford and Ostara. Rents continued to rise."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The lower inflation rate does not mean that prices generally fell. It means they rose more slowly than before. The Kestria Statistics Office collected prices from shops, service providers and online sellers across the country. July figures include the usual seasonal adjustment to the sample of summer clothing."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-26",
      "title": "Employment grows as July unemployment rate holds steady",
      "author": "Tovan Merrow",
      "author_role": "Head of Labour Market Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Kestria had an estimated 2,846,000 people in employment in July, an increase of 7,000 from June. The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate remained at 5.4 per cent. An estimated 162,000 people were unemployed, meaning they were without work, available to start and actively seeking a job."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Employment increased most in accommodation, food services and building maintenance. Manufacturing employment changed little. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 rose to 73.1 per cent from 73.0 per cent. These estimates come from the household labour survey and cover employees and people working for themselves."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Survey responses from Kestrel Bay indicated stronger seasonal hiring, although local monthly estimates are less reliable than the national figures. The Kestria Statistics Office advises users to consider several months together. Small monthly movements may reflect sampling variation rather than a lasting change in the labour market."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-27",
      "title": "Economic output rises by 0.4 per cent in second quarter",
      "author": "Elian Corven",
      "author_role": "Head of Economic Accounts",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Kestria's economic output grew by 0.4 per cent in the second quarter of 2026, following growth of 0.2 per cent in the first quarter. Output was 1.3 per cent higher than in the same quarter of 2025. These first estimates remove the effects of price changes and regular seasonal patterns."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Services provided the largest contribution to quarterly growth. Transport, warehousing and business support expanded, while retail activity was broadly unchanged. Manufacturing output increased by 0.3 per cent, supported by food processing around Amberford. Construction output fell by 0.6 per cent as several large building projects reached completion."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Household spending increased by 0.3 per cent, while business investment rose by 0.8 per cent. The Kestria Statistics Office has based this release on the information currently available. Some business returns remain outstanding, so the estimate may change as fuller survey results and administrative records are incorporated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-15",
      "title": "July goods trade deficit narrows as exports increase",
      "author": "Nessa Talvern",
      "author_role": "Head of Trade Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Kestria exported goods worth KRN 4.82 billion in July and imported goods worth KRN 5.36 billion. The resulting goods trade deficit was KRN 540 million, compared with KRN 620 million in June. On a seasonally adjusted basis, export values rose by 2.1 per cent and import values rose by 0.4 per cent."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Processed food and electrical equipment accounted for much of the export increase. Machinery imports fell, partly offsetting higher imports of fuel and household goods. Shipments recorded through Kestrel Bay increased, although a port of departure does not necessarily identify where goods were made."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "These figures measure the value of goods crossing Kestria's border. They do not include trade in services and are not adjusted for price changes. The Kestria Statistics Office has revised the June deficit from KRN 650 million to KRN 620 million after receiving corrected declarations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-16",
      "title": "August labour survey shows a small rise in unemployment",
      "author": "Tovan Merrow",
      "author_role": "Head of Labour Market Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate rose to 5.5 per cent in August, from 5.4 per cent in July. Employment was estimated at 2,843,000, down by 3,000 over the month. The number of unemployed people increased by 4,000 to 166,000, while the number outside the labour force changed little."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Accommodation and food services recorded fewer workers after July's increase. Employment in health and social care continued to grow. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 was 73.0 per cent. Average weekly hours worked were broadly stable, with no clear change in the balance between full-time and part-time work."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The monthly movements are small relative to the uncertainty around the survey estimates. Results for the three months to August show employment above its level in the previous three months. The Kestria Statistics Office recommends using that wider comparison when assessing the direction of the labour market."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-22",
      "title": "Updated business returns lift second quarter growth estimate",
      "author": "Elian Corven",
      "author_role": "Head of Economic Accounts",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Kestria's economic output grew by 0.5 per cent in the second quarter of 2026, according to updated estimates published today. This revises the initial estimate of 0.4 per cent released on 27 August. First quarter growth remains at 0.2 per cent. Output was 1.4 per cent above its level a year earlier."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The revision mainly reflects stronger activity among transport and warehousing businesses than early returns indicated. Additional responses from firms operating around Kestrel Bay account for part of that change. Manufacturing growth remains at 0.3 per cent, and the estimated fall in construction output remains at 0.6 per cent."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Household spending growth has been revised from 0.3 to 0.4 per cent. Business investment growth is unchanged at 0.8 per cent. Revisions are a normal part of producing the economic accounts: later estimates draw on more complete information. All growth figures in this release exclude the effects of price changes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-06",
      "title": "New regional tables explain differences in household spending",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "author_role": "Head of Prices Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Households in Saltmarsh devoted an average of 29 per cent of their spending to housing and household services in 2025, compared with 24 per cent across Kestria. New regional tables published today show how spending patterns differed across the country. The figures describe expenditure shares rather than differences in the price of identical goods."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Transport accounted for 17 per cent of spending in the Amberford region, above the national share of 14 per cent. In the Val-Brume region, food and non-alcoholic drinks accounted for 18 per cent, compared with 16 per cent nationally. Household size, income and access to services can all affect these shares."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The tables use the 2025 household expenditure survey and include estimates of sampling uncertainty. They cannot establish which region is cheapest to live in. The Kestria Statistics Office has published accompanying notes in both Kestria's main administrative language and French to support use of the Val-Brume results."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-07",
      "title": "August consumer price tables corrected after classification error",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "author_role": "Head of Prices Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Statistics Office has corrected two detailed tables accompanying the August consumer prices release, originally published on 17 September. The headline annual inflation rate remains 3.0 per cent, and the monthly increase remains 0.1 per cent. The error affected the allocation of some household repair charges between two service categories."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The corrected annual rate for household maintenance services is 4.6 per cent, replacing 5.2 per cent. The rate for other domestic services is 3.8 per cent, replacing 3.1 per cent. Both categories sit within the same broader spending group, whose index is unchanged. No individual price observations were altered."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Replacement tables carry a correction note and today's date. Earlier versions remain identifiable in the publication archive. Users who downloaded the detailed category tables should replace their copies. The office found the error during a routine classification review and has added a check comparing category assignments before publication."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-09",
      "title": "August goods imports rise faster than export values",
      "author": "Nessa Talvern",
      "author_role": "Head of Trade Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Kestria's goods trade deficit widened to KRN 710 million in August, from KRN 540 million in July. Exports totalled KRN 4.86 billion and imports totalled KRN 5.57 billion. Seasonally adjusted export values increased by 0.8 per cent over the month, while import values increased by 3.9 per cent."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Higher machinery and fuel imports contributed most to the wider deficit. Export growth was concentrated in processed food, while electrical equipment exports declined slightly after July's increase. Businesses in the Ostara industrial area reported several large machinery deliveries, illustrating how individual purchases can affect monthly trade totals."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Over the three months to August, exports were 1.7 per cent higher than in the previous three months and imports were 2.2 per cent higher. These comparisons use values at current prices. They therefore reflect changes in prices as well as quantities. July totals are unchanged from the previous release."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-16",
      "title": "September employment increases while unemployment rate remains unchanged",
      "author": "Tovan Merrow",
      "author_role": "Head of Labour Market Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Employment in Kestria increased by an estimated 6,000 in September to 2,849,000. The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate remained at 5.5 per cent, with 166,000 people unemployed. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 returned to 73.1 per cent, the same rate recorded in July."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Health and social care, education and business support recorded employment gains. Manufacturing employment was broadly stable. More people entered the labour force during the month, allowing employment to rise without a fall in the unemployment rate. The labour force includes everyone who is employed or meets the survey definition of unemployment."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "In the three months to September, employment averaged 2,846,000. This was 9,000 higher than in the three months to June. Regional estimates, including those for Saltmarsh and Val-Brume, are published as three-month averages because their smaller survey samples make single-month comparisons less dependable. All figures remain subject to sampling uncertainty."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-19",
      "title": "Household spending survey begins recruitment across five cities",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "author_role": "Head of Prices Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Statistics Office has begun recruiting households for its next annual spending survey. The first invitations cover selected addresses in Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume. The full sample contains 8,400 addresses across urban and rural areas. Households are selected by chance so that different living arrangements are represented."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Participants are asked about regular bills and larger purchases, then keep a spending diary for fourteen days. The survey records expenditure on items such as food, transport, housing and leisure. Its results help update the spending weights used in consumer price statistics and describe how household budgets vary."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Selected households can complete the diary on paper or through the office's survey website. French materials are available for participants in Val-Brume and elsewhere. Published results combine responses into statistical groups; they do not identify individual households. Interviewers carry an identification number that households can check with the office before taking part."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "title": "September inflation falls as food price growth slows",
      "author": "Mara Vennick",
      "author_role": "Head of Prices Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Annual consumer price inflation in Kestria fell to 2.7 per cent in September, from 3.0 per cent in August. Prices were unchanged overall between August and September. This means that increases in some parts of the household basket were offset by falls in others, rather than every price remaining the same."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Food prices were 3.2 per cent higher than a year earlier, down from 3.7 per cent in August. Lower seasonal prices for fruit and vegetables contributed to the change. Passenger transport prices also fell over the month. Rents rose by 0.4 per cent and continued to put upward pressure on the overall index."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The annual inflation rate has eased from 3.2 per cent in July, but the overall price level remains above a year earlier. September calculations use the corrected service classifications described in the office's 7 October notice. That correction did not change the August headline index or the comparison reported today."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-22",
      "title": "Quarterly trade tables show growth in services exports",
      "author": "Nessa Talvern",
      "author_role": "Head of Trade Statistics",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Kestria exported services worth KRN 3.24 billion in the second quarter of 2026, an increase of 4.2 per cent from the first quarter. Services imports rose by 2.0 per cent to KRN 2.61 billion. The resulting services surplus was KRN 630 million, according to quarterly tables published today."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Business support and remote technical services accounted for most of the export increase. Transport services also grew, with higher receipts reported by operators based in Kestrel Bay. Travel spending by visitors increased modestly. The figures are seasonally adjusted and measured at current prices, so growth can reflect both higher prices and increased activity."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Services trade is measured mainly through business surveys and administrative records, rather than the border declarations used for goods. It is published later because the necessary information takes longer to collect. These second-quarter figures should not be combined directly with July or August goods figures to calculate a monthly overall trade balance."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

