      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Kestria Revenue Service published a clarification today on payroll tax treatment of employee travel payments. It explains the existing distinction between reimbursing documented business travel and paying a fixed travel allowance. The clarification does not change the payroll tax rate or introduce a new charge."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A reimbursement matching a supported business travel expense can be excluded from taxable wages. A fixed allowance paid regardless of actual costs must be included. Ordinary travel between home and an employee’s usual workplace does not qualify as business travel under these rules."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "For example, an Ostara repair firm may reimburse KRN 86 for an employee’s documented journey to a customer in Amberford without adding that reimbursement to taxable wages. A separate KRN 200 monthly travel allowance paid without expense records must be included. Employers should retain the journey’s purpose, date and supporting receipts. The guidance answers 143 travel-related enquiries received by the payroll support team during September."
