          "text": "Harrow & Finch Bank has introduced a revised monthly statement layout for variable-rate business loans. Statements issued from today show the reference rate, the borrower’s agreed margin and the number of days charged at each rate in a single interest table."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Where a rate changes during the statement period, separate rows show the balance and calculation for each part of that period. This will help borrowers check interest following the bank’s reference rate reductions in August and September. Fees will continue to appear separately from interest."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The layout changes how information is presented and does not alter loan terms or payment dates. It was tested with 24 business customers from Saltmarsh, Amberford and Val-Brume. Large-print statements and an explanation in French are available on request. Customers who need help checking a calculation can contact the lending team named on their statement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-16",
      "title": "Kestrel Bay lending team moves to larger customer premises",
      "author": "Tomas Arlen",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Harrow & Finch Bank’s Kestrel Bay lending team will move to 18 Lantern Quay on 19 October. The new premises provide four private meeting rooms, compared with two at the current office, and a ground-floor entrance with step-free access from the pavement."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The office will serve mid-sized companies seeking lending advice and support with existing facilities. Customer telephone numbers and relationship managers will remain the same. Meetings already booked for 19 October onwards will take place at Lantern Quay, and the bank is contacting customers individually with directions."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The current office at 7 Tern Passage closes to visitors at 16:00 today. Telephone support will remain available during normal opening hours throughout the move. The new office opens at 09:00 on 19 October. It is an appointment-based lending office and does not provide cash deposits, withdrawals or counter payment services."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "title": "Half-year results show lending growth and higher impairment charges",
      "author": "Mara Ellery",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Harrow & Finch Bank today reported profit before tax of KRN 18.6 million for the six months ended 30 September 2026, compared with KRN 17.9 million a year earlier. Operating income rose to KRN 52.4 million from KRN 48.7 million, while operating costs increased to KRN 27.1 million from KRN 25.6 million."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The charge for expected lending losses increased to KRN 6.7 million from KRN 5.2 million. The increase mainly reflected weaker repayment prospects among a small number of wholesale borrowers. Gross customer lending reached KRN 1.24 billion at the period end, up from KRN 1.17 billion on 31 March 2026."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "New lending drawn during the half year totalled KRN 186 million. Customer deposits stood at KRN 1.41 billion on 30 September. The bank continued to fund equipment purchases and working capital across Kestria while reviewing borrowers showing early signs of payment difficulty. The results are unaudited."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-22",
      "title": "New borrower support team offers earlier repayment difficulty reviews",
      "author": "Devran Holt",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Harrow & Finch Bank has established a dedicated borrower support team for business customers facing repayment difficulties. The five-person team is based in Saltmarsh and accepts referrals from relationship managers across Kestria. Customers can also ask their usual lending contact for a support review."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The team will examine recent trading, cash forecasts and existing repayment commitments. Depending on the circumstances, options may include changing payment dates or agreeing a temporary reduction in principal repayments. Any arrangement requires an assessment and written agreement. Interest may continue to accrue, and extending a loan can increase its total cost."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "There is no charge for the initial support review. Customers are encouraged to make contact as soon as they expect a shortfall, even if every payment is currently up to date. French-language reviews are available through the Val-Brume lending desk. Requesting a review does not itself change contractual payment obligations."
        }
      ]
    }
