          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has published a revised delivery guide for its wheat and oat futures. The guide brings warehouse instructions, sampling rules and delivery forms into one document. It applies to delivery notices submitted from today and does not change the quantity represented by either contract."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Both contracts cover 20 tonnes. Approved delivery locations remain Ostara, Amberford and Kestrel Bay. Sellers must identify the warehouse, stored quantity and inspection reference on each notice. Buyers can request a second sample where the first inspection records a result within 0.2 percentage points of a contract quality limit."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The guide also explains how warehouse charges are divided when ownership changes. Storage remains the seller's responsibility through the transfer date, with the buyer responsible from the following day. Members can obtain the guide through the exchange's document service. A French-language version is available for members and warehouse operators in Val-Brume."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-19",
      "title": "Rapeseed margins increased following wider daily price movements",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has increased the initial margin on rapeseed futures to KRN 2,400 per contract, from KRN 2,100. The maintenance margin rises to KRN 2,000, from KRN 1,750. The revised requirements apply to positions held at today's clearing calculation and cover every listed rapeseed delivery month."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The change follows a review of price movements over the past ten trading sessions. The average daily price range reached KRN 21 per tonne, compared with KRN 14 during the preceding review period. Each rapeseed contract represents 20 tonnes. Margin is collateral against potential losses and is separate from the contract's purchase price."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Clearing members must meet any resulting collateral shortfall by 09:00 on the next trading day. Eligible collateral and payment instructions are unchanged. Wheat and oat margin requirements remain at their existing levels. The exchange reviews margins each trading day and can adjust them when price movements or the concentration of positions warrant a change."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-26",
      "title": "Oat futures gain smaller price steps across listed months",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has reduced the minimum price movement for oat futures from KRN 0.50 to KRN 0.25 per tonne. The change took effect at today's opening and applies to all listed delivery months. With a contract size of 20 tonnes, each minimum movement is now worth KRN 5."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The exchange introduced the smaller step after members requested more precise pricing between bids and offers. Orders entered before the change remain valid because prices quoted in half-crown increments also meet the new quarter-crown requirement. Contract quantities, delivery standards and the method used to calculate daily settlement prices have not changed."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Members should check that their order systems accept prices ending in .25 and .75, as well as .00 and .50. The exchange's test service includes sample orders using each permitted ending. Support staff in Ostara and Val-Brume are handling queries about rejected prices and the display of contract values on member screens."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-04",
      "title": "August trading figures show stronger activity in wheat futures",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange recorded 48,620 futures contracts traded in August, up from 44,180 in July. Wheat accounted for 27,340 contracts, oats for 8,760 and rapeseed for 12,520. The figures count each matched contract once and exclude orders that were cancelled or remained unfilled."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Wheat trading increased by 14 per cent compared with July, with the largest share of activity in the nearest two delivery months. Across all three commodities, open interest stood at 18,460 contracts at the August close. Open interest measures contracts that remain outstanding rather than the number traded during the month."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "There were 286 completed delivery transfers during August, representing 5,720 tonnes. Approved warehouses in Amberford handled 124 transfers, Ostara handled 98 and Kestrel Bay handled 64. The monthly statistics file includes delivery totals and activity by contract month. Members can download it from the exchange's document service without a separate subscription charge."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-14",
      "title": "Amberford warehouse approved for additional wheat delivery capacity",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has approved a further 12,000 tonnes of wheat storage at the Merebank warehouse in Amberford. The additional capacity is eligible for exchange delivery from today. Approval follows checks of weighing equipment, sampling facilities, stock records and the separation of grain held for different owners."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The extension brings Merebank's approved wheat capacity to 30,000 tonnes. It does not add oat or rapeseed storage to the site's approval. Wheat placed into exchange delivery must meet the existing contract standards, including a maximum moisture content of 14.5 per cent. Warehouse approval does not replace the inspection required for each delivery lot."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Members arranging delivery should confirm available space directly with the warehouse before submitting a notice. The exchange's register now shows the additional capacity and the site's receiving hours. Merebank must report eligible stocks each trading day and notify the exchange immediately if equipment faults, access restrictions or stock discrepancies affect its ability to receive or release grain."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-28",
      "title": "Exchange extends afternoon trading for all agricultural futures",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has extended its regular trading session by 30 minutes. From today, wheat, oat and rapeseed futures trade from 08:30 to 16:30, using the exchange clock in Ostara. The opening time is unchanged. Members can enter orders during the pre-opening period from 08:15."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The daily settlement calculation now uses eligible trading activity between 16:20 and 16:30. Where activity is insufficient, the exchange applies its existing rules for assessing bids, offers and related contract months. The longer session responds to requests from members handling afternoon warehouse confirmations in Amberford and freight arrangements through Kestrel Bay."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Orders marked to expire at the end of the trading day now remain active until 16:30 unless cancelled or filled. Orders carrying a specific expiry time retain that instruction. Members should review automated schedules and staffing arrangements. The extension does not alter delivery notice deadlines, collateral payment deadlines or the opening hours of approved warehouses."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-05",
      "title": "Wheat margin requirements rise after increased market volatility",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has raised the initial margin for wheat futures from KRN 1,600 to KRN 1,900 per contract. The maintenance margin increases from KRN 1,300 to KRN 1,550. The new requirements apply to all listed wheat months at today's clearing calculation, including positions opened before the announcement."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The adjustment reflects larger daily price movements and a greater concentration of positions in the nearest delivery month. Over the past five sessions, the average wheat price range was KRN 17 per tonne. Each contract covers 20 tonnes. The exchange has made no change to wheat delivery specifications or permitted price increments."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Clearing members must provide any additional collateral by 09:00 on the next trading day. Members may apply higher requirements to their own clients under their account agreements. Oat and rapeseed margins are unchanged by this notice. The exchange continues to assess both price risk and the time needed to close positions when setting its collateral requirements."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-06",
      "title": "September statistics record higher open interest across agricultural contracts",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange recorded open interest of 20,180 contracts at the September close, an increase of 1,720 from August. Wheat accounted for 11,420 outstanding contracts, oats for 3,160 and rapeseed for 5,600. All three products ended September with more outstanding contracts than at the previous month end."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "September trading volume totalled 51,260 contracts. This comprised 29,100 wheat contracts, 9,040 oat contracts and 13,120 rapeseed contracts. The busiest session recorded 3,480 contracts across the exchange. Volume counts completed trades during the month, while open interest records positions that remained outstanding after clearing at the month end."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Approved warehouses completed 312 delivery transfers, equivalent to 6,240 tonnes. Of these, 174 transfers were wheat, 58 were oats and 80 were rapeseed. The full statistics file is available through the exchange's document service. It includes daily trading totals and a breakdown by delivery month, with explanatory notes on how cancelled trades are treated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-08",
      "title": "Trading resumes after morning interruption to order entry",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Trading in wheat, oat and rapeseed futures resumed at 10:05 today after an interruption to the exchange's order entry service. Ostara Commodities Exchange suspended matching at 09:12 when monitoring identified delays in order acknowledgements. A ten-minute period for members to review and cancel orders preceded the reopening."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Trades completed before the suspension remain valid. Orders that had been accepted and remained unfilled were retained with their original priority. Messages that did not receive an acceptance acknowledgement were not added to the order book. Members were asked to reconcile their records against the exchange's order and trade reports before entering replacement instructions."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Technical staff traced the interruption to a fault in a message queue and moved the service to reserve equipment. The regular 16:30 closing time remains in place. Clearing and warehouse reporting services continued to operate during the interruption. The exchange is reviewing system records and member reports to establish why the fault was not resolved automatically."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-14",
      "title": "Rapeseed delivery rules clarify sampling and retained records",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has issued a clarification of the sampling procedure for rapeseed delivered against its futures contracts. From today, delivery inspection records must state the sampling time, lot reference and seal numbers for retained samples. The clarification applies at every warehouse approved to handle exchange rapeseed deliveries."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Inspectors must prepare three sealed samples from each delivery lot: one for the seller, one for the buyer and one held by the warehouse. The warehouse sample must be retained for 45 calendar days after ownership transfers. Where a quality dispute remains open, it must be retained until the exchange confirms that the case is closed."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The maximum moisture content remains 9 per cent, and the maximum foreign matter allowance remains 2 per cent. Contract size and approved delivery locations are unchanged. Members can obtain the revised inspection form in English or French. Forms already completed for transferred lots remain valid; warehouses are not required to reopen completed deliveries solely to add the new record fields."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-15",
      "title": "Exchange completes review of order entry service interruption",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has completed its review of the order entry interruption on 8 October. The review found that a software fault caused an internal message queue to stop releasing acknowledgements promptly. Automatic recovery did not start because the monitoring check measured connection availability without checking whether messages were progressing through the queue."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The exchange has added a separate check for message progress and tested the transfer to reserve equipment under simulated delays. It has also revised the procedure used by technical staff to confirm that order entry is ready to reopen. These changes are in operation. The review found no alteration to completed trade records."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Reconciliation with clearing members is complete, and no unresolved differences remain between accepted orders and the exchange's records for the affected period. Members can request their own message logs through the support service. The exchange has published a technical incident note setting out the interruption times, the cause and the checks applied before trading resumed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "title": "Rapeseed margins reduced as daily price ranges narrow",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has reduced the initial margin on rapeseed futures from KRN 2,400 to KRN 2,200 per contract. The maintenance margin falls from KRN 2,000 to KRN 1,850. The revised requirements apply at today's clearing calculation across all listed rapeseed delivery months, with no change to contract size."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The reduction follows a sustained narrowing of daily price ranges. The average range over the latest ten trading sessions was KRN 15 per tonne, compared with KRN 21 at the August review that led to higher margins. The exchange also assessed outstanding positions and the volume available to close them during normal trading."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Any collateral made available by the reduction remains subject to the member's other obligations and the exchange's normal withdrawal checks. The lower requirement does not change the daily settlement of gains and losses. Wheat initial margin remains KRN 1,900 per contract. Oat requirements are unchanged. Members should consult their clearing statements for the effect on individual accounts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-23",
      "title": "Exchange introduces calendar spread orders for wheat futures",
      "author": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has introduced calendar spread orders for wheat futures. Members can now submit a single order to buy one listed wheat delivery month and sell another in equal quantities. The service is available during the regular 08:30 to 16:30 session and uses the same contract specifications as outright wheat trading."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Spread prices represent the price of the later delivery month minus the price of the earlier month, quoted in KRN per tonne. The minimum price movement is KRN 0.25 per tonne, equivalent to KRN 5 for a spread covering one 20-tonne contract in each month. Both sides execute together, removing the need to submit two separate outright orders."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Completed spreads create a separate futures position in each delivery month. Existing delivery obligations and position limits continue to apply to those positions. The exchange's published margin schedule determines the collateral required, including any eligible offset between months. Members can access worked examples and test messages through the document and test services. Oat and rapeseed order types are unchanged."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "releases": [
    {
      "date": "2026-08-18",
      "title": "Workplace pension funding improves in second quarter review",
      "author": "Mara Ellwick",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Pensions Oversight Authority estimates that workplace pension plans promising a set retirement income held assets equal to 104 per cent of their liabilities at the end of June. This compares with 102 per cent at the end of March. The review covers 842 plans across Kestria, with combined assets of KRN 186 billion."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The improvement mainly reflects higher investment income and employer contributions. Funding remains uneven: 176 plans held less than the estimated amount needed to meet their promises. A national average does not show whether an individual plan can pay every benefit when it falls due."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Employers and trustees should continue making scheduled payments and reviewing their funding assumptions. The figures are estimates based on quarterly returns, rather than full valuations of every plan. The Authority has published regional tables, including separate results for Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-20",
      "title": "Employers given clearer steps for reporting late pension payments",
      "author": "Tomas Vennick",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has published guidance explaining how employers and trustees should report late workplace pension contributions. It includes examples covering payroll errors, missing payment references and repeated delays. The guidance applies to reports made from today and does not change the legal payment deadlines."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Trustees should record when a payment was due, the amount outstanding and the steps taken to recover it. Where a report is required, it should distinguish money deducted from staff pay from contributions owed by the employer. Employers should give trustees a named contact who can resolve payment queries."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "During the first six months of 2026, the Authority received 318 reports involving late payments. Of these, 241 were resolved within 20 working days of the initial report. The new guidance includes a reporting checklist and sample payment record, with versions available in English and French."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-08-21",
      "title": "Amberford employer fined after repeated delays to pension contributions",
      "author": "Leila Corven",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has fined Amberford employer Brindle Quay Components KRN 42,000 after it repeatedly paid workplace pension contributions late. The company missed payment deadlines in seven months between September 2025 and April 2026. The delays affected 286 members of its workplace pension plan."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The employer has now paid the outstanding KRN 193,400, together with KRN 8,760 to compensate the plan for lost investment income. The fine is separate from those payments and must be paid by the company. It cannot be taken from pension assets or charged to members."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Authority issued its final decision after considering the company's response to an earlier notice. The company has 28 days to appeal. Trustees have been asked to check contributions each month and report further delays. Members do not need to submit individual claims to receive the compensation within the plan."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-14",
      "title": "Consultation opens on clearer recovery plans for underfunded pensions",
      "author": "Tomas Vennick",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Pensions Oversight Authority is seeking views on proposed changes to the information employers and trustees provide when a workplace pension plan is underfunded. The consultation opens today and closes on 30 October. The proposals concern plans that promise members a set retirement income."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Under the proposals, recovery plans would explain why the payment schedule is affordable, what would happen if trading weakened and how trustees would monitor progress. A standard summary would show annual contributions in KRN alongside the expected funding shortfall. The Authority is also asking whether smaller plans need a shorter reporting form."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "No funding rules change today. Existing recovery plans and payment schedules remain in force. Employers, trustees, advisers and members can respond through the Authority's consultation service or by post to its Saltmarsh office. An accompanying paper includes worked examples for a manufacturer, a transport business and a community services employer."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-23",
      "title": "Val-Brume pension clinic offers support with annual returns",
      "author": "Celia Bramwell",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Trustees and employers in the Val-Brume area can now book free appointments at a pension reporting clinic on 8 October. Staff from the Pensions Oversight Authority will explain common annual return requirements and help visitors identify information missing from their records. Appointments will be available in French and English."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The clinic will take place at the Authority's Val-Brume office, with 36 appointments lasting 25 minutes each. Visitors should bring their plan registration number and a list of questions. They should not bring members' identity documents, bank details or individual medical records. Telephone appointments are available for those unable to attend."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The service follows a review of 94 returns from plans administered in the region. The most frequent errors concerned membership totals and changes to trustee contact details. Staff can explain reporting requirements but cannot complete returns for applicants or provide investment advice. Booking closes on 5 October, unless all appointments are filled earlier."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-25",
      "title": "Annual report records faster action on unpaid pension contributions",
      "author": "Mara Ellwick",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Pensions Oversight Authority's annual report, published today, records KRN 6.8 million in overdue employer contributions recovered following its intervention during the year ended 31 March 2026. The payments reached 73 workplace pension plans covering 18,420 members. Recoveries totalled KRN 5.1 million in the previous reporting year."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The median time from opening a contribution case to securing payment fell from 47 to 34 working days. The Authority issued 29 financial penalties and required improvements to record keeping at 61 plans. These figures cover completed cases; they do not include money still being pursued at the year end."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Operating expenditure was KRN 24.6 million against a budget of KRN 25.2 million. The report includes audited accounts, service measures and a breakdown of complaints about the Authority. Priorities for the current year include earlier checks on missed payments and clearer guidance for employers running smaller workplace plans."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-10-05",
      "title": "Trustees asked to check member addresses before annual statements",
      "author": "Celia Bramwell",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "The Pensions Oversight Authority is asking workplace pension trustees to check their member contact records before issuing annual benefit statements this autumn. A review of 120 plans found that 8.4 per cent of statements sent during the previous reporting cycle were returned or could not be delivered electronically."
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
