Over the three months to August, exports were 1.7 per cent higher than in the previous three months and imports were 2.2 per cent higher. These comparisons use values at current prices. They therefore reflect changes in prices as well as quantities. July totals are unchanged from the previous release."}

{"title": "Employment grows as July unemployment rate holds steady", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Tovan Merrow", "body": "Kestria had an estimated 2,846,000 people in employment in July, an increase of 7,000 from June. The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate remained at 5.4 per cent. An estimated 162,000 people were unemployed, meaning they were without work, available to start and actively seeking a job.

Employment increased most in accommodation, food services and building maintenance. Manufacturing employment changed little. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 rose to 73.1 per cent from 73.0 per cent. These estimates come from the household labour survey and cover employees and people working for themselves.

Survey responses from Kestrel Bay indicated stronger seasonal hiring, although local monthly estimates are less reliable than the national figures. The Kestria Statistics Office advises users to consider several months together. Small monthly movements may reflect sampling variation rather than a lasting change in the labour market."}

{"title": "Annual consumer price inflation eases to 3.2 per cent", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "Consumer prices in Kestria rose by 3.2 per cent in the year to July, down from 3.5 per cent in June. Prices increased by 0.2 per cent between June and July. The annual rate measures how much the price of a representative basket of goods and services has changed over twelve months.

Slower growth in food prices made the largest contribution to the easing. Food prices were 4.1 per cent higher than a year earlier, compared with 4.8 per cent in June. Fresh vegetable prices fell during July, particularly in markets surveyed in Amberford and Ostara. Rents continued to rise.

The lower inflation rate does not mean that prices generally fell. It means they rose more slowly than before. The Kestria Statistics Office collected prices from shops, service providers and online sellers across the country. July figures include the usual seasonal adjustment to the sample of summer clothing."}

{"title": "August consumer price tables corrected after classification error", "date": "2026-10-07", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Statistics Office has corrected two detailed tables accompanying the August consumer prices release, originally published on 17 September. The headline annual inflation rate remains 3.0 per cent, and the monthly increase remains 0.1 per cent. The error affected the allocation of some household repair charges between two service categories.

The corrected annual rate for household maintenance services is 4.6 per cent, replacing 5.2 per cent. The rate for other domestic services is 3.8 per cent, replacing 3.1 per cent. Both categories sit within the same broader spending group, whose index is unchanged. No individual price observations were altered.

Replacement tables carry a correction note and today's date. Earlier versions remain identifiable in the publication archive. Users who downloaded the detailed category tables should replace their copies. The office found the error during a routine classification review and has added a check comparing category assignments before publication."}

{"title": "New regional tables explain differences in household spending", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "Households in Saltmarsh devoted an average of 29 per cent of their spending to housing and household services in 2025, compared with 24 per cent across Kestria. New regional tables published today show how spending patterns differed across the country. The figures describe expenditure shares rather than differences in the price of identical goods.

Transport accounted for 17 per cent of spending in the Amberford region, above the national share of 14 per cent. In the Val-Brume region, food and non-alcoholic drinks accounted for 18 per cent, compared with 16 per cent nationally. Household size, income and access to services can all affect these shares.

The tables use the 2025 household expenditure survey and include estimates of sampling uncertainty. They cannot establish which region is cheapest to live in. The Kestria Statistics Office has published accompanying notes in both Kestria's main administrative language and French to support use of the Val-Brume results."}

{"title": "Quarterly trade tables show growth in services exports", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Nessa Talvern", "body": "Kestria exported services worth KRN 3.24 billion in the second quarter of 2026, an increase of 4.2 per cent from the first quarter. Services imports rose by 2.0 per cent to KRN 2.61 billion. The resulting services surplus was KRN 630 million, according to quarterly tables published today.

Business support and remote technical services accounted for most of the export increase. Transport services also grew, with higher receipts reported by operators based in Kestrel Bay. Travel spending by visitors increased modestly. The figures are seasonally adjusted and measured at current prices, so growth can reflect both higher prices and increased activity.

Services trade is measured mainly through business surveys and administrative records, rather than the border declarations used for goods. It is published later because the necessary information takes longer to collect. These second-quarter figures should not be combined directly with July or August goods figures to calculate a monthly overall trade balance."}

{"title": "September employment increases while unemployment rate remains unchanged", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Tovan Merrow", "body": "Employment in Kestria increased by an estimated 6,000 in September to 2,849,000. The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate remained at 5.5 per cent, with 166,000 people unemployed. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 returned to 73.1 per cent, the same rate recorded in July.

Health and social care, education and business support recorded employment gains. Manufacturing employment was broadly stable. More people entered the labour force during the month, allowing employment to rise without a fall in the unemployment rate. The labour force includes everyone who is employed or meets the survey definition of unemployment.

In the three months to September, employment averaged 2,846,000. This was 9,000 higher than in the three months to June. Regional estimates, including those for Saltmarsh and Val-Brume, are published as three-month averages because their smaller survey samples make single-month comparisons less dependable. All figures remain subject to sampling uncertainty."}

{"title": "Household spending survey begins recruitment across five cities", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Statistics Office has begun recruiting households for its next annual spending survey. The first invitations cover selected addresses in Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume. The full sample contains 8,400 addresses across urban and rural areas. Households are selected by chance so that different living arrangements are represented.

Participants are asked about regular bills and larger purchases, then keep a spending diary for fourteen days. The survey records expenditure on items such as food, transport, housing and leisure. Its results help update the spending weights used in consumer price statistics and describe how household budgets vary.

Selected households can complete the diary on paper or through the office's survey website. French materials are available for participants in Val-Brume and elsewhere. Published results combine responses into statistical groups; they do not identify individual households. Interviewers carry an identification number that households can check with the office before taking part."}

{"title": "Payroll corrections guide explains how to fix common errors", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service issued a payroll corrections guide today after reviewing 1,120 amended returns received in August. Almost half involved wages entered in the wrong reporting month. The guide explains how employers can correct a submitted return without creating a second charge for the same wages.

Employers should open the original return, select the correction option and enter the full replacement figures for the affected month. They should not enter only the difference between the old and correct amounts. Each accepted correction produces a new receipt linked to the original filing.

For example, an Amberford employer that reported taxable wages of KRN 42,000 instead of KRN 24,000 should replace the wages figure with KRN 24,000. Any resulting credit appears on the employer's account after processing. The guide is available in English and French, with printed copies at the Val-Brume revenue office. Employers should keep records explaining why each correction was made."}

{"title": "September inflation falls as food price growth slows", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "Annual consumer price inflation in Kestria fell to 2.7 per cent in September, from 3.0 per cent in August. Prices were unchanged overall between August and September. This means that increases in some parts of the household basket were offset by falls in others, rather than every price remaining the same.

Food prices were 3.2 per cent higher than a year earlier, down from 3.7 per cent in August. Lower seasonal prices for fruit and vegetables contributed to the change. Passenger transport prices also fell over the month. Rents rose by 0.4 per cent and continued to put upward pressure on the overall index.

The annual inflation rate has eased from 3.2 per cent in July, but the overall price level remains above a year earlier. September calculations use the corrected service classifications described in the office's 7 October notice. That correction did not change the August headline index or the comparison reported today."}

{"title": "Payroll filing service restored after morning access problems", "date": "2026-09-10", "author": null, "body": "Access to the Kestria Revenue Service payroll filing service was restored at 11.40 am today following a technical fault. The interruption began at 8.15 am and affected employers trying to open or submit payroll tax returns. Corporate tax and sales tax filing remained available.

The fault interrupted the connection between the sign-in page and payroll records. Technical checks found no evidence that employer or employee information had been lost or disclosed. Returns submitted before the interruption remain recorded, and employers do not need to send those returns again.

Employers who saw an error message during submission should check their filing history for a receipt number. If no receipt appears, they should reopen the saved return and submit it. The service logged 186 reports from employers, including businesses in Amberford and Kestrel Bay. Anyone still unable to file should contact the payroll support team and quote the message shown on screen."}

{"title": "Sales tax registration threshold rises to support smaller businesses", "date": "2026-08-27", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service today published a sales tax rule raising the compulsory registration threshold from KRN 150,000 to KRN 180,000. The change takes effect on 1 October and applies to taxable sales measured over a rolling 12-month period. The sales tax rate remains 6 per cent.

Businesses already registered must continue collecting and reporting sales tax unless the service approves their removal from the register. A business with taxable sales below the new threshold may apply to leave, but should first check whether tax adjustments are required for stock or equipment it still holds.

An explanatory notice includes worked examples for shops, repair businesses and seasonal traders. Printed copies are available at revenue offices in Kestrel Bay, Ostara and Val-Brume. The service estimates that 2,300 registered businesses have annual taxable sales between the old and new thresholds. Eligibility depends on each business’s records, rather than its size or number of employees."}

{"title": "Revenue Service opens additional help desks for small employers", "date": "2026-08-24", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service opened additional payroll tax help desks today in Saltmarsh and Amberford. The desks support employers completing their first return or correcting employee details. Each location has four advisers, with appointments available between 9 am and 4 pm.

Employers should bring their business registration number, payroll totals and records of tax already paid. Advisers can explain how to complete a return and check whether the required sections are filled in. They cannot prepare wage records on an employer’s behalf or accept payments in cash.

The service recorded 640 requests for first-return support during the first three weeks of August. Missing employee reference numbers were the most common problem. Employers outside the two cities can receive the same guidance by telephone, including assistance in French through the Val-Brume support team. There is no charge for either service."}

{"title": "Revenue Service reports progress on sales tax refunds", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service processed 92 per cent of complete sales tax refund claims within 15 working days during September. This compares with 86 per cent in August. September payments totalled KRN 18.4 million across 1,760 claims, including claims from manufacturers, wholesalers and building contractors.

The improvement followed the reassignment of six processing staff to the refund team in Ostara. Claims requiring further checks are excluded from the 15-day measure while the service waits for requested information. The published figures include both the number of claims awaiting checks and the reasons for those checks.

At the end of September, 214 claims were awaiting invoices, bank account confirmation or an explanation of unusually large purchases. Businesses can check progress through their tax account. Sending the same claim again can delay processing because staff must establish whether it is a duplicate. A business should instead reply to the information request using its existing claim reference."}

{"title": "Higher sales tax threshold now applies across Kestria", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": null, "body": "The higher compulsory sales tax registration threshold of KRN 180,000 is now in force, the Kestria Revenue Service confirmed today. It took effect on 1 October, replacing the KRN 150,000 threshold. Businesses must measure their taxable sales over a rolling 12-month period when checking whether registration is required.

By the close of business on 2 October, the service had received 486 applications to leave the sales tax register. Of these, 312 had been approved and 174 remained under review. Each approved business received a notice stating the date from which it must stop charging sales tax.

The service reminded traders in Saltmarsh, Val-Brume and other communities that applying does not immediately end their obligations. Registered businesses must keep collecting tax and filing returns until their approved removal date. The sales tax rate remains 6 per cent. Customers can ask suppliers for a corrected invoice if tax has been charged after a supplier’s registration ended."}

{"title": "Employers receive clarification on payroll tax for travel payments", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service published a clarification today on payroll tax treatment of employee travel payments. It explains the existing distinction between reimbursing documented business travel and paying a fixed travel allowance. The clarification does not change the payroll tax rate or introduce a new charge.

A reimbursement matching a supported business travel expense can be excluded from taxable wages. A fixed allowance paid regardless of actual costs must be included. Ordinary travel between home and an employee’s usual workplace does not qualify as business travel under these rules.

For example, an Ostara repair firm may reimburse KRN 86 for an employee’s documented journey to a customer in Amberford without adding that reimbursement to taxable wages. A separate KRN 200 monthly travel allowance paid without expense records must be included. Employers should retain the journey’s purpose, date and supporting receipts. The guidance answers 143 travel-related enquiries received by the payroll support team during September."}

{"title": "Val-Brume revenue office introduces French payroll support appointments", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service introduced dedicated French-language payroll support appointments at its Val-Brume office today. The office now offers 24 appointments each week for employers who need help understanding returns, account balances or requests for information. Telephone support in French remains available.

The appointment service follows a local trial held during September, when advisers assisted 68 employers. Most enquiries concerned corrections to monthly wages and matching payments to the correct reporting period. Appointments last 30 minutes and can be booked by telephone or through a business tax account.

Employers should bring the relevant return, wage records and any letter they want to discuss. A representative attending for a business must have authority recorded on its tax account before staff can discuss private information. General guidance is available without access to an account. The service does not charge for appointments, and employers do not need to use a paid intermediary to book one."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay broker fined for trading ahead of client orders", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has imposed a KRN 780,000 penalty on Kestrel Bay broker Velmora Securities for using confidential client order information when trading for its own account. The firm admitted the breaches and agreed to appoint an independent reviewer of its trading controls.

Between February and May, a trading desk bought shares before executing 23 large client purchase orders. It then sold those shares after the client purchases had pushed prices higher. The conduct generated KRN 164,000 in profits. The firm must return that amount to affected clients in addition to paying the penalty.

The investigation found that staff could view pending client orders while placing trades for the firm's account, without effective checks. Velmora Securities has restricted that access. Its independent reviewer must assess the changes and report to the Commission within 90 days. The firm must also provide clients with a written explanation of how individual repayment amounts were calculated."}

{"title": "Corrected Amberford Heatworks report satisfies Commission disclosure direction", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Selin Arvane", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has completed its review of the corrected half-year report published by Amberford Heatworks on 8 September. The revised report satisfies the disclosure direction announced on 25 August concerning a loan condition linked to the company's minimum cash reserves.

The corrected report explains the KRN 6 million cash threshold, how the lender measures compliance and when repayment could be demanded. It also sets out the company's cash position at 30 June and the steps management would consider if reserves approached the threshold. The company published the required separate notice explaining the corrections.

The Commission is closing its review of compliance with this direction. This does not amount to an assessment of the company's financial strength or a recommendation to buy its bonds. The case underlines the need to explain borrowing conditions alongside headline debt figures. Issuers should check that investors can understand both when obligations fall due and what could cause payment dates to change."}

{"title": "Commission reminds issuers of approaching annual reporting consultation deadline", "date": "2026-09-25", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission is reminding interested parties that its consultation on clearer annual reporting closes on 29 September. The consultation, opened on 18 August, covers explanations of funding risks, major customer losses and changes to accounting estimates in listed company reports.

The Commission has received 26 submissions so far, including responses from individual investors and smaller listed companies. Several respondents have asked how much detail would be expected when a company depends on one large customer. The consultation paper includes questions on this issue, and further views are welcome.

Respondents may answer any of the questions and do not need to comment on the whole paper. Submissions can be made in English or French. Anyone requesting confidential treatment should identify the relevant passages and explain why confidentiality is needed. The Commission will consider the evidence before deciding its next steps. Existing reporting requirements continue to apply while the proposals are under review."}

{"title": "Commission publishes findings from review of market announcement timing", "date": "2026-09-07", "author": "Selin Arvane", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has completed a review of how quickly listed companies publish information that could affect their securities prices. Staff examined 63 announcements issued between January and June, including notices about cancelled contracts, financing agreements and significant changes to expected earnings.

In 11 cases, companies could not provide a clear record of when senior staff first received the information. Five companies relied on a single person to approve announcements, with no cover during absences. The Commission has written to those companies requesting improvements to their procedures.

The findings do not establish that all 11 companies disclosed information late. However, incomplete records make it harder to demonstrate compliance. Companies should record key decisions, identify an alternative approver and keep disclosure arrangements under review. A briefing note published today includes practical examples. The Commission will assess the responses before deciding whether any individual case requires further investigation."}

{"title": "Commission opens consultation on clearer reporting by listed companies", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission today opened a consultation on proposed changes to annual reporting rules for listed companies. The proposals would require clearer explanations of cash shortages, major customer losses and changes to accounting estimates. Responses must reach the Commission by 29 September 2026.

A review of 48 annual reports found that 17 companies used broad statements that gave investors little information about their main risks. Nine reports described available funding without explaining when loans fell due. The proposed rules would require companies to connect these figures with their plans for meeting payments.

The consultation also asks whether smaller issuers should receive additional guidance and a longer preparation period. Investors, companies, auditors and other interested parties may submit written comments. The consultation paper is available in English and French. The Commission has not decided whether to adopt the proposals and will publish a summary of responses."}

{"title": "Annual reporting consultation closes with forty three written submissions", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission received 43 written submissions to its consultation on clearer annual reporting, which closed on 29 September. Non-confidential responses are now available in the consultation register. Staff are reviewing the evidence, and no decision has been made on changes to the rules.

The submissions came from 16 listed companies, 12 individual investors, seven audit practices and eight other respondents. Common topics included the cost of preparing additional explanations, the treatment of commercially sensitive customer information and the time smaller companies would need to update reporting procedures.

Several investors requested clearer links between borrowing figures and repayment dates. Some companies favoured practical guidance alongside any new requirements. The Commission will assess these views together rather than treating the number of responses supporting a proposal as a vote. A response paper will explain the conclusions and any proposed implementation arrangements. Companies should continue to prepare reports under the existing rules."}

{"title": "Amberford bond issuer ordered to correct missing debt disclosures", "date": "2026-08-25", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has directed Amberford Heatworks to correct its latest half-year report after the bond issuer omitted information about a loan condition. The condition allows a lender to demand repayment if the company's cash reserves fall below KRN 6 million.

The company reported cash reserves of KRN 7.4 million at 30 June but did not explain the condition or the consequences of breaching it. The Commission considers this information necessary for investors assessing the company's ability to meet payments on its KRN 42 million bond issue.

Amberford Heatworks must publish a corrected report by 8 September and explain the changes in a separate market notice. The direction does not require early repayment of the bonds. No financial penalty has been imposed. The Commission will review the corrected report and consider whether further action is needed. Bond issuers remain responsible for explaining significant borrowing conditions in plain language."}

{"title": "Ostara workshop will explain bond disclosure duties to smaller issuers", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Lucien Marcelet", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission will hold a free workshop in Ostara on 22 October for smaller companies that issue bonds to the public. The session will explain continuing disclosure duties, including how to report borrowing conditions, missed payments and material changes in available funding.

There are 60 places available, with registration closing on 19 October unless the session fills earlier. Finance staff, company secretaries and directors may attend. Commission staff will use fictional examples involving a KRN 15 million bond issue and a loan approaching its repayment date. Attendance does not satisfy any formal reporting requirement.

Participants may submit general questions when registering. Staff cannot give advance approval to company announcements or discuss confidential supervisory cases at the workshop. Written materials will be available in English and French, and a French-language online session will be arranged separately. The Commission will publish the presentation materials so that issuers unable to attend can use the same guidance."}

{"title": "Commission seeks court order over suspected false revenue statements", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has applied to the securities court in Saltmarsh for an order requiring Ordelune Storage to preserve accounting records and related correspondence. The application forms part of an investigation into suspected false revenue statements in the listed company's latest annual report.

The Commission is examining whether KRN 18.6 million recorded as storage service revenue was supported by completed work. Its application concerns records held by the company and two former executives. The Commission has asked for an expedited hearing because some relevant electronic records are subject to routine deletion.

The allegations have not been determined by the court. The application does not seek a finding of liability or a financial penalty at this stage. Ordelune Storage and the former executives will have an opportunity to respond. The Commission will publish a further update when the court decides the application. Investors should consult the company's market announcements for its statements on the matter."}

{"title": "Court orders preservation of records in Ordelune Storage investigation", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The securities court in Saltmarsh today ordered Ordelune Storage and two former executives to preserve records relevant to the Kestria Securities Commission's investigation into suspected false revenue statements. The order follows the Commission's application announced on 13 October and takes effect immediately.

The order covers accounting entries, customer contracts, invoices and related correspondence concerning KRN 18.6 million in reported storage service revenue. Those subject to it must suspend routine deletion of relevant electronic material and retain paper records. They must provide written confirmation of the preservation steps taken within seven days.

The court has not decided whether the revenue statements were false or whether any person breached securities law. The Commission's investigation continues, and the company and former executives retain the right to respond to any allegations. Preserving the records will allow the evidence to be examined. The Commission will announce any further public proceedings when they are filed."}

{"title": "Val-Brume review finds gaps in French bond disclosure documents", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Lucien Marcelet", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has asked six bond issuers to correct French disclosure documents following a review in Val-Brume. Staff examined 28 documents made available to investors in the French-speaking region and compared key payment terms with the corresponding English versions.

Four documents omitted wording about an issuer's right to repay bonds early. Two others gave an incorrect deadline for bondholders to submit payment instructions. The affected issues have a combined face value of KRN 96 million. The review did not identify any payment already missed because of these errors.

The six issuers must publish corrected documents and notify affected holders by 30 October. The Commission has also requested evidence of checks by staff with suitable language and financial knowledge. Issuers remain responsible for the accuracy of documents prepared by outside translators. Investors who believe a discrepancy has affected a payment should contact the issuer and may also report the matter to the Commission."}

{"title": "Ostara Homes Outlook Turns Negative as Sales Slow and Debt Rises", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": "Tomas Belwick", "body": "Meridian Ratings has revised the outlook on Ostara Homes to negative from stable and affirmed its BBB minus long-term issuer rating. Slower reservations and higher spending on land have increased borrowing. The company builds mid-priced homes in Ostara, Amberford and the outer districts of Saltmarsh.

Net reservations fell 14% in the eight months to August compared with the same period last year. Completed but unsold homes increased to 246 from 171 at December. Net debt reached KRN 392 million, equivalent to 3.7 times operating profit before depreciation over the preceding 12 months.

Cash and undrawn committed facilities total KRN 168 million, against KRN 61 million of debt due within a year. This supports the rating despite weaker trading. Meridian could lower the rating if inventory continues to build or net debt remains above four times operating profit before depreciation for a sustained period."}

{"title": "Meridian Upgrades Kestrel Bay Savings After Stronger Capital and Lower Arrears", "date": "2026-09-03", "author": "Elian Voss", "body": "Meridian Ratings has upgraded Kestrel Bay Savings’ long-term issuer rating to A minus from BBB plus. The outlook is stable. The upgrade reflects a sustained improvement in capital and loan quality, supported by retained earnings and a reduction in lending to speculative property developments.

The bank’s core capital ratio reached 15.4% at the end of June, compared with 13.6% a year earlier. Loans more than 90 days overdue declined to 1.2% of gross lending from 2.0%. Customer deposits funded 86% of loans, limiting reliance on borrowing from financial markets.

These improvements give the bank greater capacity to withstand the margin pressure affecting Kestrian regional lenders. Meridian nevertheless expects earnings growth to slow as savings rates rise. The stable outlook assumes that management maintains its current lending standards and does not materially increase distributions to shareholders."}

{"title": "Kestrian Regional Banks Face Narrower Margins Despite Steady Loan Demand", "date": "2026-08-24", "author": "Elian Voss", "body": "Meridian Ratings expects profitability at Kestria’s regional banks to weaken modestly during the remainder of 2026 as deposit costs rise. Its review of eight rated lenders found that average net interest margins fell to 2.7% in June from 2.9% in December. The sector outlook remains stable.

Loans grew by 3.6% over the same period, led by small business borrowing in Ostara and residential lending around Saltmarsh. Customer deposits increased by 2.8%. Banks have offered higher rates on fixed-term accounts to retain savers, reducing the benefit of growth in interest income.

Arrears remain manageable, with loans more than 90 days overdue representing 1.8% of the reviewed portfolios. Construction borrowers account for much of the recent increase. Meridian expects lenders to maintain current capital buffers, but banks with concentrated property exposure have less room to absorb unexpected losses."}

{"title": "Meridian Affirms Fenmere Commercial Bank as Property Lending Remains Concentrated", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Elian Voss", "body": "Meridian Ratings has affirmed Fenmere Commercial Bank’s BBB long-term issuer rating with a stable outlook. The Saltmarsh-based lender benefits from steady business deposits and adequate capital. Its relatively large exposure to commercial property continues to constrain the rating, particularly lending secured on smaller office buildings.

Commercial property represented 31% of gross loans at the end of September, down from 34% a year earlier. Loans more than 90 days overdue rose to 2.5% from 2.1% over that period. Additional provisions reduced nine-month profit to KRN 47 million, 12% below the comparable period.

The bank’s core capital ratio of 14.2% provides capacity to absorb further losses within Meridian’s base forecast. Deposits increased by 3.1% during the first nine months, supporting liquidity. The stable outlook assumes gradual reductions in property concentration and no sharp deterioration among the bank’s largest borrowers."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Packaging Downgraded to BB+ Following Weaker Cash Generation", "date": "2026-09-24", "author": "Elise Dorne and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has lowered Val-Brume Packaging's senior unsecured bond rating to BB+ from BBB-. The outlook is stable. The producer of cartons and food containers has reported weaker earnings and a larger working capital requirement than Northstar assumed in its previous review.

Operating profit before depreciation fell 22% to KRN 41 million in the year ended July, while debt increased to KRN 205 million. Higher paper input costs and slower customer payments contributed to the deterioration. Debt reached 5.0 times operating profit, compared with 3.6 times a year earlier. The company serves food producers across Kestria from its principal factory in Val-Brume.

Cash of KRN 19 million and an undrawn committed facility of KRN 35 million provide adequate near-term funding. No bond principal is due before 2029. The stable outlook assumes tighter stock control and lower investment spending prevent further debt growth. Another downgrade could follow if cash generation remains negative or available funding falls materially."}

{"title": "Amberford Water Receives A- Rating for Network Renewal Bond", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Mara Vellin and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has assigned an A- rating to Amberford Water's proposed KRN 230 million senior unsecured bond, with a stable outlook. The company plans to use KRN 150 million to repay existing debt and the remainder to replace ageing mains and pumping equipment. The rating is subject to final documents matching the terms reviewed.

Amberford Water supplies 340,000 households and businesses under a licence that links permitted revenue to operating costs and approved investment. Collections remained strong at 98.6% of billed revenue in the year ended June. Water losses declined to 17.2% from 19.1% following repairs to several older sections of the network.

Northstar expects debt to remain close to 4.0 times annual operating profit before depreciation during the renewal programme. The stable outlook assumes spending stays within the approved budget and service performance supports timely cost recovery. Persistent overspending or debt above 4.8 times operating profit could put downward pressure on the rating."}

{"title": "Estuary Freight Outlook Turns Negative as Bond Refinancing Remains Unresolved", "date": "2026-09-04", "author": "Tomas Arven and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has affirmed Estuary Freight's BBB- senior unsecured bond rating and revised the outlook to negative from stable. The Kestrel Bay cargo handler has KRN 160 million of bonds falling due in February 2027. Discussions with lenders are continuing, but the company has not secured replacement funding.

At 31 July, Estuary Freight held KRN 38 million in unrestricted cash and had KRN 25 million available under committed facilities. Cargo volumes fell 6% in the first seven months of the year, mainly because of lower building material shipments. Northstar estimates debt at 4.4 times annual operating profit before depreciation, against 3.8 times at the end of 2025.

The negative outlook reflects the narrowing time available to refinance. Northstar could lower the rating if funding arrangements remain incomplete or operating performance weakens further. A return to a stable outlook would require sufficient committed funding to repay the bonds and evidence that cargo earnings have steadied."}

{"title": "Northstar Opens Consultation on Proposed Corporate Liquidity Assessment Changes", "date": "2026-09-10", "author": "Nalin Verdan and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has opened a consultation on proposed changes to its corporate liquidity methodology. The draft explains how analysts would assess seasonal cash needs, restrictions on moving cash between subsidiaries and borrowing facilities that depend on financial conditions. Written responses are invited until 2 October.

The proposal would require a separate assessment of the weakest three-month cash position within the following year. Current analysis places greater emphasis on the full twelve-month period. The draft also clarifies that an uncommitted overdraft would not count as an assured funding source, even when an issuer has used it regularly.

An initial review of 24 rated Kestrian corporate groups suggests that five could receive weaker liquidity assessments under the draft. This would not automatically change their ratings, which also reflect business strength and debt levels. The existing methodology remains in force during consultation. Northstar will publish its conclusions after reviewing responses and completing further testing."}

{"title": "Kestrian Food Producers Face Narrower Margins as Packaging Costs Rise", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Elise Dorne and Nalin Verdan", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has revised its outlook for Kestrian packaged food producers to negative from stable. The change reflects higher packaging and distribution costs, together with limited scope to raise selling prices. It is a sector assessment and does not change any individual bond rating.

Across the agency's sample of seven fictional issuers, packaging costs increased by an average of 11% in the first half of 2026, while selling prices rose 3%. Northstar expects the sample's operating margin before depreciation to fall to roughly 9% this year from 10.4% in 2025. Smaller producers supplying shops around Ostara have less purchasing power and face the greatest pressure.

Most rated producers retain adequate cash reserves and have no large bond repayments within twelve months. This should limit immediate rating pressure. The agency will focus on stock levels, customer payment times and whether cost savings protect cash generation. Companies that borrow to fund dividends while margins weaken could face individual rating reviews."}

{"title": "Northstar Affirms Bracken Grid Bonds at BBB with Stable Outlook", "date": "2026-08-17", "author": "Mara Vellin and Tomas Arven", "body": "SALTMarsh — Northstar Credit Analytics has affirmed its BBB rating on Bracken Grid's KRN 420 million senior unsecured bonds. The outlook remains stable. The electricity network operator serves 610,000 connections across Amberford and surrounding districts. Its regulated network income provides a predictable source of cash, although a substantial maintenance programme limits room for additional borrowing.

For the six months ended 30 June, revenue rose 4.2% to KRN 286 million and operating profit before depreciation reached KRN 112 million. Debt stood at 3.7 times annualised operating profit, compared with 3.9 times at December. Available cash and committed borrowing facilities covered the company's next twelve months of scheduled debt payments.

The stable outlook assumes annual capital spending of about KRN 95 million and no material acquisition. Northstar could lower the rating if debt remained above 4.5 times operating profit or network penalties materially reduced cash generation. A higher rating would require sustained debt reduction alongside reliable service performance."}

{"title": "Bracken Grid Bond Rating Holds After Amberford Storm Disruption", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Tomas Arven and Mara Vellin", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has maintained Bracken Grid's BBB senior unsecured bond rating and stable outlook following the 6 October storm in the Amberford district. The storm damaged overhead lines and interrupted supplies to 74,000 connections. By the evening of 8 October, the company had restored power to all but 1,900 affected connections.

Bracken Grid's preliminary estimate places repair costs at KRN 18 million to KRN 24 million. Northstar has assumed the full upper estimate in its initial assessment without relying on insurance receipts. The company had KRN 72 million in cash and KRN 110 million of undrawn committed facilities at the end of September.

These resources should allow repairs to proceed without materially weakening liquidity. The rating also allows for occasional weather-related disruption. Northstar will review the final repair bill, compensation payments and any changes to planned maintenance. Costs substantially above the current estimate, or evidence of wider network weaknesses, could alter the assessment."}

{"title": "Corporate Liquidity Consultation Closes with Twelve Responses Under Review", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Nalin Verdan and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics received twelve responses to its proposed corporate liquidity methodology changes before the consultation closed on 2 October. Submissions came from rated companies, bond investors and lending firms. Analysts are reviewing the comments alongside additional testing of the proposed three-month cash assessment.

Several respondents supported clearer treatment of restricted cash and uncommitted borrowing. Others asked how the agency would assess seasonal businesses whose lowest cash balance occurs shortly before a predictable collection period. Four submissions requested worked examples showing how financial conditions attached to borrowing facilities would affect available liquidity.

Northstar will consider these points before deciding whether to adopt the changes. No rating or liquidity assessment has changed as a result of the consultation, and the existing methodology remains applicable. The agency plans to publish a summary of the main comments with its final decision. Confidential financial information supplied by respondents will be excluded from that summary."}

{"title": "Estuary Freight Downgraded to BB as Refinancing Options Narrow", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Tomas Arven and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has lowered Estuary Freight's senior unsecured bond rating to BB from BBB- and concluded the review opened on 13 October. The outlook is negative. Further information supplied during the review shows that the company has limited readily available funding for its KRN 160 million February 2027 bond repayment.

Of the KRN 25 million previously reported as available under committed facilities, KRN 15 million expires in January 2027. Northstar therefore excludes that amount when assessing resources for the bond maturity. The proposed storage yard sale remains at an early stage, with no agreed buyer or binding timetable. September cargo earnings also fell below management's revised budget.

The downgrade principally reflects weaker refinancing prospects and reduced financial flexibility. The revised liquidity methodology published yesterday also informs the assessment of facility availability. The negative outlook indicates further downside if replacement funding remains unsecured. A stable outlook would require a credible, committed repayment plan and enough cash to continue ordinary operations."}

{"title": "Ostara Rental Housing Outlook Remains Stable Despite Higher Maintenance Spending", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Mara Vellin and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has maintained a stable sector outlook for rated rental housing companies operating in Ostara. High occupancy and steady rent collection continue to support earnings, although repairs and borrowing costs are absorbing more cash. The assessment covers four issuers with a combined 28,400 homes.

Average occupancy stood at 97.4% at the end of August, compared with 97.1% a year earlier. Rent arrears remained modest at 1.8% of annual billed rent. Maintenance spending rose 8%, led by roof repairs and heating replacements. Fixed interest rates apply to 76% of the sample's debt, limiting the immediate effect of higher financing costs.

Northstar expects most issuers to fund routine repairs from rental income while keeping development programmes within committed borrowing limits. Ratings could come under pressure where large construction projects coincide with debt maturities. The stable sector outlook assumes occupancy remains above 96% and companies retain enough accessible funding to meet twelve months of planned spending."}

{"title": "Northstar Adopts Revised Liquidity Methodology with Seasonal Cash Guidance", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": "Nalin Verdan and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has published its revised corporate liquidity methodology, effective today. The final document adds a review of the weakest three-month cash position within the following year and clarifies how analysts treat restricted cash, uncommitted borrowing and conditions attached to committed facilities.

Following consultation, the agency has added worked examples for seasonal businesses. Analysts may recognise expected customer receipts when supported by a consistent collection record, but must also test a reasonable delay. Cash held by a subsidiary will count only where it can legally and practically meet the obligations being assessed.

Northstar has also published an anonymised summary of the twelve consultation responses. It will apply the revised methodology to existing corporate ratings over the next three months and to new assignments immediately. Publication itself changes no ratings. Any resulting action will receive a separate release explaining the issuer's circumstances and the role of the revised assessment."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Packaging Rating Affirmed After Investment Spending Is Reduced", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Céline Maret and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has affirmed Val-Brume Packaging's BB+ senior unsecured bond rating and stable outlook after the company reduced its investment budget. Management has deferred a second printing line, cutting planned spending for the current financial year to KRN 18 million from KRN 34 million.

The decision preserves cash while the company addresses the working capital pressures behind its September downgrade. Unrestricted cash stood at KRN 22 million at the end of September, with KRN 35 million still available under its committed facility. Early collections from overdue customers helped the improvement, although one month of stronger receipts does not establish a sustained recovery.

Northstar continues to expect debt near 5.0 times annual operating profit before depreciation in the near term. The revised liquidity assessment supports the existing rating because available resources cover projected seasonal needs. An upgrade would require durable earnings recovery and lower debt. Renewed cash outflows, weaker facility access or substantial additional borrowing could lead to another downgrade."}

{"title": "Estuary Freight Placed Under Review After Refinancing Talks End", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Tomas Arven and Nalin Verdan", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has placed Estuary Freight's BBB- senior unsecured bond rating under review for downgrade. The action follows the end of negotiations with a proposed lead lender without a refinancing agreement. The company's KRN 160 million bond remains due in February 2027.

Estuary Freight informed Northstar that it is approaching other lenders and considering the sale of a storage yard near Kestrel Bay. Neither option currently provides committed funds. Unrestricted cash was KRN 31 million at 30 September, down from KRN 38 million at 31 July. Available committed borrowing remained KRN 25 million, and trading continued below the agency's earlier expectations.

The review will focus on the size, timing and conditions of alternative funding. Northstar expects to resolve it once sufficient information is available and may lower the rating by more than one grade. A property valuation or an expression of lender interest alone would not establish that the February repayment can be met."}

{"title": "Kestrian Retail Bond Issuers Enter Winter with Uneven Cash Buffers", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Elise Dorne and Nalin Verdan", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has maintained a negative outlook for Kestrian discretionary retailers in its latest sector review. Households are limiting larger purchases, while retailers have already paid for much of their winter stock. The agency expects differences in cash reserves to matter more than modest changes in reported sales.

Among six rated issuers, stock values at the end of September were 9% higher than a year earlier, while sales for the preceding three months rose only 1%. Two chains operating mainly in Saltmarsh and Kestrel Bay increased promotional discounts to clear older goods. Across the sample, average operating margins before depreciation fell to 6.2% from 7.1%.

Four issuers have committed facilities extending beyond their seasonal borrowing peak. The remaining two rely more heavily on cash generated during winter trading. Northstar is testing slower stock sales and delayed supplier credit separately for each company. The sector outlook does not imply that every retailer will be downgraded, and no individual rating changes accompany this report."}

{"title": "Coppermere Components Upgraded to BBB After Sustained Debt Reduction", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Elise Dorne and Mara Vellin", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has raised Coppermere Components' senior unsecured bond rating to BBB from BBB-, with a stable outlook. The Amberford manufacturer supplies replacement parts for industrial pumps and ventilation equipment. The upgrade reflects sustained debt reduction, a broader customer base and improved cash collection.

Debt fell to KRN 126 million at the end of August from KRN 171 million eighteen months earlier. Over the same period, debt relative to annual operating profit before depreciation declined from 3.8 times to 2.6 times. The largest customer now accounts for 14% of revenue, compared with 23% two years ago. Replacement orders provide more consistent demand than sales tied to new construction.

Cash and committed facilities comfortably cover the company's lowest projected three-month funding position under Northstar's revised methodology. The stable outlook assumes moderate investment and no large debt-funded acquisition. A downgrade could follow if debt rises above 3.5 times operating profit for a sustained period or customer concentration increases materially."}

{"title": "Business reference rate falls by a further quarter point", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Mara Ellery", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank will lower its business reference rate from 6.25% to 6.00% on 21 September. Borrowers whose agreements track this rate will receive the reduction automatically. The change follows the quarter-point reduction that took effect on 24 August.

Together, the two changes reduce annual interest by KRN 5,000 on an unchanged KRN 1 million balance, before any fees and subject to the terms of the agreement. Fixed-rate borrowing is unaffected. Loans linked to a different reference rate will continue to follow the calculation set out in their contracts.

The bank reviewed its funding costs and lending commitments before making the decision. Updated repayment information will be sent where monthly payments change. Customers with interest-only facilities will see the new rate applied to the relevant days in their next interest calculation and can request a breakdown from their relationship team."}

{"title": "Equipment programme expands after strong demand from regional manufacturers", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": "Devran Holt", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has increased its equipment lending programme from KRN 40 million to KRN 60 million. Since applications opened on 24 August, the bank has approved KRN 26.4 million across 19 facilities. Of that amount, KRN 11.8 million has been drawn by borrowers.

Companies in Amberford and Ostara account for just over half the approved lending by value. Purchases include cutting machines, temperature-controlled storage and packing lines. Approval totals represent agreed facilities rather than equipment already installed, and borrowers draw funds as supplier invoices become due.

The additional allocation is available to new applications from today. Loan sizes remain between KRN 250,000 and KRN 4 million, with terms of three to seven years. Each application will receive a separate credit assessment. Businesses should obtain approval before relying on bank funding to place a binding equipment order."}

{"title": "Bank publishes revised fees for business lending administration", "date": "2026-09-02", "author": "Leonie Venn", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has published a revised schedule of business lending administration fees, taking effect on 5 October. The fee for a borrower-requested security amendment will fall from KRN 450 to KRN 325. Providing a duplicate annual interest certificate will become free, replacing the current KRN 35 charge.

The fee for reviewing a request to extend an overdraft temporarily will rise from KRN 175 to KRN 225. It covers the review and does not guarantee approval. Customers will be told whether a charge applies before they ask the bank to proceed with any chargeable work.

The changes do not alter agreed loan margins or repayment dates. Where an existing agreement fixes an administration fee, that agreement will continue to apply. The full schedule is available from relationship teams and on the bank’s website, with copies in French available for customers in Val-Brume."}

{"title": "Val-Brume lending desk adds appointments for French-speaking businesses", "date": "2026-09-03", "author": "Tomas Arlen", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has added twelve weekly appointments at its Val-Brume lending desk for businesses that prefer to discuss borrowing in French. The additional sessions are available on Tuesdays and Thursdays and can be held at the branch or by telephone.

Appointments cover working capital, equipment purchases and reviews of existing facilities. Businesses can request a document checklist in French before their meeting. The desk will also help customers identify which financial records are needed, although it cannot prepare accounts or provide independent legal advice.

There is no appointment charge and no obligation to apply for a loan. Lending decisions remain subject to the bank’s usual affordability and security checks. The expansion follows a six-week booking review in which the Val-Brume team recorded an average wait of nine working days for a French-language lending appointment."}

{"title": "New equipment lending programme opens with forty million crowns", "date": "2026-08-24", "author": "Devran Holt", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank today opened a KRN 40 million lending programme for mid-sized companies replacing production equipment. Businesses across Kestria can apply for loans of between KRN 250,000 and KRN 4 million, with repayment terms of three to seven years.

Eligible purchases include workshop machinery, commercial refrigeration and equipment used to sort or pack goods. Applicants must provide equipment quotations, their latest accounts and a cash-flow forecast. The bank will assess whether repayments remain affordable during quieter trading periods. Purchased equipment will normally form part of the security.

Both existing customers and companies new to the bank may apply. Interest rates and arrangement fees will be set for each borrower and disclosed before an agreement is signed. Applications are open through the bank's lending teams, including its industrial business desks in Amberford and Kestrel Bay."}

{"title": "Ostara businesses offered working capital review appointments this month", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Tomas Arlen", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank is offering 30 working capital review appointments for mid-sized businesses at its Ostara branch between 12 and 30 October. The sessions are intended for companies preparing for seasonal changes in stock purchases, customer payments or supplier bills.

Each appointment lasts up to an hour and carries no charge. Businesses should bring a thirteen-week cash-flow forecast, a list of unpaid customer invoices and details of existing borrowing. A relationship manager will discuss the timing of expected receipts and payments and identify any information needed for a lending application.

Attendance does not require a company to move its banking arrangements. Any subsequent request for an overdraft or loan will be assessed separately, with rates and fees explained before acceptance. Telephone appointments are available for businesses outside Ostara that cannot attend the branch during the scheduled period."}

{"title": "Harrow & Finch Bank lowers its business reference rate", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Ellery", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank will reduce its business reference rate from 6.50% to 6.25% on 24 August. The change applies to lending agreements that track the bank’s reference rate. Fixed-rate loans will keep their agreed rates until the end of their fixed terms.

A company with a KRN 2 million loan priced at the reference rate plus 2 percentage points would pay around KRN 417 less interest over a full month, assuming its balance stays unchanged. Actual savings will depend on the number of days in the interest period and the outstanding balance.

The bank is sending notices to affected borrowers this week. Customers do not need to submit a request to receive the reduction. Relationship teams in Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume can explain how the change will appear on individual statements."}

{"title": "Revised administration fees take effect for business borrowers today", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Leonie Venn", "body": "The business lending administration fees announced by Harrow & Finch Bank on 2 September take effect today. A borrower-requested security amendment now costs KRN 325, down from KRN 450. Duplicate annual interest certificates are now free, removing the previous KRN 35 charge.

Reviewing a request for a temporary overdraft extension now costs KRN 225, an increase of KRN 50. The review fee does not depend on whether the request is approved. Staff must explain the applicable fee and obtain the customer's agreement before starting a chargeable review.

Requests accepted before today will be charged under the previous schedule. Existing contracts that specify a fixed administration fee retain that fee. Customers can obtain the current schedule from any lending team or download it from the bank's website. A French-language version is also available through the Val-Brume desk."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay lending team moves to larger customer premises", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Tomas Arlen", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank’s Kestrel Bay lending team will move to 18 Lantern Quay on 19 October. The new premises provide four private meeting rooms, compared with two at the current office, and a ground-floor entrance with step-free access from the pavement.

The office will serve mid-sized companies seeking lending advice and support with existing facilities. Customer telephone numbers and relationship managers will remain the same. Meetings already booked for 19 October onwards will take place at Lantern Quay, and the bank is contacting customers individually with directions.

The current office at 7 Tern Passage closes to visitors at 16:00 today. Telephone support will remain available during normal opening hours throughout the move. The new office opens at 09:00 on 19 October. It is an appointment-based lending office and does not provide cash deposits, withdrawals or counter payment services."}

{"title": "Bank introduces clearer monthly statements for variable-rate business loans", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Leonie Venn", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has introduced a revised monthly statement layout for variable-rate business loans. Statements issued from today show the reference rate, the borrower’s agreed margin and the number of days charged at each rate in a single interest table.

Where a rate changes during the statement period, separate rows show the balance and calculation for each part of that period. This will help borrowers check interest following the bank’s reference rate reductions in August and September. Fees will continue to appear separately from interest.

The layout changes how information is presented and does not alter loan terms or payment dates. It was tested with 24 business customers from Saltmarsh, Amberford and Val-Brume. Large-print statements and an explanation in French are available on request. Customers who need help checking a calculation can contact the lending team named on their statement."}

{"title": "Half-year results show lending growth and higher impairment charges", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Mara Ellery", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank today reported profit before tax of KRN 18.6 million for the six months ended 30 September 2026, compared with KRN 17.9 million a year earlier. Operating income rose to KRN 52.4 million from KRN 48.7 million, while operating costs increased to KRN 27.1 million from KRN 25.6 million.

The charge for expected lending losses increased to KRN 6.7 million from KRN 5.2 million. The increase mainly reflected weaker repayment prospects among a small number of wholesale borrowers. Gross customer lending reached KRN 1.24 billion at the period end, up from KRN 1.17 billion on 31 March 2026.

New lending drawn during the half year totalled KRN 186 million. Customer deposits stood at KRN 1.41 billion on 30 September. The bank continued to fund equipment purchases and working capital across Kestria while reviewing borrowers showing early signs of payment difficulty. The results are unaudited."}

{"title": "New borrower support team offers earlier repayment difficulty reviews", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Devran Holt", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has established a dedicated borrower support team for business customers facing repayment difficulties. The five-person team is based in Saltmarsh and accepts referrals from relationship managers across Kestria. Customers can also ask their usual lending contact for a support review.

The team will examine recent trading, cash forecasts and existing repayment commitments. Depending on the circumstances, options may include changing payment dates or agreeing a temporary reduction in principal repayments. Any arrangement requires an assessment and written agreement. Interest may continue to accrue, and extending a loan can increase its total cost.

There is no charge for the initial support review. Customers are encouraged to make contact as soon as they expect a shortfall, even if every payment is currently up to date. French-language reviews are available through the Val-Brume lending desk. Requesting a review does not itself change contractual payment obligations."}

{"title": "Half-year sales rise as GreenBasket reports lower operating profit", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Daniel Rooke", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets, Kestria's largest supermarket chain by store count, reported revenue of KRN 6.42 billion for the 26 weeks ended 30 August 2026, up 4.1 per cent on the comparable period. Sales at stores open for at least a year rose 2.7 per cent.

Operating profit fell from KRN 206 million to KRN 193 million. Higher wage and refrigeration maintenance costs outweighed savings in distribution. GreenBasket invested KRN 118 million in stores, equipment and delivery vehicles during the period, including the Eastmere opening in Amberford. It ended the half year with 215 stores.

Finance director Anika Pell said customers were buying more own-label groceries and making smaller, more frequent trips. Own-label products accounted for 58 per cent of grocery units sold, compared with 55 per cent a year earlier. Online orders represented 9.4 per cent of revenue, up from 8.7 per cent in the comparable period."}

{"title": "New Val-Brume supermarket brings GreenBasket network to 216 stores", "date": "2026-09-21", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets opened its 216th store today in the Montelier district of Val-Brume. The KRN 8.6 million investment has created 62 jobs. The store has 2,100 square metres of sales space and includes a bakery, fresh fish counter and covered collection bays for online orders.

Signs, customer information and checkout instructions are available in French, reflecting the language used across the region. Store manager Lucien Varel said all customer-facing teams included French-speaking colleagues. The opening range includes cheese, preserves and baked goods from 29 regional producers, with supplier names displayed beside the products.

The building occupies a former storage yard that had stood empty for six years. GreenBasket retained the site's boundary trees and installed a rainwater tank for cleaning outdoor areas. A pedestrian entrance connects directly with the Montelier footpath, while bicycle stands and customer parking are located on opposite sides of the building."}

{"title": "GreenBasket recalls selected oat biscuits after incorrect allergen labelling", "date": "2026-09-09", "author": "Sofia Merrow", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets is recalling its 250-gram Honeyfield Oat Biscuits with batch code HB0902 and a best-before date of 18 February 2027. The affected packs contain milk, which is missing from the allergen statement. They are unsuitable for people with a milk allergy.

The biscuits were sold in 87 GreenBasket stores across Kestria between 3 and 8 September. Customers with a milk allergy should not eat them. Anyone who bought an affected pack can return it to any GreenBasket store for a full refund without a receipt. Other batch codes are not included in this recall.

GreenBasket identified the error during a packaging check yesterday and removed the affected stock from sale that evening. Recall notices are displayed in the relevant stores and on the customer website. Product safety manager Orla Venn said the company had received no reports of illness linked to the affected batch."}

{"title": "Small suppliers receive shorter payment terms under new purchasing policy", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Idris Weller", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has introduced payment terms of 14 calendar days for qualifying small suppliers, replacing its previous standard of 30 days. The policy applies to new purchase orders issued from today. A total of 186 existing suppliers meet the published eligibility rules.

Suppliers qualify if their annual turnover is below KRN 12 million and they employ fewer than 50 people. Payment is measured from receipt of a valid invoice after delivery. GreenBasket has removed the administration charge previously applied when suppliers requested payment before their contractual due date.

Procurement director Nella Corven said smaller producers often paid wages and bought materials well before receiving money for their goods. A dedicated accounts team in Saltmarsh now handles questions about the scheme. Suppliers can check eligibility and invoice status through their existing account, or request the same information by telephone."}

{"title": "Refitted Kestrel Bay store adds quieter shopping sessions", "date": "2026-09-08", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has completed a KRN 3.8 million refurbishment of its Harbour Steps store in Kestrel Bay. The shop remained open throughout the eight-week project. Work included replacement refrigeration, clearer aisle signs and a redesigned entrance with more space around baskets and trolleys.

The store now offers quieter shopping sessions on Tuesday and Thursday mornings between nine and ten. During these sessions, background music is switched off, routine announcements are suspended and colleagues reduce trolley movements on the shop floor. Essential safety announcements remain in use.

Customer services lead Elian Dacre said the changes followed comments from 164 shoppers who took part in a local survey. The refurbishment also added a seated service point for customers who find standing difficult. Printed store maps are available at the entrance, and colleagues can provide help finding products throughout opening hours."}

{"title": "GreenBasket opens new neighbourhood store in eastern Amberford", "date": "2026-08-17", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets opened its Eastmere store in Amberford today, bringing its network to 215 shops across Kestria. The 1,420-square-metre store employs 46 people, including 31 colleagues recruited locally. It has eight staffed checkouts, a bakery and a collection desk for online orders.

The store stocks bread, dairy products and vegetables from 23 suppliers based within 80 kilometres of Amberford. Shelf labels identify where these products come from. Customers can buy loose portions of 34 fruit and vegetable lines, with weighing scales beside the produce section.

Store manager Tessa Brindle said the layout had been tested with residents before opening. Changes included wider gaps between promotional displays and lower shelving near the entrance. The store also has step-free access, two accessible checkout lanes and a covered seating area beside the main doors."}

{"title": "Ostara depot changes cut damaged produce deliveries by nearly a quarter", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Idris Weller", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has reduced damaged fruit and vegetable deliveries from its Ostara depot by 24 per cent following changes to packing and loading. The result compares damage recorded per thousand crates during July to September with the same three months of 2025.

The depot supplies 68 stores and handles about 410,000 produce crates each month. Changes included shallower crates for soft fruit, separate loading positions for heavier root vegetables and temperature checks at each loading bay. GreenBasket spent KRN 1.7 million on equipment and training for the work.

Depot manager Brenna Holt said staff had recorded where damage occurred before changing handling routines. The measured reduction represents approximately 7,800 fewer damaged crates over the quarter. Produce rejected by stores remains subject to the same quality checks, and items suitable for sale are assessed separately from stock that must be discarded."}

{"title": "GreenBasket publishes clearer rules for supplier deductions and invoice disputes", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Idris Weller", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has introduced a supplier code covering invoice disputes, delivery deductions and changes to orders. Effective today, the code requires buyers to provide written reasons and supporting records before making a deduction. It applies to all goods suppliers across the company's 216 stores.

Suppliers now have a single route for challenging deductions through a commercial review team separate from their usual buyer. The code sets a ten-working-day deadline for an initial written response. Undisputed invoice amounts remain payable under the supplier's agreed terms while a challenge is examined.

Procurement director Nella Corven said discussions with 73 suppliers had highlighted inconsistent explanations for shortages and damaged deliveries. The new code also requires written agreement before suppliers are charged for a promotion. It does not change the 14-day payment terms for eligible small businesses, whose first payment report was published yesterday."}

{"title": "First payment report shows progress for GreenBasket small suppliers", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": "Daniel Rooke", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has published its first payment report since introducing 14-day terms for qualifying small suppliers on 26 August. Of the 2,460 invoices due under the scheme by 30 September, 96.2 per cent were paid within the agreed period. Average payment time was 12.6 calendar days.

The report covers invoices with a combined value of KRN 18.4 million. Ninety-four were paid late, most following internal approval delays. All 94 had been settled by 9 October. GreenBasket has added a daily review of approaching payment deadlines and assigned a named contact to each participating supplier.

Finance director Anika Pell said the late payments fell short of the company's commitment. The report lists disputed invoices separately and does not count them as paid on time. Seventeen invoices remained in dispute at the reporting date, with reasons supplied to the businesses concerned. The figures cover the small-supplier scheme rather than GreenBasket's entire supplier base."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay branch sets temporary hours during roof repairs", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": null, "body": "Lumen Home Retail will shorten opening hours at its Kestrel Bay Harbour Lane branch while contractors replace part of the roof. From 5 to 16 October, the store will open from 9 am to 6 pm each day. Its usual opening hours are 7 am to 9 pm.

A routine building inspection found worn seals above the homeware stockroom. The affected storage area has been cleared, and work will take place behind a separate barrier. The repair budget is KRN 640,000. Grocery shopping, the pharmacy counter and customer toilets will remain available during the temporary opening hours.

Online collection customers with bookings outside the revised hours will be contacted directly and offered another slot or free home delivery. Staff will retain their contracted pay, with some shifts moved to stock preparation and nearby delivery operations. Lumen expects normal hours to resume on 17 October, subject to the final building inspection."}

{"title": "GreenBasket lowers prices on  eighty everyday grocery products nationwide", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Sofia Merrow", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has reduced regular shelf prices on 80 everyday grocery products across all 216 stores and its online shop. The changes took effect today and cover selected bread, rice, pasta, milk and household cleaning products. No membership or minimum purchase is required.

A one-kilogram bag of GreenBasket long-grain rice now costs KRN 14.20, down from KRN 15.80. A 500-gram pack of dried pasta has fallen from KRN 9.40 to KRN 8.60. Across the 80 products, the average price reduction is 6.3 per cent, calculated without weighting for sales volumes.

Commercial director Rowan Selwick said the reductions were funded through lower packaging costs, simpler product ranges and reduced handling expenses. Existing supplier payment terms remain in force. Stores display the previous and current prices beside affected products, and the full list is available at customer service desks and on the GreenBasket website."}

{"title": "Vegetable growers join longer supply programme for Kestrian produce", "date": "2026-09-25", "author": "Mara Selwick", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has agreed a two-year vegetable supply programme with Reedplain Growers, a group of 18 farms outside Amberford. The programme covers potatoes, carrots, onions and cabbages sold in Lumen stores across Kestria. The first deliveries under the new terms begin on 1 October.

The agreement sets minimum purchase volumes for each crop and provides a quarterly review of growing, packing and transport costs. Lumen expects to buy about 6,400 tonnes during the first year. Orders will include smaller and unevenly shaped vegetables for its lower-priced cooking bags, provided they meet the same freshness requirements as other produce.

Participating farms will receive payment within 14 days of an accepted delivery. Lumen will share a rolling 12-week forecast, updated each week, to help growers plan harvesting and labour. Produce labels will identify the growing area, while store teams will receive guidance on storage temperatures and handling to reduce damage between delivery and sale."}

{"title": "Lumen agrees new credit facility to support planned store investment", "date": "2026-09-23", "author": "Daren Pell", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has signed a KRN 120 million revolving credit facility with Brackenmere Commercial Bank and Ternfold Lending. The facility matures in September 2030 and replaces a KRN 90 million agreement that was due to expire next year. It provides funds that Lumen can draw and repay as needed.

At signing, KRN 28 million was drawn under the new agreement to repay the outstanding balance on the previous facility. The remaining capacity is available for seasonal stock purchases and planned investment in stores and distribution equipment. No shares were issued as part of the financing.

Finance director Sella Morven said the agreement gave Lumen greater certainty over funding while retaining room for quieter trading periods. Interest is charged at a variable reference rate plus an agreed margin. The facility includes quarterly borrowing and interest-cover tests. Lumen met those requirements at signing and has not changed its full-year investment budget of KRN 34 million."}

{"title": "New Ostara bakery agreement brings daily deliveries to more stores", "date": "2026-09-16", "author": "Mara Selwick", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has signed a three-year supply agreement with Hearthmere Baking, a family-owned producer based in Ostara. From 21 September, the bakery will supply bread and rolls to 14 Lumen stores, extending an existing arrangement covering six branches. The agreement is worth an estimated KRN 7.2 million over its term.

Hearthmere has installed an additional oven and created nine production and packing jobs to support the wider service. Deliveries will reach stores before opening on six mornings each week. The range includes a small sliced loaf developed after customers asked for more choices suited to smaller households.

Lumen will provide weekly order forecasts and pay agreed invoices within 21 days. Both businesses will review unsold quantities each month, with the aim of reducing surplus without leaving shelves empty late in the day. Existing bakery suppliers will continue serving Lumen stores outside the expanded delivery area."}

{"title": "Amberford store introduces earlier collections and a simpler entrance", "date": "2026-09-08", "author": "Tobin Rusk", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has completed a KRN 1.8 million refurbishment of its Amberford Millgate store. The work adds a separate collection counter beside the main entrance, wider aisles in the grocery section and clearer signs for household essentials. The store remained open throughout the six-week project.

Customers can now collect online orders from 7 am, one hour earlier than before. The counter has space for chilled orders and eight numbered collection bays outside. Lumen has added 12 roles at the store, taking its workforce to 96, with recruitment completed locally.

Store manager Orla Fenwick said feedback from customers had shaped the entrance layout, particularly the position of baskets and the service desk. The refurbishment also includes enclosed refrigerated cabinets and new lighting. Lumen will compare electricity use over the next three months with the same period last year before deciding whether to extend those changes to other stores."}

{"title": "Lumen reports steady sales growth across food and home ranges", "date": "2026-09-02", "author": null, "body": "Lumen Home Retail recorded sales of KRN 486 million in the 13 weeks ended 29 August, an increase of 4.1% on the same period last year. Sales at stores open for at least a year rose by 2.3%. Grocery sales grew most strongly, while demand for kitchen storage and everyday bedding also improved.

The retailer ended the period with 42 stores across Kestria. Online orders accounted for 8.6% of sales, compared with 7.9% a year earlier. Collection orders increased particularly quickly in Saltmarsh and Amberford, where additional collection slots were introduced during the quarter.

Chief executive Neris Talven said customers remained careful about larger purchases and were comparing prices closely. Lumen kept prices unchanged on its 150-item Everyday Basket throughout the quarter. Higher delivery costs were partly offset by fuller vehicle loads and lower food waste. The company maintained its existing expectations for the financial year."}

{"title": "Val-Brume stores introduce clearer French labels across household essentials", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Elira Venn", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has introduced clearer French-language shelf labels at its three Val-Brume stores. The change covers 2,300 household and grocery products, including cleaning supplies, bedding and cupboard staples. Product names, pack sizes and unit prices now appear together in a larger, consistent format.

The work follows an eight-week review with customers and store colleagues in Kestria's French-speaking region. Participants said that unit prices were sometimes difficult to locate and that several household product descriptions needed simpler wording. Printed guidance at service desks and online collection instructions have also been revised.

The project cost KRN 210,000, including translation, printing and staff training. It does not change product prices or the ranges stocked. Lumen will collect feedback at the three stores through November and check a sample of labels each week for accuracy. Customers can report unclear wording at any service desk or through the retailer's website."}

{"title": "Lumen reduces prices on forty everyday food and cleaning products", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Daren Pell", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has reduced prices on 40 products in its Everyday Basket range, effective today across all 42 stores and online. The reductions average 5.4% and cover items including porridge oats, dried beans, washing-up liquid and kitchen cloths. Pack sizes and product specifications remain the same.

A 1 kg bag of porridge oats now costs KRN 8.40, down from KRN 8.90, while a 500 ml bottle of washing-up liquid falls from KRN 6.20 to KRN 5.80. Shelf labels show the previous price alongside the new price to help customers compare.

Lower packaging costs and changes to delivery schedules have helped fund the reductions. Lumen has committed to hold the new prices until at least 31 December. The remaining 110 Everyday Basket products retain their existing prices. The company said the reductions were included in its current trading plans and did not alter its financial expectations for the year."}

{"title": "Lumen outlines Saltmarsh depot upgrade ahead of winter trading", "date": "2026-10-23", "author": "Elira Venn", "body": "Lumen Home Retail will invest KRN 4.6 million in new sorting equipment and additional chilled holding space at its Saltmarsh distribution depot. Installation is scheduled to begin on 2 November and finish in stages by mid-December. The project forms part of the retailer's existing KRN 34 million annual investment budget.

The upgrade will add 320 chilled pallet spaces and replace two ageing conveyor sections used for household goods. Lumen expects the changes to reduce manual handling and give stores more flexibility when ordering fresh food. The depot currently supplies 27 of the company's 42 stores, including branches in Ostara and Val-Brume.

Work will take place in separate sections while normal deliveries continue. Lumen has leased temporary chilled storage for the installation period and scheduled staff training before each new section enters use. Eight maintenance and warehouse roles are being recruited. The company expects to assess delivery accuracy and equipment reliability after the upgraded sections have operated for eight weeks."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay store resumes full hours after completed roof repairs", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Tobin Rusk", "body": "Lumen Home Retail's Harbour Lane store in Kestrel Bay resumed its usual 7 am to 9 pm opening hours on 17 October, following completion of roof repairs. The final inspection was completed on 16 October, allowing the homeware stockroom to return to use before the store reopened the following morning.

The work replaced worn roof seals and a damaged section of insulation above the stockroom. The final cost was KRN 618,000, compared with the KRN 640,000 budget. Contractors completed the repairs within the planned period, and the grocery section remained open throughout the temporary reduction in hours.

During the work, 286 collection bookings were moved to alternative times and 74 customers accepted free home delivery. Store manager Iven Rosk thanked customers for adjusting their visits and staff for covering revised shifts. The temporary collection arrangements have now ended, and the full homeware range is available on the shop floor again."}

{"title": "Ostara tray plant cuts trim waste through tooling changes", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": "Tobin Merrow", "body": "Corvel Packaging's Ostara plant has reduced trim waste on two chilled-food tray lines following changes to cutting tools and sheet positioning. Across eight weeks of measured production, trim represented 13.1 per cent of material fed into the lines, down from 16.4 per cent during the preceding eight weeks.

The comparison covers the same four tray designs and excludes trial runs. Staff adjusted the spacing between tray shapes and replaced worn cutting edges. The plant used 18 tonnes less sheet material than it would have used at the earlier trim rate for the recorded volume.

Tray dimensions, sealing surfaces and approved material specifications are unchanged. Clean trim from these lines is separated for controlled reprocessing, subject to the requirements of each product specification. Corvel is reporting the reduction as a material efficiency measure; it has not calculated a carbon saving from the work."}

{"title": "Energy surcharge rises as packaging production costs increase", "date": "2026-09-22", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has increased its energy surcharge from KRN 42 to KRN 58 per tonne for goods dispatched from today. The rate covers cartons, trays and films made at its three production sites. Base prices remain unchanged under this adjustment.

Purchased energy costs have risen further since the surcharge was introduced on 31 August. The latest four weeks of production records show an average energy cost increase of KRN 67 per tonne against the company's May reference level. Corvel is absorbing the difference between that increase and the surcharge.

An order containing 2.5 tonnes of packaging now carries a surcharge of KRN 145 before tax. The dispatch date determines the applicable rate, including for orders placed earlier. Invoice weight details remain available by product line. Customers with a fixed-price agreement should refer to its surcharge terms or contact their account manager for an explanation."}

{"title": "Corvel updates carton prices following higher board costs", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has increased base prices for newly quoted folding cartons by an average of 3.4 per cent from today. Individual changes range from 2.1 to 4.6 per cent, depending on board grade, carton weight and print requirements. Confirmed orders retain their agreed base prices.

The adjustment follows higher delivered board costs recorded during August and September. It applies to cartons produced at Amberford and does not change the base prices of trays or films. The energy surcharge remains separate at KRN 58 per tonne and is calculated using dispatch weight.

Revised quotations show the base price and estimated surcharge separately so customers can compare the full cost at a stated order quantity. Existing written quotations remain valid until their stated expiry dates. Account teams can review board grades or order sizes with customers, although any specification change still requires approval before production."}

{"title": "Corvel introduces separate energy surcharge on food packaging orders", "date": "2026-08-31", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has introduced an energy surcharge of KRN 42 per tonne on cartons, trays and films dispatched from today. The charge applies to domestic orders across Kestria and appears as a separate invoice line. Base product prices are unchanged.

The surcharge reflects higher electricity and process heating costs at the company's Amberford, Ostara and Kestrel Bay plants. Corvel's combined energy cost per tonne of finished packaging was 14 per cent higher in August than in May, despite lower consumption on several production lines.

For an order weighing 2.5 tonnes, the surcharge is KRN 105 before tax. It does not apply to goods dispatched before today, even where the invoice is issued later. Customer statements show the dispatch weight used in the calculation, and account teams have received a common worksheet to explain the charge and resolve weight queries."}

{"title": "Corvel publishes clearer weight details on packaging invoices", "date": "2026-09-01", "author": "Nadia Pell", "body": "Corvel Packaging has updated its invoices to show the net packaging weight used to calculate its energy surcharge. The revised layout lists product weight, surcharge rate and surcharge value together. Pallets, outer wrapping and returnable transport frames are excluded from the calculation.

The change follows questions received when the KRN 42 per tonne surcharge took effect yesterday. For mixed deliveries, each product line carries its own weight and charge. Where an order is split between dispatches, the invoice includes only the quantity sent in that delivery.

Customers can also obtain the same weight breakdown from their account contact without requesting a replacement invoice. Corvel has checked the revised layout against 30 sample orders covering cartons, trays and films. The checks found no change to the amount charged; the update makes the calculation easier to follow and compare with delivery records."}

{"title": "Corvel Packaging adds carton capacity at Amberford plant", "date": "2026-08-25", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Corvel Packaging has brought a second carton folding line into service at its Amberford plant. The line adds capacity for 1.8 million food cartons a month, increasing the site's monthly folding capacity to 7.2 million units. It handles sleeves and folding cartons used for chilled meals, bakery products and dry foods.

Installation and production trials cost KRN 4.6 million. During acceptance checks, staff ran six carton sizes and measured fold accuracy, glue application and print alignment. Twelve employees have completed training on the equipment, including operators transferred from the site's older finishing area.

The additional capacity addresses demand from customers in Amberford and Saltmarsh. Orders remain subject to the usual checks on board availability and artwork approval. Customers with approved carton designs can ask their account contact to review order quantities against the expanded production schedule."}

{"title": "Corvel publishes evidence sheets for selected carton recycling claims", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Tobin Merrow", "body": "Corvel Packaging has issued evidence sheets for 18 uncoated carton specifications supplied from Amberford. Each sheet identifies the board grade, coatings, inks and adhesives covered by the assessment. It also explains the conditions attached to the company's recycling statements.

The sheets draw on fibre separation tests and checks of collection guidance available in Saltmarsh, Amberford and Val-Brume. Results apply only to the listed specifications. Cartons with film windows, barrier layers or heavy food contamination require separate consideration and are outside the scope of this release.

Customers can request a sheet using their carton specification number. The documents support packaging decisions but do not establish that every local collection service accepts a carton. Corvel advises customers to check disposal wording against the collection arrangements serving their buyers. No new recycling claim has been added to customer artwork as part of this work."}

{"title": "Corvel adds recycled content option for selected food trays", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Tobin Merrow", "body": "Corvel Packaging has added a tray material containing 35 per cent recycled plastic by weight to its Ostara product range. The option is available for two clear chilled-food tray sizes. It uses recycled material from a controlled process assessed for the specified food contact conditions.

Qualification checks covered material traceability, tray strength, sealing and migration under chilled storage conditions. The trays are approved for the listed chilled foods and temperatures in their technical sheets. They are not approved for oven use. Customers must confirm that their intended food and filling process match those conditions.

The recycled content figure applies to the tray body and excludes any lid, label or absorbent pad. Batch records identify the material blend used in production. Initial supply is available in minimum orders of 40,000 trays per size, with pricing provided by quotation. Existing tray specifications remain available for customers with different requirements."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay film line returns after planned maintenance", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Corvel Packaging has returned its second film line at Kestrel Bay to normal production following a four-day maintenance shutdown. Work included replacing heater controls, checking winding equipment and servicing the cooling system. The line produces sealing films for prepared-food trays.

Operators completed restart checks on three film thicknesses before releasing production. Tests covered thickness variation, roll tension and seal performance against approved tray samples. All three trial batches met their existing specifications. The work has not changed the materials used or the approved uses of the films.

Corvel supplied scheduled orders from stock built before the shutdown, and no confirmed delivery dates were missed. Both film lines at Kestrel Bay are now operating. Customers receiving material from the restarted line can find the production batch reference on each roll label and on the accompanying quality record."}

{"title": "Corvel opens French language customer desk in Val-Brume", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Nadia Pell", "body": "Corvel Packaging has opened a French language customer desk in Val-Brume to support food producers in Kestria's French-speaking region. Three customer service staff now handle order enquiries, delivery updates and invoice questions from the office. The desk operates on weekdays from 08:30 to 17:00 local time.

Customers can request quotations, specification summaries and surcharge explanations in French. Technical approval remains with the production site responsible for the packaging. The desk uses the same order records as Corvel's Saltmarsh team, allowing staff to check quantities and dispatch information without creating a separate customer account.

The office supports customers buying cartons, trays and films; it does not hold stock or receive returned goods. During its internal service trial, staff handled 64 sample enquiries covering all three product groups. Customers already assigned an account manager retain that contact and can use the new desk for routine order support."}

{"title": "Corvel expands returnable pallet scheme after Saltmarsh customer trial", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Nadia Pell", "body": "Corvel Packaging has expanded its returnable pallet scheme to regular delivery routes serving Amberford and Val-Brume. The scheme follows a 12-week trial with eight food producers in Saltmarsh. Participating customers returned 438 of the 480 pallets issued during the trial.

Of the returned pallets, 421 passed inspection for reuse and 17 required repair. Corvel records each pallet by reference number and collects empty pallets during an agreed delivery visit. The scheme uses a refundable deposit of KRN 85 per pallet, shown separately from packaging and transport charges.

Customers need a dry storage area and enough space to hold pallets safely between collections. Deposits are credited after return and inspection, subject to the scheme's damage terms. Availability depends on the delivery route and collection space. The trial measured returns and condition; Corvel has not used those results to publish a carbon saving claim."}

{"title": "Amberford carton line completes first full production month", "date": "2026-10-12", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Corvel Packaging's new Amberford folding line produced 1.42 million saleable cartons during September, its first full calendar month in service. The line entered production on 25 August and has a rated monthly capacity of 1.8 million cartons under the company's standard shift pattern.

September output covered 23 customer designs, including chilled-meal sleeves and bakery cartons. Average time between completing one job and starting the next fell from 74 minutes in the first production week to 56 minutes in the final week. Operators recorded these times using the same changeover checklist.

The plant dispatched 96 per cent of September carton orders on their confirmed dates, compared with 92 per cent in August. Corvel attributes the improvement partly to the additional line and partly to steadier board deliveries. The figures cover all Amberford carton orders, rather than only work assigned to the new equipment."}

{"title": "Corvel lowers energy surcharge following reduced purchased power costs", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has reduced its energy surcharge from KRN 58 to KRN 31 per tonne for goods dispatched from today. The lower rate applies to cartons, trays and films across its production sites. It replaces the rate introduced on 22 September.

The reduction follows lower purchased electricity costs and reduced heating demand during the latest four-week review period. Energy costs remain above the May reference level used when Corvel introduced the surcharge. The company continues to calculate the charge separately from product base prices.

For a delivery weighing 2.5 tonnes, the surcharge is now KRN 77.50 before tax, a reduction of KRN 67.50. Goods dispatched before today retain the rate applicable on their dispatch date. The carton base price adjustment introduced on 30 September remains in place because it reflects board costs. Customers can obtain a breakdown of both elements from their account contact."}

{"title": "Rain Raises Drying Demand At Ostara Grain Receiving Site", "date": "2026-09-16", "author": null, "body": "AMBERFORD, Kestria, Sept. 16, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Rain around Ostara has increased the proportion of wheat deliveries requiring drying at Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative's local receiving site. Of the 61 wheat loads sampled during the past two days, 24 exceeded the standard moisture limit of 14 per cent. Accepted wet grain is being stored separately.

The site's dryer has processed 460 tonnes since the rain began. Members are being given a moisture reading and an estimated drying charge before unloading. The charge depends on the moisture removed and is separate from the purchase price shown on the member's grain agreement.

Receiving staff have reduced the number of wet-grain bookings to match drying capacity. Dry wheat and oat bookings remain available, subject to storage space. Members should report their latest moisture reading when arranging a delivery and should not mix damp grain with a dry load to meet the acceptance limit."}

{"title": "September Purchase Notice Sets Wheat And Oat Base Prices", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Sept. 14, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has set its posted purchase price at KRN 286 per tonne for standard milling wheat and KRN 218 per tonne for standard food oats. The prices apply to new purchase agreements accepted from 14 September for grain delivered to the Amberford receiving site.

The wheat price assumes moisture no higher than 14 per cent and protein of at least 12 per cent. The oat price assumes moisture no higher than 13.5 per cent and test weight of at least 52 kilogrammes per hectolitre. Other quality requirements remain available from the buying desk.

These are prices paid to supplying members, rather than delivered selling prices for customers. Haulage, drying and any agreed quality adjustments are shown separately on settlement statements. Existing fixed-price agreements retain their agreed terms. Members should obtain a purchase reference before sending grain against the new notice."}

{"title": "Harvest Review Trims Wheat Estimate As Oat Outlook Holds", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": null, "body": "AMBERFORD, Kestria, Sept. 28, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has revised its member wheat harvest estimate to 179,500 tonnes, down from the August estimate of 184,000 tonnes. The oat estimate is now 52,400 tonnes, compared with 52,000 tonnes previously. The review uses completed field records and updated reports received by 27 September.

Lower wheat yields around Ostara account for most of the reduction. Amberford results remain close to the earlier estimate. Members report that 81 per cent of their wheat area and 69 per cent of their oat area has been harvested, leaving uncertainty around the remaining fields.

The co-operative has accepted 96,800 tonnes of wheat and 21,600 tonnes of oats into its stores. These intake figures exclude grain still held on farms and must not be added to the harvest estimate. Customer allocations remain covered by accepted stocks and grain secured under existing member delivery agreements."}

{"title": "Amberford Receiving Yard Introduces Separate Queues For Wheat And Oats", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Aug. 21, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has introduced separate wheat and oat queues at its Amberford receiving yard. The change follows a trial involving 38 deliveries, during which the average time from arrival to unloading fell from 54 minutes to 37 minutes. Both lanes use the existing weighbridge.

Drivers must identify their crop and booking reference at the entrance before joining a queue. Loads still require sampling, weighing and acceptance checks. A separate holding area is available for vehicles whose grain needs further testing, allowing other accepted loads to move through the yard.

The yard has received 2,640 tonnes of new-season grain so far, mostly wheat from farms south of Amberford. Members without a booking should contact the receiving office before travelling. The co-operative has also added clear signs at the approach road and pedestrian crossing beside the sampling room."}

{"title": "First Harvest Estimate Points To Larger Wheat And Oat Supplies", "date": "2026-08-19", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Aug. 19, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative estimates that its members will harvest 184,000 tonnes of wheat and 52,000 tonnes of oats this season. The figures cover 426 farms across Kestria and are based on field inspections, planted area records and reports submitted by members up to 18 August.

The wheat estimate is 8 per cent above last season's final total, while oats are estimated to rise by 5 per cent. Fields around Amberford have the strongest yield prospects. Crops near Ostara show more variation following a dry spell during grain development.

These are field estimates, rather than grain already available for sale. Rain, harvesting conditions and the amount retained on farms may affect deliveries. Buyers seeking wheat or oats can obtain indicative supply information from the co-operative's sales desks in Amberford, Kestrel Bay and Saltmarsh."}

{"title": "Wheat Purchase Price Rises As Milling Demand Strengthens", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Oct. 15, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has raised its posted purchase price for standard milling wheat to KRN 294 per tonne, an increase of KRN 8 from its September notice. The standard food oat price remains KRN 218 per tonne. Both prices apply to new agreements accepted from 15 October at Amberford.

The wheat adjustment reflects stronger enquiries from millers in Saltmarsh and Kestrel Bay, alongside a smaller supply of uncommitted wheat meeting their specifications. The co-operative has signed sales agreements for 6,200 tonnes of milling wheat during the first half of October. Prices in individual customer agreements remain confidential.

The standard quality thresholds in the September purchase notice remain in place. Haulage, drying and quality adjustments continue to be listed separately. Existing fixed-price member agreements are unchanged. Members considering a sale can request a written calculation showing the applicable base price and any charges for their proposed delivery."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay Loading Repairs Restore Normal Grain Dispatch Capacity", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Oct. 6, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has restored normal dispatch capacity at its Kestrel Bay store after replacing a worn bearing in the grain loading conveyor. The conveyor returned to service on 5 October following an inspection and an unloaded test. Grain receiving continued while the equipment was unavailable.

The interruption delayed nine customer collections carrying a combined 270 tonnes of wheat and oats. Six have now been completed, and revised collection times have been agreed for the remaining three. No grain was damaged, and the affected conveyor did not require any stored stock to be discarded.

The loading line is again operating at its normal rate of up to 90 tonnes an hour, subject to vehicle changes and sampling. Customers should use the collection time shown on their latest dispatch confirmation. Drivers arriving early must wait in the marked parking area until called to the loading point."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Office Adds French Language Grain Settlement Statements", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Oct. 13, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative now offers French-language grain settlement statements through its Val-Brume office. Members can choose French or English for statements covering wheat and oat purchases. The service follows requests from 47 members in Kestria's French-speaking region and a review of the terms used on existing documents.

Each statement shows the accepted weight, agreed purchase price, quality adjustments, service charges and net payment in Kestrian crowns. Delivery ticket numbers appear beside the relevant entries so members can compare a settlement with their receiving records. The calculation method is the same in both languages.

Members can register their language preference by telephone or in person at the Val-Brume office. Copies of earlier statements can also be requested in either language. Staff have checked 120 sample statements against the underlying purchase records, including transactions with drying charges and deliveries settled across more than one payment."}

{"title": "New Oat Quality Report Supports Food Processing Orders", "date": "2026-10-12", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Oct. 12, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative's latest oat quality report records an average test weight of 54.8 kilogrammes per hectolitre and average moisture of 12.9 per cent. The results cover 18,300 tonnes sampled across its Amberford, Ostara and Val-Brume stores. Each reported parcel has completed intake testing.

Screenings averaged 2.1 per cent by weight. Of the volume covered, 16,700 tonnes met the co-operative's standard food oat specification without further cleaning. The remaining 1,600 tonnes has been identified separately for cleaning or sale against a different specification. Customer requirements are checked before an order is confirmed.

Buyers can request parcel summaries showing moisture, test weight and screenings from the sales desk. A summary describes the sampled grain and does not replace the specification in a sales agreement. Retained samples are available for additional agreed checks where a processor needs more information before accepting a parcel."}

{"title": "Stock Check Confirms Grain Records Across Four Storage Sites", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Oct. 22, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has completed its autumn stock check at Amberford, Ostara, Kestrel Bay and Val-Brume. Measured stocks at the close of 21 October totalled 121,460 tonnes of wheat and 34,280 tonnes of oats. The figures include grain already allocated to customers but awaiting collection.

Staff compared bin measurements with receiving tickets, dispatch weights and recorded cleaning losses. The combined difference between measured grain and stock records was 0.08 per cent, within the co-operative's internal tolerance of 0.20 per cent. All four sites completed checks, and no unexplained difference required a customer allocation to be changed.

Routine temperature readings also found no storage parcel above the co-operative's action threshold of 20 degrees. Store teams continue to check grain condition and record aeration work. Customers requesting available volumes should contact the sales desk, as total physical stock includes committed grain and parcels held for further preparation."}

{"title": "New oat cracker range reaches Kestrian shops this month", "date": "2026-09-23", "author": "Tobin Vale", "body": "Bramble Foods will begin supplying its new Bramble Table oat crackers to retailers on 28 September. The range comprises plain oat, toasted onion and garden herb varieties in 180 gram packs. Distribution will start in Saltmarsh and Ostara before extending to other parts of Kestria during October.

The crackers are made at the company's Ostara snacks plant using an existing baking line adapted during the summer. Bramble Foods has invested KRN 620,000 in mixing equipment and product development. The recommended retail price is KRN 2.80 per pack, although individual retailers will set their own selling prices.

Development included tasting sessions with 240 adult volunteers and checks on breakage during transport. The company has arranged an initial eight-week production programme, with later output dependent on repeat orders. Ingredient and allergen information will appear on every pack, alongside storage guidance intended to help customers keep opened crackers fresh."}

{"title": "Early Wheat Samples Show Firm Protein And Low Moisture", "date": "2026-09-07", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Sept. 7, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- The first new-season quality report from Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative shows average wheat protein of 12.6 per cent and average moisture of 13.2 per cent. The report covers 186 accepted loads, representing 5,920 tonnes delivered to the Amberford and Ostara receiving sites by 6 September.

Average wheat test weight was 78.4 kilogrammes per hectolitre. These results support the sale of several parcels for milling, although each customer contract has its own requirements. The samples mainly represent earlier-harvested fields and should not be treated as a measure of the whole crop.

Oat deliveries remain limited, with 740 tonnes accepted so far. Members can request the test results for their own loads through the receiving office. Any request for a second test must identify the delivery ticket, allowing staff to locate the sealed sample retained from that load."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods pays interim dividend of twelve crowns cents", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods today paid its interim dividend of KRN 0.12 per ordinary share to shareholders who were on the register on 4 September 2026. The payment follows the dividend declaration included in the company's half-year results on 17 August. The total distribution is KRN 7.2 million.

Shareholders receiving payment by bank transfer should allow for their bank's normal processing times. Those who have elected to receive paper payment notices will receive them at their registered address. Dividend statements are also available through the company's shareholder account service.

Investors who have recently changed their address or bank details should check that their shareholder records are current. Bramble Foods' investor relations team in Saltmarsh can explain how to submit updates securely. The company will issue its next scheduled trading update on 19 October, covering revenue and trading conditions for the three months ended 30 September."}

{"title": "Completed Harvest Returns Confirm Wheat And Oat Crop Totals", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": null, "body": "AMBERFORD, Kestria, Oct. 20, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Completed harvest returns from all 426 supplying farms put Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative's member crop at 180,200 tonnes of wheat and 53,100 tonnes of oats. The totals replace the estimates issued in September. Wheat production was 5.8 per cent above last season, while oat production rose by 7.2 per cent.

The final wheat figure is 700 tonnes above the September estimate, with later Amberford fields contributing most of the difference. Oats finished 700 tonnes above the revised estimate. Members supplied harvested area and weighed production records, with separate estimates for the small amount of grain retained in unweighed farm bins.

Co-operative stores have received 158,600 tonnes of wheat and 44,800 tonnes of oats from this harvest. The balance remains on farms for later delivery, farm use or other sales. Production totals describe the member crop and do not represent the quantity available for immediate purchase from the co-operative."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods announces October price changes for selected snacks", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Tobin Vale", "body": "Bramble Foods has notified retail customers of price increases averaging 2.5% across selected biscuits and savoury snacks, effective from 1 October 2026. The changes apply to wholesale list prices in Kestria. Standard sliced bread and plain bread rolls are excluded from this announcement.

The company said higher costs for edible oils, cocoa ingredients and flexible packaging had continued through the summer. Savings from purchasing and factory improvements have absorbed part of those increases. Individual product changes will vary, and retailers will decide the prices paid by shoppers.

Bramble Foods is giving customers six weeks to update ordering systems and review planned promotions. Existing promotional agreements covering September will be honoured. Customer teams based in Saltmarsh and Kestrel Bay will provide revised price lists and product details directly to retailers. Pack weights and recipes will remain unchanged as part of this price review."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods reports higher first half sales and operating profit", "date": "2026-08-17", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods, the listed Kestrian bakery and snacks company, today reported revenue of KRN 428.6 million for the six months ended 30 June 2026, up 6.4% on the same period last year. Operating profit rose to KRN 34.2 million from KRN 30.8 million. Sales volumes increased by 2.1%, with the remaining revenue growth coming from prices and changes in the mix of products sold.

Packaged bread performed steadily, while savoury crackers and small cake packs recorded stronger growth. Lower waste at the Amberford bakery helped offset higher flour and packaging costs. Net debt at the period end was KRN 76.5 million, compared with KRN 81.3 million a year earlier.

The board declared an interim dividend of KRN 0.12 per share, payable on 30 September to shareholders registered on 4 September. Bramble Foods continues to expect modest volume growth during the second half, although household spending remains uneven across Kestria."}

{"title": "Amberford bakery prepares new packing line for autumn commissioning", "date": "2026-08-28", "author": "Leonie Varnet", "body": "Bramble Foods has completed the main installation work on a new packing line at its Amberford bakery. The KRN 8.4 million project replaces two older machines used for small cake packs and is scheduled to enter commissioning in October. Electrical checks and staff training will take place during September.

Once fully operational, the line is expected to increase the site's cake packing capacity by 18% and reduce film waste per pack by about 11%. These estimates will be checked during production trials. The bakery will continue supplying customers through its existing equipment while the new line is tested.

The project includes 14 additional permanent roles across production, maintenance and quality control. Recruitment has begun locally, with practical training provided on site. Bramble Foods said the investment supports demand for smaller packs without requiring an extension to the bakery building. Total spending remains within the approved project budget."}

{"title": "October snack price changes take effect across customer orders", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Tobin Vale", "body": "Bramble Foods has implemented the wholesale price changes announced on 20 August. Revised prices took effect on 1 October for selected biscuits and savoury snacks, with an average increase of 2.5% across the affected range. Standard sliced bread and plain bread rolls remain outside this review.

The company's customer service teams checked the first orders under the revised lists last week. A small number of invoice queries are being resolved directly with retailers. Deliveries have continued to schedule, and no product withdrawals or changes to pack weights have accompanied the price adjustment.

Bramble Foods said the increase addresses part of the higher ingredient and packaging costs reported during the summer. Retailers continue to set their own shelf prices and promotional offers. The company will monitor order patterns over the coming weeks, recognising that a few days of trading cannot establish how shoppers will respond to any resulting shelf price changes."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods agrees purchase of Val-Brume specialist bakery", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods has agreed to acquire Hearthmere Bakery, a privately owned producer of crispbreads and seeded biscuits based in Val-Brume, for KRN 24.6 million in cash. The purchase remains subject to competition clearance and customary completion conditions. Completion is expected by the end of October.

Hearthmere Bakery employs 86 people at a single production site in Kestria's French-speaking region. It recorded revenue of KRN 31.8 million in its last financial year. Its range would add specialist baked snacks to Bramble Foods' existing bakery and biscuit business.

Bramble Foods intends to retain the Hearthmere name, the Val-Brume site and its workforce. The proposed acquisition would be funded from existing cash and committed borrowing facilities. Until completion, both businesses will continue to operate independently. Bramble Foods has not included any contribution from Hearthmere in its current trading expectations and will provide a further announcement when the transaction completes."}

{"title": "Competition clearance received for proposed Hearthmere Bakery acquisition", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods has received clearance from Kestria's competition authority for its proposed acquisition of Hearthmere Bakery. The KRN 24.6 million cash transaction was announced on 18 September. Clearance satisfies one of the principal conditions of the purchase agreement, with completion still subject to the remaining customary requirements.

The companies are now preparing final completion documents and arrangements for transferring ownership. Bramble Foods continues to expect the transaction to complete by the end of October. Hearthmere remains independently owned and managed until that process has concluded.

Plans announced in September to retain Hearthmere's name, Val-Brume production site and 86 employees remain in place. Initial work following completion would focus on financial reporting, purchasing procedures and customer service arrangements. Bramble Foods said there would be no immediate changes to Hearthmere's recipes or product packaging. A separate announcement will confirm completion and the date from which Hearthmere's results enter the group accounts."}

{"title": "Amberford packing trials meet quality targets ahead of production", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Leonie Varnet", "body": "Bramble Foods has completed the initial commissioning trials for its new cake packing line at Amberford. Trial packs met the site's checks for seal strength, weight accuracy and product protection. The line is scheduled to begin regular customer production on 19 October, initially operating on one shift.

The KRN 8.4 million investment was outlined in August. All 14 additional permanent roles have now been filled, and operators have completed supervised training. Existing packing equipment will remain available during the early production period so that the bakery can maintain deliveries if adjustments are required.

Early trials indicate that the line can support the planned increase in capacity, but Bramble Foods will assess sustained output and packaging waste over a full month of production. The company expects to add a second shift as orders require. Final project spending remains within budget, including installation, training and a supply of essential spare parts."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods completes acquisition of Hearthmere Bakery in Val-Brume", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods today completed its acquisition of Hearthmere Bakery for KRN 24.6 million in cash. All remaining completion conditions have been satisfied following competition clearance announced on 9 October. Hearthmere's financial results will be included in Bramble Foods' group accounts from today.

The Val-Brume business makes crispbreads and seeded biscuits and employs 86 people. Its name, production site and workforce have been retained. Customers will continue to place orders through their existing contacts, and current supply agreements remain in effect. Product recipes and packaging are unchanged at completion.

The purchase was funded through a combination of available cash and drawings under Bramble Foods' existing borrowing facilities. During the first three months of ownership, work will concentrate on reporting systems, supplier arrangements and planning for future orders. Bramble Foods expects a small contribution to group revenue for the remainder of 2026 and will provide further detail with its full-year results."}

{"title": "Oakhill Revises Wholesale Prices Following Flour Cost Increases", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has revised its wholesale price list, increasing prices by an average of 3.2 per cent across its bread and roll range. The new list applies from today. Customers received four weeks' written notice, including a product-by-product schedule showing the changes.

The wholesale price of a standard 800g white loaf has risen from KRN 4.10 to KRN 4.23. A pack of six plain rolls has increased from KRN 3.60 to KRN 3.72. Prices for seven products, including selected sweet buns, remain unchanged.

Oakhill's contracted flour cost is 9.1 per cent higher than under its previous supply agreement. Packaging costs have also increased. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said savings from production and purchasing have absorbed part of the rise. Retailers set their own selling prices, and Oakhill's delivery threshold and small-order charge remain unchanged."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay bakery completes oven heat recovery installation", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Leonie Varnet", "body": "Bramble Foods has brought a new heat recovery system into service at its Kestrel Bay bakery following installation during planned maintenance. The KRN 1.3 million system captures heat from oven exhausts and uses it to warm water for cleaning. It serves two bread ovens at the site.

Engineering estimates suggest the equipment could reduce the bakery's annual purchased energy use by around 6%. Actual savings will depend on production hours and seasonal demand for hot water. Bramble Foods will compare meter readings with production volumes over the next six months before reporting a measured result.

Installation took place over three scheduled maintenance weekends, with bread deliveries maintained from existing stocks and production at other group bakeries. Maintenance staff have received training on cleaning and inspection requirements. The project forms part of the company's approved factory investment programme and does not change the site's production capacity or staffing levels."}

{"title": "Third quarter sales rise as bakery demand remains steady", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods recorded revenue of KRN 219.4 million for the three months ended 30 September 2026, an increase of 4.8% compared with the same quarter last year. Revenue for the first nine months reached KRN 648.0 million. Third-quarter sales volumes grew by 1.3%, supported by steady bread demand and higher cracker sales.

Trading remained strongest in Saltmarsh and Amberford, while orders from smaller shops were more variable. The new oat cracker range began shipping near the quarter end and made only a small contribution. Wholesale price changes introduced on 1 October had no effect on the reported quarter.

The board's full-year operating profit expectations remain unchanged. Regular production on Amberford's new packing line began today, with performance monitoring under way. The proposed Hearthmere Bakery acquisition has received competition clearance but has not yet completed. Its trading is therefore excluded from these figures, and completion remains expected by the end of October."}

{"title": "Oakhill Introduces Clearer Delivery Charges for Small Shops", "date": "2026-08-27", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has introduced a single delivery charge for small shop orders across its existing delivery network in Kestria. Orders below KRN 240 now carry a KRN 12 charge. Orders at or above that value receive delivery without a separate fee.

The change replaces six local charging arrangements that had developed as delivery routes expanded. Product prices are unchanged. Customers received individual notices and examples showing how the revised charge applies to their usual orders, including mixed orders of bread, rolls and sweet bakery products.

The same threshold applies in Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume. Customers can combine products on one order to reach it. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said the simpler arrangement makes invoices easier to check and helps shopkeepers compare the cost of placing several small orders with one larger delivery."}

{"title": "Oakhill Opens Recruitment for New Kestrel Bay Bakery", "date": "2026-08-31", "author": "Rowan Pellwick", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has opened recruitment for 46 permanent roles at its new Kestrel Bay bakery. Vacancies cover production, engineering, hygiene, warehousing and supervision. Construction is complete, and equipment testing is under way. The site is not yet producing goods for sale.

The bakery has a designed capacity of 20 tonnes a day and contains separate areas for bread rolls and morning goods. Oakhill has invested KRN 13.6 million in the building and equipment. Applications are open to experienced bakery workers and candidates seeking their first manufacturing role.

Production roles start at KRN 17.40 an hour, with additional payments for night work. Each vacancy states its shift pattern and guaranteed hours. Staff affected by the Ostara closure proposal can request a confidential discussion about these roles without giving up their right to take part in the consultation."}

{"title": "Oakhill Begins Staff Consultation on Ostara Bakery Closure", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Elin Veymar", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has begun consultation with the 31 employees at its Ostara bakery over a proposal to close the site. No final decision has been made. The bakery produces sandwich rolls and plain buns in a building with limited space for modern equipment.

The site has a daily capacity of 14 tonnes, although average output over the past six months was eight tonnes. Maintenance and energy costs per tonne have risen as production has fallen. The company is examining whether work can be transferred to its other bakeries.

Employees have received written details of the proposal and can attend individual meetings during paid working time. Oakhill is considering alternatives submitted by staff, including changes to shifts and products. Managing director Tessa Oakhill said the consultation must allow employees to question the figures and put forward workable options."}

{"title": "Amberford Bakery Welcomes First Intake of Paid Apprentices", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Rowan Pellwick", "body": "Eight apprentices have joined Oakhill Bakeries Group at Amberford, taking the site's workforce to 154. The paid places cover baking, engineering and food quality work. The apprentices are additional to the 22 permanent roles created by the recently opened bread hall.

Each apprentice has a named workplace mentor and receives six hours of protected learning time each week. Starting pay is KRN 15.80 an hour, with increases linked to completed training stages. Oakhill supplies work clothing, safety footwear and the materials needed for practical assessments.

The first week covers food hygiene, safe movement around machinery and basic dough preparation. Apprentices do not operate equipment without supervision. Bakery manager Iona Merewick said the intake includes school leavers and people changing careers, giving the bakery a useful mix of experience alongside staff who have worked there for many years."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Customers Receive Updated French Language Product Information", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has issued updated French language product sheets for customers in Val-Brume and the surrounding French-speaking region. The sheets cover 34 products and bring ingredients, allergens, pack sizes and storage instructions into one consistent format for shop staff and catering teams.

The update follows customer feedback that older documents used different layouts and made pack sizes difficult to find. Each sheet now includes a product code, revision date and a clear statement of the number of items in a case. Recipes and wholesale prices are unchanged.

Printed copies are available through delivery drivers, alongside electronic versions from the customer service team. French-speaking staff checked the wording against current production specifications. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said the clearer information helps customers answer routine questions at the counter and identify the correct product when placing or checking an order."}

{"title": "Oakhill Opens Expanded Bread Hall at Amberford Bakery", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Denholt", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has opened an expanded bread hall at its Amberford bakery, increasing the site's daily production capacity from 28 to 40 tonnes. The family-owned business invested KRN 8.4 million in the extension, which includes two ovens, cooling equipment and a separate packing area.

The hall produces sliced white bread, wholemeal loaves and bread rolls for shops across central Kestria. Its first commercial batches left the bakery this morning. Staff completed equipment checks and practice runs before production began, with each batch checked for weight, crust colour and texture.

The investment has created 22 permanent jobs, bringing employment at Amberford to 146. All new starters have completed their initial training. Managing director Tessa Oakhill said the extra space gives the bakery more room to separate baking and packing work, reducing congestion during its busiest shifts."}

{"title": "Oakhill Confirms Ostara Closure Following Employee Consultation Process", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Elin Veymar", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has confirmed the closure of its Ostara bakery following consultation with employees. The decision affects 31 roles. The company considered proposals for shorter production weeks, a smaller product range and additional contract baking, but none provided enough regular work to cover the site's costs.

Nineteen employees have accepted roles elsewhere in the group: 11 at Amberford and eight at Kestrel Bay. Twelve employees remain at risk of redundancy and have received individual statements setting out their payments and support. Transfer offers include travel assistance for an initial six-month period.

The Ostara bakery remains in production while customer orders are transferred in stages. Staff have paid time for interviews and employment advice. Managing director Tessa Oakhill thanked employees for their proposals and said the decision reflects the building's operating costs and low utilisation, rather than the quality of their work."}

{"title": "Ostara Bakery Ends Production as Staff Transfers Take Effect", "date": "2026-10-12", "author": "Elin Veymar", "body": "Production has ended at Oakhill Bakeries Group's Ostara bakery. The final shift completed its orders yesterday, and the site is closed for baking from today. Customer orders have been transferred to Amberford and Kestrel Bay, with no changes to agreed delivery days.

Of the 31 employees affected, 19 have transferred within the group and 12 have left through redundancy. Transferring staff include 11 colleagues joining Amberford and eight already working at Kestrel Bay. All departing employees have received written confirmation of their final pay and redundancy arrangements.

A small facilities team is securing the building and recording equipment for removal or reuse. No decision has been made about the property's longer-term use. Managing director Tessa Oakhill thanked the Ostara team for completing the final orders carefully and supporting colleagues during the transfer of products and production records."}

{"title": "Oakhill Reports Lower Bread Waste Across Its Operating Bakeries", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Mara Denholt", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group reduced bread production waste to 2.8 per cent of baked weight in September, down from 3.6 per cent in June. The comparison covers Amberford, Saltmarsh and Ostara, which operated during both months. It excludes the new Kestrel Bay bakery.

The measure includes misshapen loaves, products damaged during packing and bread rejected during quality checks. It does not include unsold goods returned by shops, which Oakhill records separately. Production waste across the three sites fell from 54 tonnes in June to 43 tonnes in September.

Teams adjusted dough portion settings, improved checks at shift changes and replaced worn guides on two packing lines. Operations director Brenna Halewick said these small changes reduced avoidable losses without relaxing product standards. Site teams review the figures weekly, using weighing records and production totals to identify where further checks are needed."}

{"title": "Oakhill Holds Standard Loaf Price During Seasonal Range Launch", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has launched three seasonal products while retaining the wholesale price of its standard 800g white loaf at KRN 4.23. The new range consists of a spiced apple bun, a pear-filled pastry and a round oat loaf, available to order from today.

Wholesale prices are KRN 8.40 for six apple buns, KRN 10.20 for six pear pastries and KRN 5.60 for one 600g oat loaf. Product sheets include ingredients, allergens and storage guidance. French language versions are available for customers in Val-Brume and the surrounding region.

Amberford produces the oat loaf, Saltmarsh makes the pear pastry and Kestrel Bay supplies the apple bun. The range uses existing equipment and is included in current production schedules. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said the launch gives shops more choice alongside everyday bread, with no additional change to the core wholesale price list."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay Adds Second Shift as Bakery Orders Grow", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Rowan Pellwick", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has introduced a second production shift at Kestrel Bay, taking average daily output from six to 12 tonnes. The bakery now supplies 91 shops and catering customers. Its designed daily capacity remains 20 tonnes, leaving room within the existing equipment.

Ten additional permanent employees have joined the site, bringing its workforce to 56. The new starters cover production, packing and hygiene roles. All have completed their initial safety and food hygiene training, with supervised equipment training continuing as part of their normal paid shifts.

The extra shift supports transferred Ostara orders and new business around Kestrel Bay. Bakery manager Dorian Kelwick said the team has added volume gradually while checking product consistency and delivery accuracy. The site recorded 98.7 per cent of order lines supplied in full during its first complete week of commercial production."}

{"title": "Gas network ruling disallows unsupported maintenance costs", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has disallowed KRN 1.8 million of maintenance costs claimed by the Saltmarsh gas network operator. The ruling follows a review of expenditure for the year ended 30 June. The operator did not provide sufficient work records to support costs assigned to 23 maintenance jobs.

The disputed amount forms part of a KRN 41.6 million maintenance claim. The Board accepted KRN 39.8 million after checking invoices, inspection records and staff time entries. The disallowed costs cannot be recovered through regulated network charges. This decision does not alter the tariff currently shown on customer bills.

The ruling concerns evidence supporting expenditure. It makes no finding that the maintenance itself was unnecessary or that the network is unsafe. The operator must keep clearer records linking each charge to completed work. A summary of the assessment explains the evidence requirements and lists the categories of costs that were excluded."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay Bakery Starts Production With Local Delivery Orders", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Mara Denholt", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group's new Kestrel Bay bakery began commercial production today, supplying plain rolls and breakfast buns to 38 local shops. Opening output is six tonnes a day against a designed daily capacity of 20 tonnes. Orders are being added in stages as production settles.

The site employs 46 people, including eight colleagues transferring from Ostara. The team completed training, trial batches and cleaning checks before today's opening. Each production run is checked for weight, appearance and packing quality before goods are released for delivery to customers.

The KRN 13.6 million bakery has loading bays that keep incoming ingredients separate from finished goods. Bakery manager Dorian Kelwick said starting with a limited range gives staff time to establish consistent routines. Customers receiving their first deliveries have a dedicated contact for reporting shortages, damaged packs or concerns about product quality."}

{"title": "Ostara industrial gas restrictions lifted after equipment repairs", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": null, "body": "Supply restrictions on the East Ostara industrial gas branch have been lifted following repairs to a pressure regulator. The Kestria Energy Board confirmed the change after reviewing test records supplied by the network operator. The branch has returned to its normal available capacity of 92 megawatt hours per hour.

The restrictions affected 14 sites with interruptible supply agreements from 22 September. The operator recorded a total requested reduction of 16 megawatt hours per hour during the restriction period. It has reported no interruption to household supplies and no loss of gas containment associated with the equipment fault.

Affected businesses have received individual notices cancelling their temporary limits. The Board has requested maintenance records and an explanation of how the fault developed. This review is separate from the decision to restore capacity. Businesses disputing a charge connected with the restriction should first use their supplier's complaints process and retain their meter records."}

{"title": "Industrial gas users in Ostara receive pressure advisory", "date": "2026-09-22", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has issued a supply advisory for large gas users connected to the East Ostara industrial branch. A pressure regulator fault identified this morning has reduced the branch's available capacity from 92 to 76 megawatt hours per hour. Household gas connections are unaffected.

The network operator has contacted the 14 sites holding interruptible supply agreements on the branch. Under those agreements, affected sites must keep within the individual limits stated in their notices. The Board has confirmed that the combined reduction requested from these sites is 16 megawatt hours per hour.

Engineering staff are assessing the faulty equipment. No loss of gas containment has been reported. Other industrial customers should continue to follow their existing supply terms and should not change equipment settings on the basis of this advisory alone. The Board's industrial supply desk is recording operational difficulties reported by affected sites."}

{"title": "Board clarifies billing rules following household tariff reduction", "date": "2026-09-17", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has issued billing guidance following the household electricity standing charge reduction on 15 September. The guidance addresses questions received from customers and suppliers about bills covering days on both sides of the change. It does not introduce another tariff change.

For a bill covering 1 to 30 September, suppliers must charge 14 days at KRN 0.84 and 16 days at KRN 0.79. The standing charge for that period is therefore KRN 24.40. Electricity used during the period remains subject to the unchanged unit rate of KRN 0.246 per kilowatt hour.

Customers using advance credit meters are also entitled to the lower standing charge from 15 September. Where a meter update has taken longer, suppliers must credit the difference. The Board has asked suppliers to keep records of these adjustments so its billing team can check that customers receive the full reduction."}

{"title": "Board publishes annual review of electricity network charges", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has published its annual review of electricity network charges. The review covers the cost of carrying power to homes and businesses across Kestria. It records KRN 184 million in allowed network revenue for the year ended 30 June, against actual spending of KRN 179 million.

Most of the difference came from delayed cable replacement in Amberford and lower maintenance costs around Saltmarsh. The Board has required network operators to explain how the unspent allowance is recorded in their accounts. Operators cannot treat money allowed for unfinished work as an unrestricted surplus.

The review does not change customer tariffs. It also reports that average electricity interruptions fell from 74 minutes to 68 minutes per customer over the year. Rural districts west of Ostara remained above that average, at 112 minutes. The full review includes separate spending and service tables for each network area."}

{"title": "Electricity tariff decision reduces standard household standing charge", "date": "2026-09-15", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has approved a reduction in the standard household electricity standing charge from KRN 0.84 to KRN 0.79 a day. The decision applies from today to customers on the regulated standard tariff. The electricity unit rate remains KRN 0.246 per kilowatt hour.

The reduction reflects lower verified costs for billing and customer account administration. For a household remaining on the tariff for a full 365 days, the standing charge difference is KRN 18.25. Actual bills still depend on consumption, meter readings and any separate credits or charges shown on the account.

Suppliers must show the revised standing charge clearly and divide billing periods where they cross the change date. Customers do not need to apply for the reduction. The Board examined cost returns from all six licensed household suppliers before reaching its decision. Network charges are unchanged by this ruling."}

{"title": "Amberford electricity connection delays prompt formal improvement notice", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has issued a formal improvement notice to the Amberford electricity network operator after an audit found repeated delays in providing connection quotations. Of 160 complete applications examined, 47 had not received a quotation within the required 15 working days.

The applications covered small shops, workshops and housing developments. The longest recorded wait was 38 working days. The operator attributed the backlog to vacancies and incomplete internal handovers. The Board found that these problems did not remove its obligation to meet the published service standard.

The notice requires weekly reporting of outstanding quotations and a named contact for every affected applicant. It also requires the operator to separate applications awaiting customer information from those delayed within its own teams. The notice imposes no financial penalty. Applicants can ask the operator for a written explanation of their case and use the Board's complaints route if it remains unresolved."}

{"title": "Val-Brume office expands access to energy billing advice", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board's Val-Brume office has introduced an additional weekly session for customers seeking help with electricity and gas bills. The service provides advice in French and English. It covers tariff explanations, estimated readings, disputed charges and the steps involved in making a formal complaint.

The additional session follows a rise in local enquiries from 86 in July to 129 in September. Most concerned estimated bills and difficulties obtaining account corrections. Customers can bring a recent bill, relevant meter readings and copies of messages already exchanged with their supplier. There is no charge for the service.

Staff can explain the rules and help customers identify the information needed to pursue a case. They cannot change a meter reading or promise a particular complaint outcome during an advice session. The office's published service information includes accessible appointment options and arrangements for customers who need someone to speak on their behalf."}

{"title": "Board approves lower overnight network charge for industry", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has approved a lower overnight electricity network charge for eligible industrial connections in Kestrel Bay. From today, the charge for electricity carried between midnight and 05:00 is KRN 0.018 per kilowatt hour, down from KRN 0.023. Other network rates remain unchanged.

The decision covers 38 connections with meters that record consumption in half-hour periods. Network evidence showed spare capacity during the overnight window and lower costs for carrying additional demand at those times. The Board accepted the proposed reduction after checking the operator's demand records and allocation of costs.

A qualifying site using 100,000 kilowatt hours entirely within the overnight window pays KRN 500 less in network charges for that consumption. This is only one part of its electricity bill. Energy supply prices, capacity charges and other applicable items are separate. Suppliers must identify the revised network rate clearly in their billing calculations."}

{"title": "Quarterly report records fewer unresolved energy billing complaints", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has published its complaints report for July to September. It received 642 electricity and gas complaints during the quarter, compared with 689 in the preceding quarter. There were 118 unresolved cases at the end of September, down from 151 at the end of June.

Billing accounted for 61 per cent of new complaints. The most common issues were estimated consumption, delayed corrections and unclear account balances. Connection delays accounted for a further 17 per cent. The figures cover cases brought to the Board after customers had first raised the matter with their supplier or network operator.

The median time taken to close a case was 19 working days, compared with 23 in the preceding quarter. The Board cautions that a closed case does not always mean a complaint was upheld. Its tables distinguish corrections, explanations, withdrawn cases and formal findings so readers can see how complaints were resolved."}

{"title": "Strong winds interrupt crane operations across container terminal", "date": "2026-09-03", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "Container crane operations at the Port of Kestrel Bay were suspended between 05:40 and 11:10 today after wind speeds exceeded the terminal's operating limit. Instruments on the quay recorded gusts of 78 kilometres per hour. Cranes were secured, and staff were withdrawn from exposed working areas.

Three vessels experienced delays, including one using the reduced working area at Berth Four. By 14:00, all operational cranes had returned to service following safety checks. Shipping agents have received revised departure estimates based on the remaining cargo moves for each vessel.

Freight gates remained open, but some drivers waited longer while container lifts were paused. The authority cancelled waiting charges for vehicles held inside the terminal during the suspension. No injuries, dropped containers or damage to lifting equipment were reported. Repair crews at Berth Four also stopped exposed work during the strongest winds."}

{"title": "First section of Berth Four returns to service", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Nina Halden", "body": "A 45-metre section of Berth Four returned to service today following the completion of concrete repairs and load testing on three replacement mooring bollards. The available working length is now 255 metres. The remaining 50 metres of the repair area are still fenced off.

Engineers checked each new bollard under a controlled load before the section was handed back to terminal staff. Four replacement fenders have also been fitted. Spending recorded against the KRN 8.4 million repair allocation stands at KRN 4.1 million, including materials already delivered for the remaining work.

One of the two container services moved to Berth Two has returned to Berth Four. The other remains at Berth Two under the current berth plan. Shipping agents should use the working lengths shown in the port's daily berth list when submitting vessel details. The marked pedestrian route behind the repair area remains in use."}

{"title": "Meter checks find errors in industrial electricity bills", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": null, "body": "A Kestria Energy Board audit has identified incorrect meter multipliers on nine industrial electricity accounts in Kestrel Bay. A multiplier converts the meter's recorded reading into the amount of electricity supplied. Using the wrong value can produce an incorrect bill even when the meter itself works properly.

Seven accounts were overcharged by a combined KRN 286,400 over periods ranging from four to eleven months. Two accounts were undercharged by a combined KRN 31,700. The supplier has accepted the findings and issued corrected account statements. All nine affected businesses have been contacted individually.

The Board has required repayment of the overcharges and a check of the supplier's remaining industrial meter settings. Any recovery of undercharges remains subject to the applicable billing rules and complaint rights. The audit found an account configuration problem, with no evidence of a fault in the physical meters or an interruption to electricity supply."}

{"title": "Port publishes revised charges for empty container storage", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": "Tomas Bellwick", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay published a revised empty-container storage tariff today. Under the tariff now in force, the first five calendar days remain free. Storage from day six to day ten costs KRN 18 per standard container unit per day, compared with KRN 15 under the previous tariff.

The rate from day eleven is KRN 31 per unit per day, an increase of KRN 4. Loaded-container storage, vessel dues and rail handling charges are unchanged. Containers already in the empty yard before today remain subject to the previous rates for their current stay.

Empty containers occupied an average of 81 per cent of designated yard spaces during August. The revised charges follow a tariff review circulated to account holders in July. Customers can view the applicable rate and arrival date for each container in their account record, or request a written breakdown from the port billing team."}

{"title": "Berth Four repairs begin with reduced working area", "date": "2026-09-02", "author": "Nina Halden", "body": "Repair work began at Berth Four today after an inspection found worn fittings along the outer quay edge. A 95-metre section has been fenced off while crews replace six mooring bollards and repair the concrete around their foundations. The remaining 210 metres of the berth remain available for smaller vessels.

The authority has allocated KRN 8.4 million to the repairs, including replacement fenders and electrical checks. Two container services normally handled at Berth Four have been assigned to Berth Two. Shipping agents received revised working arrangements this morning, and the published berth list reflects those changes.

A marked pedestrian route runs behind the work area. Port users must keep clear of the fencing and follow instructions from quay staff. The inspection found no damage to the main quay structure. Cargo handling continues elsewhere in the terminal, with no change to freight gate opening hours."}

{"title": "North freight gate reopens after overnight equipment fault", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "The north freight gate at the Port of Kestrel Bay reopened at 09:20 today after a fault in its vehicle recognition equipment. The gate closed at 02:15. Freight traffic used the south entrance while technicians replaced a damaged power unit and checked the control system.

A total of 186 lorry bookings were affected. Drivers with valid bookings were admitted through the south gate without making a second booking, and the authority waived missed-slot charges linked to the closure. The longest recorded queue was 38 vehicles shortly before 07:00. No injuries or damage to cargo were reported.

Both freight entrances are now operating normally. Drivers whose booking records still show a missed visit can ask the gate office to correct the entry, quoting their vehicle registration and booking number. The fault did not affect rail movements, vessel operations or access to the empty-container yard."}

{"title": "Berth Four fully reopens after quay repairs finish", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Nina Halden", "body": "Berth Four returned to its full working length of 305 metres today after the final repaired section passed inspection. Work began on 2 September and covered six mooring bollards, eight fenders and the concrete around the bollard foundations. The temporary fencing has been removed.

The completed work cost KRN 8.1 million against an allocation of KRN 8.4 million. The total includes engineering inspections, load tests and disposal of removed materials. Engineers signed off the final three bollards yesterday, and terminal staff completed checks of lighting and quay access before reopening the section.

Both container services temporarily reassigned to Berth Two are now back at Berth Four. Shipping agents have received the updated berth plan. The authority has also closed the temporary pedestrian diversion behind the works. Port users can use the usual marked walkway, while access to the quay edge remains restricted to authorised staff."}

{"title": "September figures show shorter queues at freight gates", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Average lorry turnaround time at the Port of Kestrel Bay fell to 39 minutes in September, compared with 43 minutes in July. The monthly figure covers 31,480 completed visits and includes time spent waiting inside the terminal. Visits cancelled before arrival are excluded.

The port handled 82,900 standard container units during September. This was 2.4 per cent above the same month last year, despite the wind stoppage on 3 September and the reduced working length at Berth Four. Rail accounted for 29 per cent of containers leaving the port by land.

Gate staff recorded fewer arrivals without bookings after hauliers began receiving an additional booking reminder in August. Unbooked arrivals fell from 412 in August to 267 in September. The authority asks drivers to check the container release status before travelling, as a gate booking alone does not confirm that cargo is ready for collection."}

{"title": "Container volumes rise as inland rail services expand", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay handled 84,600 standard container units in July, an increase of 6.2 per cent compared with July last year. Food products, household goods and machine parts accounted for most of the growth. The figures count a forty-foot container as two standard units.

Rail carried 28 per cent of containers leaving the port by land, up from 24 per cent a year earlier. Two additional weekly services between the port and Amberford began operating in June. The inland terminal at Amberford now receives eight port trains each week.

Average lorry turnaround time was 43 minutes during July, measured from entry at the freight gate to departure. This was four minutes shorter than in June. The authority publishes these figures to help hauliers and cargo owners compare port performance. Empty containers and loaded containers are included in the gate measure."}

{"title": "Rail signalling fault delays inland container departures today", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "A signalling fault at Reedbank Junction interrupted freight rail access to the Port of Kestrel Bay today. The junction operator stopped movements at 06:25 after a track detection circuit failed. Three outbound trains carrying a combined 144 containers were held in the port rail yard.

One train was bound for Amberford and two for the Saltmarsh inland terminal. The line reopened at 13:40 following replacement of the faulty component and a test movement. The first delayed train left the port at 14:05. No containers were transferred to road transport during the closure.

Rail customers received revised departure details through their booking contacts. Containers held on the affected trains have been exempted from additional port storage charges caused by the interruption. Vessel loading and unloading continued throughout the incident, and freight gates remained open. The fault occurred outside the port boundary and did not affect the terminal's internal rail equipment."}

{"title": "Port clears delayed vessel queue after fog restrictions", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay cleared its delayed vessel queue at 15:30 today after dense fog restricted harbour movements overnight. Visibility at the outer entrance fell below 200 metres, and vessel movements were suspended from 23:10 yesterday until 07:45 today. Five arriving vessels were held at the designated anchorage.

Harbour staff resumed movements after visibility improved and patrol crews checked the entrance channel. Departing vessels moved first where this released an occupied berth. Two container vessels, two general cargo ships and one coastal tanker then entered under revised arrival slots agreed with their agents.

Cargo work continued on vessels already alongside during the restriction. Freight gates and rail services operated normally, although collection times changed for some containers aboard the delayed arrivals. Customers can check release status through their usual account record. No collisions, groundings or injuries were reported during the fog restriction or the clearance of the queue."}

{"title": "New bilingual freight guidance supports Val-Brume cargo customers", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Tomas Bellwick", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay has published freight collection guidance in English and French following requests from cargo customers in Val-Brume. The guidance covers gate bookings, container release checks, driver identification and the documents needed to collect goods through an appointed haulier.

Customers with billing addresses in the French-speaking region accounted for 12 per cent of loaded import collections during the first nine months of this year. The port's customer team reviewed 160 enquiries from those customers when preparing the guidance. Missing release references and uncertainty about collection authority were the most common problems.

Both language versions are available from the freight desk and in the customer account area. Printed copies carry the same numbered steps so staff and drivers can refer to the same instruction. The guidance explains existing procedures and introduces no additional documents or charges. Customers can request help from a French-speaking member of the customer team."}

{"title": "Exchange extends afternoon trading for all agricultural futures", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has extended its regular trading session by 30 minutes. From today, wheat, oat and rapeseed futures trade from 08:30 to 16:30, using the exchange clock in Ostara. The opening time is unchanged. Members can enter orders during the pre-opening period from 08:15.

The daily settlement calculation now uses eligible trading activity between 16:20 and 16:30. Where activity is insufficient, the exchange applies its existing rules for assessing bids, offers and related contract months. The longer session responds to requests from members handling afternoon warehouse confirmations in Amberford and freight arrangements through Kestrel Bay.

Orders marked to expire at the end of the trading day now remain active until 16:30 unless cancelled or filled. Orders carrying a specific expiry time retain that instruction. Members should review automated schedules and staffing arrangements. The extension does not alter delivery notice deadlines, collateral payment deadlines or the opening hours of approved warehouses."}

{"title": "Oat futures gain smaller price steps across listed months", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has reduced the minimum price movement for oat futures from KRN 0.50 to KRN 0.25 per tonne. The change took effect at today's opening and applies to all listed delivery months. With a contract size of 20 tonnes, each minimum movement is now worth KRN 5.

The exchange introduced the smaller step after members requested more precise pricing between bids and offers. Orders entered before the change remain valid because prices quoted in half-crown increments also meet the new quarter-crown requirement. Contract quantities, delivery standards and the method used to calculate daily settlement prices have not changed.

Members should check that their order systems accept prices ending in .25 and .75, as well as .00 and .50. The exchange's test service includes sample orders using each permitted ending. Support staff in Ostara and Val-Brume are handling queries about rejected prices and the display of contract values on member screens."}

{"title": "Amberford warehouse approved for additional wheat delivery capacity", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has approved a further 12,000 tonnes of wheat storage at the Merebank warehouse in Amberford. The additional capacity is eligible for exchange delivery from today. Approval follows checks of weighing equipment, sampling facilities, stock records and the separation of grain held for different owners.

The extension brings Merebank's approved wheat capacity to 30,000 tonnes. It does not add oat or rapeseed storage to the site's approval. Wheat placed into exchange delivery must meet the existing contract standards, including a maximum moisture content of 14.5 per cent. Warehouse approval does not replace the inspection required for each delivery lot.

Members arranging delivery should confirm available space directly with the warehouse before submitting a notice. The exchange's register now shows the additional capacity and the site's receiving hours. Merebank must report eligible stocks each trading day and notify the exchange immediately if equipment faults, access restrictions or stock discrepancies affect its ability to receive or release grain."}

{"title": "August trading figures show stronger activity in wheat futures", "date": "2026-09-04", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange recorded 48,620 futures contracts traded in August, up from 44,180 in July. Wheat accounted for 27,340 contracts, oats for 8,760 and rapeseed for 12,520. The figures count each matched contract once and exclude orders that were cancelled or remained unfilled.

Wheat trading increased by 14 per cent compared with July, with the largest share of activity in the nearest two delivery months. Across all three commodities, open interest stood at 18,460 contracts at the August close. Open interest measures contracts that remain outstanding rather than the number traded during the month.

There were 286 completed delivery transfers during August, representing 5,720 tonnes. Approved warehouses in Amberford handled 124 transfers, Ostara handled 98 and Kestrel Bay handled 64. The monthly statistics file includes delivery totals and activity by contract month. Members can download it from the exchange's document service without a separate subscription charge."}

{"title": "Rapeseed margins increased following wider daily price movements", "date": "2026-08-19", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has increased the initial margin on rapeseed futures to KRN 2,400 per contract, from KRN 2,100. The maintenance margin rises to KRN 2,000, from KRN 1,750. The revised requirements apply to positions held at today's clearing calculation and cover every listed rapeseed delivery month.

The change follows a review of price movements over the past ten trading sessions. The average daily price range reached KRN 21 per tonne, compared with KRN 14 during the preceding review period. Each rapeseed contract represents 20 tonnes. Margin is collateral against potential losses and is separate from the contract's purchase price.

Clearing members must meet any resulting collateral shortfall by 09:00 on the next trading day. Eligible collateral and payment instructions are unchanged. Wheat and oat margin requirements remain at their existing levels. The exchange reviews margins each trading day and can adjust them when price movements or the concentration of positions warrant a change."}

{"title": "Exchange publishes revised delivery guide for grain futures", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has published a revised delivery guide for its wheat and oat futures. The guide brings warehouse instructions, sampling rules and delivery forms into one document. It applies to delivery notices submitted from today and does not change the quantity represented by either contract.

Both contracts cover 20 tonnes. Approved delivery locations remain Ostara, Amberford and Kestrel Bay. Sellers must identify the warehouse, stored quantity and inspection reference on each notice. Buyers can request a second sample where the first inspection records a result within 0.2 percentage points of a contract quality limit.

The guide also explains how warehouse charges are divided when ownership changes. Storage remains the seller's responsibility through the transfer date, with the buyer responsible from the following day. Members can obtain the guide through the exchange's document service. A French-language version is available for members and warehouse operators in Val-Brume."}

{"title": "September statistics record higher open interest across agricultural contracts", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange recorded open interest of 20,180 contracts at the September close, an increase of 1,720 from August. Wheat accounted for 11,420 outstanding contracts, oats for 3,160 and rapeseed for 5,600. All three products ended September with more outstanding contracts than at the previous month end.

September trading volume totalled 51,260 contracts. This comprised 29,100 wheat contracts, 9,040 oat contracts and 13,120 rapeseed contracts. The busiest session recorded 3,480 contracts across the exchange. Volume counts completed trades during the month, while open interest records positions that remained outstanding after clearing at the month end.

Approved warehouses completed 312 delivery transfers, equivalent to 6,240 tonnes. Of these, 174 transfers were wheat, 58 were oats and 80 were rapeseed. The full statistics file is available through the exchange's document service. It includes daily trading totals and a breakdown by delivery month, with explanatory notes on how cancelled trades are treated."}

{"title": "Trading resumes after morning interruption to order entry", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": null, "body": "Trading in wheat, oat and rapeseed futures resumed at 10:05 today after an interruption to the exchange's order entry service. Ostara Commodities Exchange suspended matching at 09:12 when monitoring identified delays in order acknowledgements. A ten-minute period for members to review and cancel orders preceded the reopening.

Trades completed before the suspension remain valid. Orders that had been accepted and remained unfilled were retained with their original priority. Messages that did not receive an acceptance acknowledgement were not added to the order book. Members were asked to reconcile their records against the exchange's order and trade reports before entering replacement instructions.

Technical staff traced the interruption to a fault in a message queue and moved the service to reserve equipment. The regular 16:30 closing time remains in place. Clearing and warehouse reporting services continued to operate during the interruption. The exchange is reviewing system records and member reports to establish why the fault was not resolved automatically."}

{"title": "Exchange completes review of order entry service interruption", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has completed its review of the order entry interruption on 8 October. The review found that a software fault caused an internal message queue to stop releasing acknowledgements promptly. Automatic recovery did not start because the monitoring check measured connection availability without checking whether messages were progressing through the queue.

The exchange has added a separate check for message progress and tested the transfer to reserve equipment under simulated delays. It has also revised the procedure used by technical staff to confirm that order entry is ready to reopen. These changes are in operation. The review found no alteration to completed trade records.

Reconciliation with clearing members is complete, and no unresolved differences remain between accepted orders and the exchange's records for the affected period. Members can request their own message logs through the support service. The exchange has published a technical incident note setting out the interruption times, the cause and the checks applied before trading resumed."}

{"title": "Rapeseed delivery rules clarify sampling and retained records", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has issued a clarification of the sampling procedure for rapeseed delivered against its futures contracts. From today, delivery inspection records must state the sampling time, lot reference and seal numbers for retained samples. The clarification applies at every warehouse approved to handle exchange rapeseed deliveries.

Inspectors must prepare three sealed samples from each delivery lot: one for the seller, one for the buyer and one held by the warehouse. The warehouse sample must be retained for 45 calendar days after ownership transfers. Where a quality dispute remains open, it must be retained until the exchange confirms that the case is closed.

The maximum moisture content remains 9 per cent, and the maximum foreign matter allowance remains 2 per cent. Contract size and approved delivery locations are unchanged. Members can obtain the revised inspection form in English or French. Forms already completed for transferred lots remain valid; warehouses are not required to reopen completed deliveries solely to add the new record fields."}

{"title": "Wheat margin requirements rise after increased market volatility", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has raised the initial margin for wheat futures from KRN 1,600 to KRN 1,900 per contract. The maintenance margin increases from KRN 1,300 to KRN 1,550. The new requirements apply to all listed wheat months at today's clearing calculation, including positions opened before the announcement.

The adjustment reflects larger daily price movements and a greater concentration of positions in the nearest delivery month. Over the past five sessions, the average wheat price range was KRN 17 per tonne. Each contract covers 20 tonnes. The exchange has made no change to wheat delivery specifications or permitted price increments.

Clearing members must provide any additional collateral by 09:00 on the next trading day. Members may apply higher requirements to their own clients under their account agreements. Oat and rapeseed margins are unchanged by this notice. The exchange continues to assess both price risk and the time needed to close positions when setting its collateral requirements."}

{"title": "Exchange introduces calendar spread orders for wheat futures", "date": "2026-10-23", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has introduced calendar spread orders for wheat futures. Members can now submit a single order to buy one listed wheat delivery month and sell another in equal quantities. The service is available during the regular 08:30 to 16:30 session and uses the same contract specifications as outright wheat trading.

Spread prices represent the price of the later delivery month minus the price of the earlier month, quoted in KRN per tonne. The minimum price movement is KRN 0.25 per tonne, equivalent to KRN 5 for a spread covering one 20-tonne contract in each month. Both sides execute together, removing the need to submit two separate outright orders.

Completed spreads create a separate futures position in each delivery month. Existing delivery obligations and position limits continue to apply to those positions. The exchange's published margin schedule determines the collateral required, including any eligible offset between months. Members can access worked examples and test messages through the document and test services. Oat and rapeseed order types are unchanged."}

{"title": "Rapeseed margins reduced as daily price ranges narrow", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has reduced the initial margin on rapeseed futures from KRN 2,400 to KRN 2,200 per contract. The maintenance margin falls from KRN 2,000 to KRN 1,850. The revised requirements apply at today's clearing calculation across all listed rapeseed delivery months, with no change to contract size.

The reduction follows a sustained narrowing of daily price ranges. The average range over the latest ten trading sessions was KRN 15 per tonne, compared with KRN 21 at the August review that led to higher margins. The exchange also assessed outstanding positions and the volume available to close them during normal trading.

Any collateral made available by the reduction remains subject to the member's other obligations and the exchange's normal withdrawal checks. The lower requirement does not change the daily settlement of gains and losses. Wheat initial margin remains KRN 1,900 per contract. Oat requirements are unchanged. Members should consult their clearing statements for the effect on individual accounts."}

{"title": "Annual report records faster action on unpaid pension contributions", "date": "2026-09-25", "author": "Mara Ellwick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority's annual report, published today, records KRN 6.8 million in overdue employer contributions recovered following its intervention during the year ended 31 March 2026. The payments reached 73 workplace pension plans covering 18,420 members. Recoveries totalled KRN 5.1 million in the previous reporting year.

The median time from opening a contribution case to securing payment fell from 47 to 34 working days. The Authority issued 29 financial penalties and required improvements to record keeping at 61 plans. These figures cover completed cases; they do not include money still being pursued at the year end.

Operating expenditure was KRN 24.6 million against a budget of KRN 25.2 million. The report includes audited accounts, service measures and a breakdown of complaints about the Authority. Priorities for the current year include earlier checks on missed payments and clearer guidance for employers running smaller workplace plans."}

{"title": "Consultation opens on clearer recovery plans for underfunded pensions", "date": null, "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority is seeking views on proposed changes to the information employers and trustees provide when a workplace pension plan is underfunded. The consultation opens today and closes on 30 October. The proposals concern plans that promise members a set retirement income.

Under the proposals, recovery plans would explain why the payment schedule is affordable, what would happen if trading weakened and how trustees would monitor progress. A standard summary would show annual contributions in KRN alongside the expected funding shortfall. The Authority is also asking whether smaller plans need a shorter reporting form.

No funding rules change today. Existing recovery plans and payment schedules remain in force. Employers, trustees, advisers and members can respond through the Authority's consultation service or by post to its Saltmarsh office. An accompanying paper includes worked examples for a manufacturer, a transport business and a community services employer."}

{"title": "Val-Brume pension clinic offers support with annual returns", "date": "2026-09-23", "author": "Celia Bramwell", "body": "Trustees and employers in the Val-Brume area can now book free appointments at a pension reporting clinic on 8 October. Staff from the Pensions Oversight Authority will explain common annual return requirements and help visitors identify information missing from their records. Appointments will be available in French and English.

The clinic will take place at the Authority's Val-Brume office, with 36 appointments lasting 25 minutes each. Visitors should bring their plan registration number and a list of questions. They should not bring members' identity documents, bank details or individual medical records. Telephone appointments are available for those unable to attend.

The service follows a review of 94 returns from plans administered in the region. The most frequent errors concerned membership totals and changes to trustee contact details. Staff can explain reporting requirements but cannot complete returns for applicants or provide investment advice. Booking closes on 5 October, unless all appointments are filled earlier."}

{"title": "Amberford employer fined after repeated delays to pension contributions", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": "Leila Corven", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has fined Amberford employer Brindle Quay Components KRN 42,000 after it repeatedly paid workplace pension contributions late. The company missed payment deadlines in seven months between September 2025 and April 2026. The delays affected 286 members of its workplace pension plan.

The employer has now paid the outstanding KRN 193,400, together with KRN 8,760 to compensate the plan for lost investment income. The fine is separate from those payments and must be paid by the company. It cannot be taken from pension assets or charged to members.

The Authority issued its final decision after considering the company's response to an earlier notice. The company has 28 days to appeal. Trustees have been asked to check contributions each month and report further delays. Members do not need to submit individual claims to receive the compensation within the plan."}

{"title": "Workplace pension funding improves in second quarter review", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Ellwick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority estimates that workplace pension plans promising a set retirement income held assets equal to 104 per cent of their liabilities at the end of June. This compares with 102 per cent at the end of March. The review covers 842 plans across Kestria, with combined assets of KRN 186 billion.

The improvement mainly reflects higher investment income and employer contributions. Funding remains uneven: 176 plans held less than the estimated amount needed to meet their promises. A national average does not show whether an individual plan can pay every benefit when it falls due.

Employers and trustees should continue making scheduled payments and reviewing their funding assumptions. The figures are estimates based on quarterly returns, rather than full valuations of every plan. The Authority has published regional tables, including separate results for Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume."}

{"title": "Employers given clearer steps for reporting late pension payments", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has published guidance explaining how employers and trustees should report late workplace pension contributions. It includes examples covering payroll errors, missing payment references and repeated delays. The guidance applies to reports made from today and does not change the legal payment deadlines.

Trustees should record when a payment was due, the amount outstanding and the steps taken to recover it. Where a report is required, it should distinguish money deducted from staff pay from contributions owed by the employer. Employers should give trustees a named contact who can resolve payment queries.

During the first six months of 2026, the Authority received 318 reports involving late payments. Of these, 241 were resolved within 20 working days of the initial report. The new guidance includes a reporting checklist and sample payment record, with versions available in English and French."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay trustees directed to replace incomplete pension records", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Leila Corven", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has directed the trustees of the Merehaven Packaging Retirement Plan in Kestrel Bay to repair incomplete membership records. An inspection found missing service dates or pay histories for 417 of the plan's 2,860 members. These records are needed to calculate promised retirement benefits accurately.

The trustees must produce a costed repair programme within 30 days and complete the work by 31 March 2027. They must also arrange an independent check of corrected records and send monthly progress reports to the Authority. The direction follows an earlier request for improvements that was not completed.

The inspection did not find evidence that assets were missing. Existing pension payments will continue, and the administrator will contact members if it needs information from them. Members should retain old benefit statements and employment records where available. The trustees have 28 days to appeal against the direction."}

{"title": "Val-Brume clinic identifies common questions about pension membership totals", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Celia Bramwell", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has published additional annual return guidance following its reporting clinic in Val-Brume on 8 October. Staff held 34 appointments with employers and trustees, including nine by telephone. Most questions concerned how to count members who had left employment but retained pension benefits.

The updated guidance explains how to report contributing members, members with benefits left in a plan and members already receiving a pension. It also covers people who appear in more than one category during a reporting year. Worked examples show which position to record on the return's specified reporting date.

The examples are available in French and English and can be used by plans throughout Kestria. They clarify existing requirements and do not introduce new categories. Plans that have already submitted a return should check the guidance and contact the Authority if they identify a material error. Routine contact changes can be submitted through the usual update service."}

{"title": "New guidance explains pension duties when employers change payroll", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "Employers changing payroll systems should test pension deductions before the first live pay run, according to guidance published today by the Pensions Oversight Authority. The guidance addresses errors involving contribution rates, pensionable pay and the transfer of employee records. It applies to employers of all sizes.

The Authority reviewed 52 contribution incidents linked to payroll changes between January and August 2026. In 31 cases, an incorrect setting was copied across a whole group of employees. The guidance recommends checking sample calculations, agreeing payment references with the pension administrator and confirming who will investigate any mismatch.

Changing a payroll provider does not change an employer's responsibility to pay contributions in full and on time. Employers should keep a record of checks and explain corrections clearly to affected staff. The publication includes examples for weekly and monthly payrolls, together with a handover checklist that can be shared with payroll providers."}

{"title": "Trustees asked to check member addresses before annual statements", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Celia Bramwell", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority is asking workplace pension trustees to check their member contact records before issuing annual benefit statements this autumn. A review of 120 plans found that 8.4 per cent of statements sent during the previous reporting cycle were returned or could not be delivered electronically.

Plans should compare their records with recent updates from members and employers, investigate returned post and provide a straightforward way to correct an address. Trustees should take particular care with former employees who still hold benefits in the plan. An employer's current payroll list will not necessarily include these members.

The Authority has published a short checklist covering address checks, secure identity verification and records of delivery failures. Members who have moved should contact their pension administrator using details from an existing statement. They should never be asked to pay a fee simply to update their postal or email address."}

{"title": "Third quarter figures show fewer unresolved pension payment reports", "date": null, "author": "Mara Ellwick", "body": "There were 64 unresolved reports of late workplace pension contributions at 30 September, down from 81 at 30 June, according to figures published today by the Pensions Oversight Authority. The figures count reports under investigation and do not represent every late payment across Kestria.

The Authority received 147 new reports during the third quarter and closed 164. Of the closed reports, 139 ended with full payment, 17 concerned reporting errors and eight were combined with existing investigations. Reports can cover several missed payments by the same employer, so case numbers should not be read as employer totals.

Among the unresolved reports, 12 had been open for more than 90 days. These included disputed payroll records and employers experiencing financial difficulty. Trustees should continue pursuing unpaid contributions while an investigation is open. Reporting a delay to the Authority does not transfer responsibility for collecting payments from the plan's trustees."}

{"title": "One week remains to comment on pension recovery proposals", "date": "2026-10-23", "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "Employers, trustees and pension members have until 30 October to respond to the Pensions Oversight Authority's consultation on recovery plans for underfunded workplace pensions. The consultation opened on 14 September. It proposes clearer explanations of payment schedules, business risks and the steps trustees would take if a recovery plan fell behind.

By 22 October, the Authority had received 68 responses, including 21 from employers and 14 from trustee boards. Responses received so far raise questions about the work required of smaller plans and how employers should describe uncertain future income. The Authority has added answers to procedural questions to its consultation page.

Respondents can answer individual questions without completing the whole document. They should identify any information they consider confidential and explain why. Existing funding requirements remain in place while the proposals are considered. The Authority will review submissions after the closing date and publish its response before setting any implementation timetable."}

{"title": "Un premier bilan des demandes au programme Atelier durable", "date": "2026-09-17", "author": "Bastien Corvelin", "body": "Depuis l’ouverture du programme Atelier durable le 21 août, l’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume a reçu 43 demandes représentant 2,1 millions de KRN d’aide sollicitée. De ce nombre, 31 dossiers contiennent toutes les pièces nécessaires à leur analyse. Les douze autres nécessitent des renseignements complémentaires, principalement des soumissions détaillées.

Les demandes proviennent de huit localités. Elles concernent surtout le travail du bois, la fabrication de pièces et la réparation d’équipements. À ce jour, aucune contribution n’a été accordée. Le montant demandé ne constitue donc pas un engagement de l’Agence et ne doit pas être interprété comme une somme déjà versée aux entreprises.

L’enveloppe du programme demeure de 3,6 millions de KRN. Pour faciliter l’analyse, l’Agence rappelle que chaque soumission doit préciser le modèle d’équipement, son prix et les frais d’installation. Les entreprises peuvent compléter leur dossier avec l’aide des équipes de Val-Brume ou de Rive-des-Ormes. Une demande d’information auprès d’un conseiller ne réserve aucune portion de l’enveloppe."}

{"title": "Ostara employer agrees repayment schedule for outstanding pension contributions", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Leila Corven", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has accepted an enforceable undertaking from Ostara employer Tarnwick Cold Stores to repay KRN 724,000 in outstanding workplace pension contributions. The arrears relate to employer contributions due between February and July 2026 and affect 612 members. Contributions deducted from employees' pay were paid to the plan.

The company paid KRN 224,000 on 19 October. The remaining KRN 500,000 must be paid in five monthly instalments, beginning on 20 November, alongside all new contributions as they fall due. The company must also pay compensation for lost investment income, calculated under the terms of the undertaking.

Trustees will confirm receipt of each payment directly to the Authority. If the employer breaches the undertaking, the Authority can seek a court order requiring compliance. Members will receive a written update from the plan administrator. Acceptance of the undertaking does not mean the arrears have been cleared."}

{"title": "Un soutien de proximité pour les commerces de Clairvallon", "date": "2026-09-10", "author": "Maëlle Vardelin", "body": "Neuf commerces de Clairvallon bénéficient d’un soutien total de 342 000 KRN pour adapter leurs locaux et améliorer leur fonctionnement. L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume a confirmé ces aides dans le cadre du programme Commerce de proximité. Les investissements admissibles des entreprises totalisent 855 000 KRN.

Les projets comprennent l’installation de portes accessibles, le remplacement de chambres froides et l’aménagement de zones de préparation de commandes. L’Épicerie des Aulnes reçoit notamment 48 000 KRN pour réorganiser sa réserve et remplacer deux appareils de réfrigération. La Maison du Fil de Brume obtient 21 000 KRN pour adapter son entrée et son espace de service.

Chaque contribution représente 40 % des dépenses reconnues. Les travaux de décoration et les achats destinés à la revente sont exclus. Les commerces retenus comptent ensemble 61 emplois, mais les conventions ne présentent pas ce nombre comme une création d’emplois. Le suivi porte sur les factures, la réalisation des aménagements approuvés et le maintien de l’activité dans les locaux concernés."}

{"title": "Un comptoir supplémentaire pour accompagner les entreprises de Rive-des-Ormes", "date": "2026-08-27", "author": "Bastien Corvelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume a ouvert aujourd’hui un comptoir de services à Rive-des-Ormes, dans la maison régionale des métiers. Deux conseillères y accompagnent les entreprises dans la préparation de leurs demandes de subvention et de crédit d’impôt. Ce point de service dessert aussi les secteurs voisins de Clairvallon et du Hameau-des-Saules.

Le comptoir dispose de trois espaces de consultation, dont un bureau fermé pour les échanges confidentiels. Son aménagement a nécessité un investissement de 86 000 KRN, payé à même le budget de fonctionnement de l’Agence. Les services sont gratuits. Les entreprises peuvent prendre rendez-vous par téléphone ou demander une première orientation directement à l’accueil.

L’équipe aide notamment à vérifier les dépenses admissibles au programme Atelier durable, ouvert le 21 août. Elle peut aussi expliquer les pièces à joindre aux demandes existantes. L’ouverture de ce comptoir répond à une consultation menée en juin auprès de 74 entreprises, dont 46 avaient signalé des difficultés de déplacement vers Val-Brume."}

{"title": "Le crédit régional simplifie la déclaration des dépenses admissibles", "date": "2026-09-01", "author": "Lucien Dorselin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume met aujourd’hui en service un formulaire simplifié pour le crédit régional à l’investissement productif. Les entreprises peuvent désormais regrouper leurs dépenses d’équipement dans un seul tableau. Cette modification réduit le nombre de pièces à remplir, sans changer le taux du crédit ni les critères d’admissibilité.

Le crédit correspond à 12 % des dépenses reconnues, jusqu’à concurrence de 240 000 KRN par entreprise et par exercice. Le nouveau tableau distingue le prix d’achat, les frais d’installation et les autres aides reçues. Il comporte aussi une case pour signaler un équipement partagé entre plusieurs établissements, une situation qui demandait auparavant une note séparée.

La révision s’appuie sur un essai réalisé en juillet avec 18 entreprises et six cabinets de tenue de livres. Le temps médian de préparation observé est passé de 95 à 62 minutes. Les demandes déjà déposées demeurent valides. L’Agence n’exige pas leur transcription dans le nouveau formulaire et poursuit leur traitement selon leur date de réception."}

{"title": "Douze entreprises reçoivent une aide pour leurs diagnostics énergétiques", "date": "2026-08-28", "author": "Élodie Mireval", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume accorde une aide totale de 216 000 KRN à douze entreprises dans le cadre du volet Diagnostic énergétique. Ces contributions soutiennent l’examen des systèmes de chauffage, de ventilation et de production. Les entreprises retenues emploient ensemble 287 personnes dans cinq localités de la région.

Parmi les bénéficiaires, les Séchoirs de Brumecôte obtiennent 24 000 KRN pour mesurer les pertes de chaleur de leurs installations. La Menuiserie des Trois-Vernes reçoit 16 500 KRN pour analyser sa consommation d’électricité. Les montants accordés couvrent la moitié des honoraires admissibles, selon la portée de chaque diagnostic et les soumissions déposées.

Les conventions d’aide ont toutes été signées. Le versement des contributions exige un rapport technique et des factures acquittées. Aucune économie d’énergie n’est comptabilisée à cette étape : le diagnostic sert à établir les possibilités d’amélioration. Une entreprise qui souhaite ensuite financer des équipements doit présenter une demande distincte au programme correspondant."}

{"title": "Les délais de traitement diminuent pour le crédit régional", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Lucien Dorselin", "body": "Le délai médian de traitement des dossiers complets du crédit régional à l’investissement productif s’établit à 19 jours ouvrables en septembre, comparativement à 27 en août. L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume publie aujourd’hui cet indicateur avec le nombre de décisions rendues. Il mesure la période entre la réception d’un dossier complet et la transmission de la décision.

En septembre, l’Agence a rendu 38 décisions : 33 attestations favorables et cinq refus. Les dépenses reconnues dans les dossiers acceptés totalisent 8,4 millions de KRN, pour des crédits de 1 008 000 KRN. Les refus concernent des équipements non admissibles ou des dépenses engagées hors de la période couverte par le programme.

Le formulaire simplifié, offert depuis le 1er septembre, facilite la vérification des montants déclarés. Une réaffectation interne a aussi ajouté une personne à l’équipe d’analyse. Le délai publié n’est pas une garantie individuelle : les dossiers qui nécessitent des précisions peuvent demander davantage de temps. L’attestation de l’Agence ne constitue pas un versement de fonds."}

{"title": "Une enveloppe régionale pour moderniser les petits ateliers", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": "Maëlle Vardelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume annonce aujourd’hui une enveloppe de 3,6 millions de couronnes kestrianes (KRN) pour la modernisation des petits ateliers de la région. Le programme Atelier durable est ouvert aux entreprises comptant de 3 à 49 personnes. Il vise le remplacement d’équipements usés, la réduction des pertes de matières et l’amélioration des postes de travail.

L’aide couvre jusqu’à 35 % des dépenses admissibles, pour un maximum de 180 000 KRN par entreprise. Les projets doivent représenter un investissement d’au moins 40 000 KRN. Les achats effectués avant le dépôt d’une demande ne sont pas admissibles. Chaque dossier doit comprendre deux soumissions et une description des gains attendus.

Les entreprises de Val-Brume, de Rive-des-Ormes et de Mont-des-Filons peuvent obtenir un accompagnement gratuit auprès de l’Agence. Le formulaire et le guide du programme sont accessibles dès aujourd’hui sur son site. Une version imprimée est également disponible au comptoir d’accueil de Val-Brume."}

{"title": "Sept ateliers obtiennent un appui pour moderniser leurs équipements", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Élodie Mireval", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume confirme les sept premières contributions du programme Atelier durable, pour un total de 924 000 KRN. Les projets approuvés représentent des investissements de 2,8 millions de KRN. Ils concernent quatre entreprises de Val-Brume, deux de Mont-des-Filons et une de Rive-des-Ormes.

Les Ateliers du Haut-Saule reçoivent 168 000 KRN pour remplacer une ligne de découpe et installer un système de récupération des chutes. À Mont-des-Filons, Mécanique Brumacier obtient 126 000 KRN pour acquérir deux appareils d’usinage. Les évaluations techniques jointes aux dossiers décrivent les gains attendus; ceux-ci restent à vérifier après la mise en service.

Ces décisions portent sur des demandes complètes examinées depuis le bilan du 17 septembre. Les sommes annoncées sont engagées dans les conventions, mais aucun remboursement final n’a encore été effectué. Les versements exigent des preuves d’achat et d’installation. L’Agence maintient une enveloppe de 3,6 millions de KRN pour l’ensemble du programme et poursuit l’analyse des autres dossiers reçus."}

{"title": "Une aide ciblée pour six entreprises du Hameau-des-Saules", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": "Maëlle Vardelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume accorde 198 000 KRN à six entreprises du Hameau-des-Saules pour remettre en état des équipements endommagés par le refoulement du collecteur local du 30 septembre. Le soutien provient du fonds de continuité des petites entreprises. Les dossiers ont été examinés à partir des constats de dommages et des estimations de réparation.

L’aide couvre jusqu’à 50 % des dépenses admissibles qui ne sont pas remboursées par une assurance. Chaque entreprise doit déclarer les indemnités reçues. Les conventions prévoient un ajustement si une indemnité supplémentaire couvre une dépense déjà soutenue par l’Agence. Les pertes de ventes et la valeur des stocks détruits sont exclues de cette aide.

Les projets acceptés comprennent la réparation de moteurs, le nettoyage spécialisé d’installations et le remplacement de commandes électriques. Les six entreprises comptent 44 personnes à leur emploi. Deux ont déjà repris leurs activités dans leurs locaux. L’Agence accompagne les autres dans la préparation des pièces requises pour le remboursement des travaux autorisés."}

{"title": "Une vérification précise les règles de cumul des aides", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Lucien Dorselin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume publie une fiche explicative sur le cumul des aides après une vérification de 24 dossiers d’investissement. L’examen, terminé le 19 octobre, a relevé trois déclarations incomplètes concernant d’autres contributions publiques. Les entreprises concernées ont fourni les renseignements manquants. Les corrections représentent ensemble 37 200 KRN.

Dans deux dossiers, la correction a réduit un solde de subvention qui n’avait pas encore été versé. Dans le troisième, un remboursement de 8 400 KRN a été reçu le 20 octobre. La vérification n’a relevé aucune autre somme à récupérer dans l’échantillon examiné. Elle ne permet toutefois pas de conclure sur l’ensemble des dossiers de l’Agence.

La nouvelle fiche présente trois exemples chiffrés et indique où déclarer une subvention, un crédit d’impôt ou une aide remboursable. Les plafonds demeurent ceux de chaque programme. L’Agence rappelle qu’une même facture peut devoir être déclarée dans plusieurs formulaires, même lorsque les aides sont administrées par des équipes différentes. Les entreprises peuvent faire vérifier leur calcul avant de transmettre leur déclaration."}

{"title": "Le rapport annuel détaille les aides et leurs résultats", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Bastien Corvelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume publie son rapport annuel pour l’exercice terminé le 30 juin 2026. Elle y présente 146 conventions de subvention totalisant 18,7 millions de KRN et 92 attestations de crédit d’impôt représentant 6,2 millions de KRN. Ces deux formes de soutien sont comptabilisées séparément dans les tableaux financiers.

Les conventions de subvention concernent 131 entreprises, certaines ayant obtenu une aide pour deux projets distincts. Parmi les 88 projets dont le suivi technique est terminé, 76 ont atteint tous les objectifs inscrits à leur convention. Huit les ont atteints en partie et quatre ont été abandonnés. Les sommes versées pour ces quatre projets ont été récupérées.

Le rapport comprend aussi les dépenses de fonctionnement de l’Agence, établies à 2,9 millions de KRN, ainsi que la répartition territoriale des aides. Les résultats reposent sur les factures, les visites et les déclarations vérifiées disponibles à la clôture. Le document et ses tableaux en format accessible sont offerts sur le site de l’Agence et à ses deux comptoirs."}

{"title": "Les ateliers de demande ont accompagné cinquante-deux entreprises régionales", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Élodie Mireval", "body": "Cinquante-deux entreprises ont participé aux ateliers gratuits de préparation des demandes offerts par l’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume entre le 2 septembre et le 15 octobre. Les six séances, tenues à Val-Brume et à Rive-des-Ormes, ont réuni 67 personnes. Elles portaient sur les budgets de projet, les soumissions et les preuves de paiement.

Les exercices utilisaient des dossiers fictifs pour expliquer comment distinguer une dépense admissible d’une dépense courante. Une attention particulière a été accordée au cumul des subventions et des crédits d’impôt. Sur les 49 questionnaires remis, 41 indiquent que la séance a permis de corriger au moins une erreur dans un budget en préparation.

Le coût total des séances s’élève à 14 600 KRN, incluant les salles, les documents et le temps de préparation externe. Les fiches utilisées sont maintenant accessibles sur le site de l’Agence. La participation ne donne aucun avantage dans l’évaluation d’une demande. Les entreprises qui n’ont pas assisté aux séances peuvent obtenir les mêmes explications auprès d’un conseiller."}

{"title": "Settlement system completes recovery exercise from Amberford backup site", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": null, "body": "The Bank of Kestria has completed a recovery exercise for the system that settles large payments between regulated banks. During the exercise on 27 September, staff transferred test operations from Saltmarsh to the Bank's backup site in Amberford. The exercise used simulated payment instructions and did not move customer funds.

All 14 participating institutions connected successfully to the backup service. Processing resumed after 43 minutes, within the Bank's internal recovery limit of 60 minutes. The test included the loss of a primary communications link and a check that completed instructions could not be processed twice.

Observers identified two areas for improvement: clearer contact arrangements during staff changes and faster confirmation that every participant had received the recovery message. Neither affected the completion of the exercise. The Bank has assigned corrective actions and will track them through its operational risk programme. Normal settlement services remained available throughout."}

{"title": "Bank reduces policy interest rate as price pressures ease", "date": "2026-09-25", "author": null, "body": "The Bank of Kestria today reduced its policy interest rate by 0.25 percentage points to 3.75 per cent. Annual consumer price inflation slowed to 2.4 per cent in August, from 2.8 per cent in July. Lower goods inflation and a further easing in wage growth supported the decision.

Recent evidence suggests that domestic demand remains subdued. Retail sales volumes declined slightly over the three months to August, while businesses reported limited plans to expand their workforce. Service prices are still rising faster than the overall price index, and the Bank remains attentive to that pressure.

The reduction provides a modest adjustment as inflation moves closer to the 2 per cent target. It does not establish a fixed path for interest rates. The Bank will assess each decision using the information available at the time, including household spending, labour costs and businesses' pricing decisions."}

{"title": "Bank opens consultation on clearer payment outage notices", "date": "2026-09-07", "author": null, "body": "The Bank of Kestria today opened a consultation on proposed rules for informing customers about payment service outages. The proposals would require regulated providers to publish an initial notice within 20 minutes of confirming a significant interruption and to update it at least once an hour.

Notices would need to explain which services are affected, whether customers should retry a payment and what alternatives remain available. Providers would also have to publish a closing notice when normal service resumes. The proposed rules would apply to both planned maintenance and unexpected failures, with different notice requirements for each.

The consultation draws on the Bank's review of 17 interruptions reported during the first half of this year. In six cases, public messages did not clearly identify the affected service. The Bank invites comments from payment providers, businesses and individual customers. The proposals are open for comment and are not yet binding."}

{"title": "New payment tables show wider use of instant transfers", "date": "2026-08-25", "author": null, "body": "Instant transfers accounted for 38 per cent of electronic account payments in July, according to payment statistics published today by the Bank of Kestria. This compares with 31 per cent a year earlier. The tables cover domestic payments in Kestrian crowns and exclude cash withdrawals and transfers between a bank's own accounts.

The average instant transfer was KRN 246. Payments below KRN 100 made up just over half of transactions, reflecting their use for small purchases and shared household expenses. Providers reported that 99.3 per cent of transfers reached the receiving account within ten seconds.

The Bank has added separate figures for rejected and returned payments to help users understand service performance. These categories do not by themselves indicate fraud or a system failure. The accompanying notes explain how providers count transactions and how revisions are recorded. All figures are available without charge."}

{"title": "Financial stability review highlights commercial property refinancing risks", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": null, "body": "Kestria's banking system has sufficient capital to absorb substantial losses, but commercial property lending warrants close attention, according to the Bank of Kestria's financial stability review published today. Regulated banks reported an aggregate core capital ratio of 15.6 per cent at the end of June, compared with 15.2 per cent a year earlier.

The review estimates that KRN 8.7 billion of commercial property loans require refinancing within twelve months. Office buildings in Saltmarsh and warehouse developments around Kestrel Bay account for a large share. Lower rental income could make refinancing harder even following September's reduction in the policy rate.

In the Bank's adverse scenario, property values fall by 25 per cent and unemployment rises sharply. Aggregate bank capital remains above minimum requirements, although outcomes vary between lenders. The scenario is a resilience test, not a forecast. The Bank has asked lenders to strengthen records on lease expiry dates and borrower cash flows."}

{"title": "Household debt review finds uneven pressure across income groups", "date": "2026-09-08", "author": null, "body": "Most households continued to meet their loan payments during the second quarter, but pressure increased among borrowers with smaller financial reserves, the Bank of Kestria said today. Its household debt review found that 2.4 per cent of outstanding housing loans were more than 90 days overdue, compared with 2.2 per cent in the previous quarter.

The share of overdue unsecured loans rose from 4.6 to 4.9 per cent. Borrowers reporting reduced working hours accounted for much of the increase. The review combines regulated lenders' returns with an anonymous household survey covering Saltmarsh, Ostara, Amberford, Kestrel Bay and Val-Brume.

The Bank found no evidence of a broad withdrawal of household credit. However, lenders should assess whether repayment plans remain affordable when income falls. The review's survey estimates carry a margin of uncertainty, and differences between cities should not be treated as rankings of local financial health."}

{"title": "Bank of Kestria holds policy rate at four per cent", "date": "2026-08-19", "author": null, "body": "The Bank of Kestria today maintained its policy interest rate at 4.00 per cent. The decision balances slower price growth against continuing pressure from service costs. Annual consumer price inflation fell to 2.8 per cent in July, from 3.1 per cent in June, but remains above the Bank's 2 per cent target.

Household spending was broadly unchanged during the second quarter. Business surveys showed weaker demand for manufactured goods in Amberford and steadier activity among transport firms in Kestrel Bay. Wage growth eased, although employers continued to report difficulty filling some skilled posts.

The Bank judged that the current rate remains appropriate while these pressures work through the economy. Further decisions will depend on evidence about inflation, wages and domestic demand. Today's decision does not change the Bank's operating arrangements for supplying short-term funds to regulated banks."}

{"title": "Quarterly survey shows inflation expectations moving closer to target", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": null, "body": "Households expect prices to rise by 2.6 per cent over the next year, according to the median response in the Bank of Kestria's quarterly expectations survey. This is down from 2.9 per cent in the previous survey. Expectations for annual inflation three years ahead remained at 2.2 per cent.

The survey collected responses from 2,400 adults between 21 September and 2 October. Respondents in all five surveyed cities reported lower expectations for food price increases. Views on housing costs were more varied, with tenants generally expecting larger increases than households that owned their homes outright.

The collection period included the policy rate decision announced on 25 September, so the results cannot isolate its effect on public expectations. Survey responses describe what people expect, rather than measuring actual price changes. The Bank considers this evidence alongside observed inflation, wage growth and spending when assessing price pressures."}

{"title": "Bank publishes French payment guidance for Val-Brume businesses", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": null, "body": "The Bank of Kestria today published a French-language guide to payment services for businesses in Val-Brume and the surrounding French-speaking region. The guide explains settlement times, payment references, refunds and the steps a business can take when money does not arrive as expected.

The publication follows six local workshops attended by 186 business owners and bookkeepers. Participants most often asked how to distinguish a pending payment from a completed transfer and how to reconcile several customer payments against one invoice. Worked examples use fictional transactions in Kestrian crowns.

The guide also explains the Bank's role in regulating payment systems and the separate responsibility of providers to resolve individual customer complaints. It includes a one-page checklist for recording a payment problem before contacting a provider. Both French and English editions are available free of charge in accessible digital formats, with printed copies supplied through the Bank's public information service."}

{"title": "Updated business returns lift second quarter growth estimate", "date": "2026-09-22", "author": "Elian Corven", "body": "Kestria's economic output grew by 0.5 per cent in the second quarter of 2026, according to updated estimates published today. This revises the initial estimate of 0.4 per cent released on 27 August. First quarter growth remains at 0.2 per cent. Output was 1.4 per cent above its level a year earlier.

The revision mainly reflects stronger activity among transport and warehousing businesses than early returns indicated. Additional responses from firms operating around Kestrel Bay account for part of that change. Manufacturing growth remains at 0.3 per cent, and the estimated fall in construction output remains at 0.6 per cent.

Household spending growth has been revised from 0.3 to 0.4 per cent. Business investment growth is unchanged at 0.8 per cent. Revisions are a normal part of producing the economic accounts: later estimates draw on more complete information. All growth figures in this release exclude the effects of price changes."}

{"title": "August labour survey shows a small rise in unemployment", "date": "2026-09-16", "author": "Tovan Merrow", "body": "The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate rose to 5.5 per cent in August, from 5.4 per cent in July. Employment was estimated at 2,843,000, down by 3,000 over the month. The number of unemployed people increased by 4,000 to 166,000, while the number outside the labour force changed little.

Accommodation and food services recorded fewer workers after July's increase. Employment in health and social care continued to grow. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 was 73.0 per cent. Average weekly hours worked were broadly stable, with no clear change in the balance between full-time and part-time work.

The monthly movements are small relative to the uncertainty around the survey estimates. Results for the three months to August show employment above its level in the previous three months. The Kestria Statistics Office recommends using that wider comparison when assessing the direction of the labour market."}

{"title": "Economic output rises by 0.4 per cent in second quarter", "date": "2026-08-27", "author": "Elian Corven", "body": "Kestria's economic output grew by 0.4 per cent in the second quarter of 2026, following growth of 0.2 per cent in the first quarter. Output was 1.3 per cent higher than in the same quarter of 2025. These first estimates remove the effects of price changes and regular seasonal patterns.

Services provided the largest contribution to quarterly growth. Transport, warehousing and business support expanded, while retail activity was broadly unchanged. Manufacturing output increased by 0.3 per cent, supported by food processing around Amberford. Construction output fell by 0.6 per cent as several large building projects reached completion.

Household spending increased by 0.3 per cent, while business investment rose by 0.8 per cent. The Kestria Statistics Office has based this release on the information currently available. Some business returns remain outstanding, so the estimate may change as fuller survey results and administrative records are incorporated."}

{"title": "Payment providers directed to improve duplicate transfer controls", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": null, "body": "The Bank of Kestria has directed three regulated payment providers to improve controls that prevent duplicate transfers after a connection failure. A supervisory review found that some systems could accept a repeated instruction without reliably checking whether the original payment had already been completed.

The review examined 11 providers and identified 428 duplicate transfers, worth KRN 312,600, during July to September. All affected customers have been repaid. The Bank found that the weaknesses arose within the three providers' own processing systems; the central settlement system recorded the instructions it received correctly.

The directions require stronger transaction checks, clearer records of repeated instructions and independent confirmation that the changes work. The Bank is monitoring compliance directly with each provider. These technical requirements are separate from the consultation opened on 7 September about public outage notices. Customers who suspect a duplicate payment should contact their provider with the transaction references."}

{"title": "Employment grows as July unemployment rate holds steady", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Tovan Merrow", "body": "Kestria had an estimated 2,846,000 people in employment in July, an increase of 7,000 from June. The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate remained at 5.4 per cent. An estimated 162,000 people were unemployed, meaning they were without work, available to start and actively seeking a job.

Employment increased most in accommodation, food services and building maintenance. Manufacturing employment changed little. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 rose to 73.1 per cent from 73.0 per cent. These estimates come from the household labour survey and cover employees and people working for themselves.

Survey responses from Kestrel Bay indicated stronger seasonal hiring, although local monthly estimates are less reliable than the national figures. The Kestria Statistics Office advises users to consider several months together. Small monthly movements may reflect sampling variation rather than a lasting change in the labour market."}

{"title": "August goods imports rise faster than export values", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Nessa Talvern", "body": "Kestria's goods trade deficit widened to KRN 710 million in August, from KRN 540 million in July. Exports totalled KRN 4.86 billion and imports totalled KRN 5.57 billion. Seasonally adjusted export values increased by 0.8 per cent over the month, while import values increased by 3.9 per cent.

Higher machinery and fuel imports contributed most to the wider deficit. Export growth was concentrated in processed food, while electrical equipment exports declined slightly after July's increase. Businesses in the Ostara industrial area reported several large machinery deliveries, illustrating how individual purchases can affect monthly trade totals.

Over the three months to August, exports were 1.7 per cent higher than in the previous three months and imports were 2.2 per cent higher. These comparisons use values at current prices. They therefore reflect changes in prices as well as quantities. July totals are unchanged from the previous release."}

{"title": "New regional tables explain differences in household spending", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "Households in Saltmarsh devoted an average of 29 per cent of their spending to housing and household services in 2025, compared with 24 per cent across Kestria. New regional tables published today show how spending patterns differed across the country. The figures describe expenditure shares rather than differences in the price of identical goods.

Transport accounted for 17 per cent of spending in the Amberford region, above the national share of 14 per cent. In the Val-Brume region, food and non-alcoholic drinks accounted for 18 per cent, compared with 16 per cent nationally. Household size, income and access to services can all affect these shares.

The tables use the 2025 household expenditure survey and include estimates of sampling uncertainty. They cannot establish which region is cheapest to live in. The Kestria Statistics Office has published accompanying notes in both Kestria's main administrative language and French to support use of the Val-Brume results."}

{"title": "Annual consumer price inflation eases to 3.2 per cent", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "Consumer prices in Kestria rose by 3.2 per cent in the year to July, down from 3.5 per cent in June. Prices increased by 0.2 per cent between June and July. The annual rate measures how much the price of a representative basket of goods and services has changed over twelve months.

Slower growth in food prices made the largest contribution to the easing. Food prices were 4.1 per cent higher than a year earlier, compared with 4.8 per cent in June. Fresh vegetable prices fell during July, particularly in markets surveyed in Amberford and Ostara. Rents continued to rise.

The lower inflation rate does not mean that prices generally fell. It means they rose more slowly than before. The Kestria Statistics Office collected prices from shops, service providers and online sellers across the country. July figures include the usual seasonal adjustment to the sample of summer clothing."}

{"title": "August consumer price tables corrected after classification error", "date": "2026-10-07", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Statistics Office has corrected two detailed tables accompanying the August consumer prices release, originally published on 17 September. The headline annual inflation rate remains 3.0 per cent, and the monthly increase remains 0.1 per cent. The error affected the allocation of some household repair charges between two service categories.

The corrected annual rate for household maintenance services is 4.6 per cent, replacing 5.2 per cent. The rate for other domestic services is 3.8 per cent, replacing 3.1 per cent. Both categories sit within the same broader spending group, whose index is unchanged. No individual price observations were altered.

Replacement tables carry a correction note and today's date. Earlier versions remain identifiable in the publication archive. Users who downloaded the detailed category tables should replace their copies. The office found the error during a routine classification review and has added a check comparing category assignments before publication."}

{"title": "September employment increases while unemployment rate remains unchanged", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Tovan Merrow", "body": "Employment in Kestria increased by an estimated 6,000 in September to 2,849,000. The seasonally adjusted unemployment rate remained at 5.5 per cent, with 166,000 people unemployed. The employment rate for people aged 16 to 64 returned to 73.1 per cent, the same rate recorded in July.

Health and social care, education and business support recorded employment gains. Manufacturing employment was broadly stable. More people entered the labour force during the month, allowing employment to rise without a fall in the unemployment rate. The labour force includes everyone who is employed or meets the survey definition of unemployment.

In the three months to September, employment averaged 2,846,000. This was 9,000 higher than in the three months to June. Regional estimates, including those for Saltmarsh and Val-Brume, are published as three-month averages because their smaller survey samples make single-month comparisons less dependable. All figures remain subject to sampling uncertainty."}

{"title": "July goods trade deficit narrows as exports increase", "date": "2026-09-15", "author": "Nessa Talvern", "body": "Kestria exported goods worth KRN 4.82 billion in July and imported goods worth KRN 5.36 billion. The resulting goods trade deficit was KRN 540 million, compared with KRN 620 million in June. On a seasonally adjusted basis, export values rose by 2.1 per cent and import values rose by 0.4 per cent.

Processed food and electrical equipment accounted for much of the export increase. Machinery imports fell, partly offsetting higher imports of fuel and household goods. Shipments recorded through Kestrel Bay increased, although a port of departure does not necessarily identify where goods were made.

These figures measure the value of goods crossing Kestria's border. They do not include trade in services and are not adjusted for price changes. The Kestria Statistics Office has revised the June deficit from KRN 650 million to KRN 620 million after receiving corrected declarations."}

{"title": "Quarterly trade tables show growth in services exports", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Nessa Talvern", "body": "Kestria exported services worth KRN 3.24 billion in the second quarter of 2026, an increase of 4.2 per cent from the first quarter. Services imports rose by 2.0 per cent to KRN 2.61 billion. The resulting services surplus was KRN 630 million, according to quarterly tables published today.

Business support and remote technical services accounted for most of the export increase. Transport services also grew, with higher receipts reported by operators based in Kestrel Bay. Travel spending by visitors increased modestly. The figures are seasonally adjusted and measured at current prices, so growth can reflect both higher prices and increased activity.

Services trade is measured mainly through business surveys and administrative records, rather than the border declarations used for goods. It is published later because the necessary information takes longer to collect. These second-quarter figures should not be combined directly with July or August goods figures to calculate a monthly overall trade balance."}

{"title": "September inflation falls as food price growth slows", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "Annual consumer price inflation in Kestria fell to 2.7 per cent in September, from 3.0 per cent in August. Prices were unchanged overall between August and September. This means that increases in some parts of the household basket were offset by falls in others, rather than every price remaining the same.

Food prices were 3.2 per cent higher than a year earlier, down from 3.7 per cent in August. Lower seasonal prices for fruit and vegetables contributed to the change. Passenger transport prices also fell over the month. Rents rose by 0.4 per cent and continued to put upward pressure on the overall index.

The annual inflation rate has eased from 3.2 per cent in July, but the overall price level remains above a year earlier. September calculations use the corrected service classifications described in the office's 7 October notice. That correction did not change the August headline index or the comparison reported today."}

{"title": "Payroll corrections guide explains how to fix common errors", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service issued a payroll corrections guide today after reviewing 1,120 amended returns received in August. Almost half involved wages entered in the wrong reporting month. The guide explains how employers can correct a submitted return without creating a second charge for the same wages.

Employers should open the original return, select the correction option and enter the full replacement figures for the affected month. They should not enter only the difference between the old and correct amounts. Each accepted correction produces a new receipt linked to the original filing.

For example, an Amberford employer that reported taxable wages of KRN 42,000 instead of KRN 24,000 should replace the wages figure with KRN 24,000. Any resulting credit appears on the employer’s account after processing. The guide is available in English and French, with printed copies at the Val-Brume revenue office. Employers should keep records explaining why each correction was made."}

{"title": "Household spending survey begins recruitment across five cities", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Statistics Office has begun recruiting households for its next annual spending survey. The first invitations cover selected addresses in Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume. The full sample contains 8,400 addresses across urban and rural areas. Households are selected by chance so that different living arrangements are represented.

Participants are asked about regular bills and larger purchases, then keep a spending diary for fourteen days. The survey records expenditure on items such as food, transport, housing and leisure. Its results help update the spending weights used in consumer price statistics and describe how household budgets vary.

Selected households can complete the diary on paper or through the office's survey website. French materials are available for participants in Val-Brume and elsewhere. Published results combine responses into statistical groups; they do not identify individual households. Interviewers carry an identification number that households can check with the office before taking part."}

{"title": "Payroll filing service restored after morning access problems", "date": "2026-09-10", "author": null, "body": "Access to the Kestria Revenue Service payroll filing service was restored at 11.40 am today following a technical fault. The interruption began at 8.15 am and affected employers trying to open or submit payroll tax returns. Corporate tax and sales tax filing remained available.

The fault interrupted the connection between the sign-in page and payroll records. Technical checks found no evidence that employer or employee information had been lost or disclosed. Returns submitted before the interruption remain recorded, and employers do not need to send those returns again.

Employers who saw an error message during submission should check their filing history for a receipt number. If no receipt appears, they should reopen the saved return and submit it. The service logged 186 reports from employers, including businesses in Amberford and Kestrel Bay. Anyone still unable to file should contact the payroll support team and quote the message shown on screen."}

{"title": "Sales tax registration threshold rises to support smaller businesses", "date": "2026-08-27", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service today published a sales tax rule raising the compulsory registration threshold from KRN 150,000 to KRN 180,000. The change takes effect on 1 October and applies to taxable sales measured over a rolling 12-month period. The sales tax rate remains 6 per cent.

Businesses already registered must continue collecting and reporting sales tax unless the service approves their removal from the register. A business with taxable sales below the new threshold may apply to leave, but should first check whether tax adjustments are required for stock or equipment it still holds.

An explanatory notice includes worked examples for shops, repair businesses and seasonal traders. Printed copies are available at revenue offices in Kestrel Bay, Ostara and Val-Brume. The service estimates that 2,300 registered businesses have annual taxable sales between the old and new thresholds. Eligibility depends on each business’s records, rather than its size or number of employees."}

{"title": "Revenue Service opens additional help desks for small employers", "date": "2026-08-24", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service opened additional payroll tax help desks today in Saltmarsh and Amberford. The desks support employers completing their first return or correcting employee details. Each location has four advisers, with appointments available between 9 am and 4 pm.

Employers should bring their business registration number, payroll totals and records of tax already paid. Advisers can explain how to complete a return and check whether the required sections are filled in. They cannot prepare wage records on an employer’s behalf or accept payments in cash.

The service recorded 640 requests for first-return support during the first three weeks of August. Missing employee reference numbers were the most common problem. Employers outside the two cities can receive the same guidance by telephone, including assistance in French through the Val-Brume support team. There is no charge for either service."}

{"title": "Revenue Service reports progress on sales tax refunds", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service processed 92 per cent of complete sales tax refund claims within 15 working days during September. This compares with 86 per cent in August. September payments totalled KRN 18.4 million across 1,760 claims, including claims from manufacturers, wholesalers and building contractors.

The improvement followed the reassignment of six processing staff to the refund team in Ostara. Claims requiring further checks are excluded from the 15-day measure while the service waits for requested information. The published figures include both the number of claims awaiting checks and the reasons for those checks.

At the end of September, 214 claims were awaiting invoices, bank account confirmation or an explanation of unusually large purchases. Businesses can check progress through their tax account. Sending the same claim again can delay processing because staff must establish whether it is a duplicate. A business should instead reply to the information request using its existing claim reference."}

{"title": "Higher sales tax threshold now applies across Kestria", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": null, "body": "The higher compulsory sales tax registration threshold of KRN 180,000 is now in force, the Kestria Revenue Service confirmed today. It took effect on 1 October, replacing the KRN 150,000 threshold. Businesses must measure their taxable sales over a rolling 12-month period when checking whether registration is required.

By the close of business on 2 October, the service had received 486 applications to leave the sales tax register. Of these, 312 had been approved and 174 remained under review. Each approved business received a notice stating the date from which it must stop charging sales tax.

The service reminded traders in Saltmarsh, Val-Brume and other communities that applying does not immediately end their obligations. Registered businesses must keep collecting tax and filing returns until their approved removal date. The sales tax rate remains 6 per cent. Customers can ask suppliers for a corrected invoice if tax has been charged after a supplier’s registration ended."}

{"title": "Val-Brume revenue office introduces French payroll support appointments", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service introduced dedicated French-language payroll support appointments at its Val-Brume office today. The office now offers 24 appointments each week for employers who need help understanding returns, account balances or requests for information. Telephone support in French remains available.

The appointment service follows a local trial held during September, when advisers assisted 68 employers. Most enquiries concerned corrections to monthly wages and matching payments to the correct reporting period. Appointments last 30 minutes and can be booked by telephone or through a business tax account.

Employers should bring the relevant return, wage records and any letter they want to discuss. A representative attending for a business must have authority recorded on its tax account before staff can discuss private information. General guidance is available without access to an account. The service does not charge for appointments, and employers do not need to use a paid intermediary to book one."}

{"title": "Employers receive clarification on payroll tax for travel payments", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Revenue Service published a clarification today on payroll tax treatment of employee travel payments. It explains the existing distinction between reimbursing documented business travel and paying a fixed travel allowance. The clarification does not change the payroll tax rate or introduce a new charge.

A reimbursement matching a supported business travel expense can be excluded from taxable wages. A fixed allowance paid regardless of actual costs must be included. Ordinary travel between home and an employee’s usual workplace does not qualify as business travel under these rules.

For example, an Ostara repair firm may reimburse KRN 86 for an employee’s documented journey to a customer in Amberford without adding that reimbursement to taxable wages. A separate KRN 200 monthly travel allowance paid without expense records must be included. Employers should retain the journey’s purpose, date and supporting receipts. The guidance answers 143 travel-related enquiries received by the payroll support team during September."}

{"title": "Corrected Amberford Heatworks report satisfies Commission disclosure direction", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Selin Arvane", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has completed its review of the corrected half-year report published by Amberford Heatworks on 8 September. The revised report satisfies the disclosure direction announced on 25 August concerning a loan condition linked to the company's minimum cash reserves.

The corrected report explains the KRN 6 million cash threshold, how the lender measures compliance and when repayment could be demanded. It also sets out the company's cash position at 30 June and the steps management would consider if reserves approached the threshold. The company published the required separate notice explaining the corrections.

The Commission is closing its review of compliance with this direction. This does not amount to an assessment of the company's financial strength or a recommendation to buy its bonds. The case underlines the need to explain borrowing conditions alongside headline debt figures. Issuers should check that investors can understand both when obligations fall due and what could cause payment dates to change."}

{"title": "Commission reminds issuers of approaching annual reporting consultation deadline", "date": "2026-09-25", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission is reminding interested parties that its consultation on clearer annual reporting closes on 29 September. The consultation, opened on 18 August, covers explanations of funding risks, major customer losses and changes to accounting estimates in listed company reports.

The Commission has received 26 submissions so far, including responses from individual investors and smaller listed companies. Several respondents have asked how much detail would be expected when a company depends on one large customer. The consultation paper includes questions on this issue, and further views are welcome.

Respondents may answer any of the questions and do not need to comment on the whole paper. Submissions can be made in English or French. Anyone requesting confidential treatment should identify the relevant passages and explain why confidentiality is needed. The Commission will consider the evidence before deciding its next steps. Existing reporting requirements continue to apply while the proposals are under review."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay broker fined for trading ahead of client orders", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has imposed a KRN 780,000 penalty on Kestrel Bay broker Velmora Securities for using confidential client order information when trading for its own account. The firm admitted the breaches and agreed to appoint an independent reviewer of its trading controls.

Between February and May, a trading desk bought shares before executing 23 large client purchase orders. It then sold those shares after the client purchases had pushed prices higher. The conduct generated KRN 164,000 in profits. The firm must return that amount to affected clients in addition to paying the penalty.

The investigation found that staff could view pending client orders while placing trades for the firm's account, without effective checks. Velmora Securities has restricted that access. Its independent reviewer must assess the changes and report to the Commission within 90 days. The firm must also provide clients with a written explanation of how individual repayment amounts were calculated."}

{"title": "Commission publishes findings from review of market announcement timing", "date": "2026-09-07", "author": "Selin Arvane", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has completed a review of how quickly listed companies publish information that could affect their securities prices. Staff examined 63 announcements issued between January and June, including notices about cancelled contracts, financing agreements and significant changes to expected earnings.

In 11 cases, companies could not provide a clear record of when senior staff first received the information. Five companies relied on a single person to approve announcements, with no cover during absences. The Commission has written to those companies requesting improvements to their procedures.

The findings do not establish that all 11 companies disclosed information late. However, incomplete records make it harder to demonstrate compliance. Companies should record key decisions, identify an alternative approver and keep disclosure arrangements under review. A briefing note published today includes practical examples. The Commission will assess the responses before deciding whether any individual case requires further investigation."}

{"title": "Ostara workshop will explain bond disclosure duties to smaller issuers", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Lucien Marcelet", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission will hold a free workshop in Ostara on 22 October for smaller companies that issue bonds to the public. The session will explain continuing disclosure duties, including how to report borrowing conditions, missed payments and material changes in available funding.

There are 60 places available, with registration closing on 19 October unless the session fills earlier. Finance staff, company secretaries and directors may attend. Commission staff will use fictional examples involving a KRN 15 million bond issue and a loan approaching its repayment date. Attendance does not satisfy any formal reporting requirement.

Participants may submit general questions when registering. Staff cannot give advance approval to company announcements or discuss confidential supervisory cases at the workshop. Written materials will be available in English and French, and a French-language online session will be arranged separately. The Commission will publish the presentation materials so that issuers unable to attend can use the same guidance."}

{"title": "Amberford bond issuer ordered to correct missing debt disclosures", "date": "2026-08-25", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has directed Amberford Heatworks to correct its latest half-year report after the bond issuer omitted information about a loan condition. The condition allows a lender to demand repayment if the company's cash reserves fall below KRN 6 million.

The company reported cash reserves of KRN 7.4 million at 30 June but did not explain the condition or the consequences of breaching it. The Commission considers this information necessary for investors assessing the company's ability to meet payments on its KRN 42 million bond issue.

Amberford Heatworks must publish a corrected report by 8 September and explain the changes in a separate market notice. The direction does not require early repayment of the bonds. No financial penalty has been imposed. The Commission will review the corrected report and consider whether further action is needed. Bond issuers remain responsible for explaining significant borrowing conditions in plain language."}

{"title": "Commission opens consultation on clearer reporting by listed companies", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission today opened a consultation on proposed changes to annual reporting rules for listed companies. The proposals would require clearer explanations of cash shortages, major customer losses and changes to accounting estimates. Responses must reach the Commission by 29 September 2026.

A review of 48 annual reports found that 17 companies used broad statements that gave investors little information about their main risks. Nine reports described available funding without explaining when loans fell due. The proposed rules would require companies to connect these figures with their plans for meeting payments.

The consultation also asks whether smaller issuers should receive additional guidance and a longer preparation period. Investors, companies, auditors and other interested parties may submit written comments. The consultation paper is available in English and French. The Commission has not decided whether to adopt the proposals and will publish a summary of responses."}

{"title": "Annual reporting consultation closes with forty three written submissions", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Mara Vennick", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission received 43 written submissions to its consultation on clearer annual reporting, which closed on 29 September. Non-confidential responses are now available in the consultation register. Staff are reviewing the evidence, and no decision has been made on changes to the rules.

The submissions came from 16 listed companies, 12 individual investors, seven audit practices and eight other respondents. Common topics included the cost of preparing additional explanations, the treatment of commercially sensitive customer information and the time smaller companies would need to update reporting procedures.

Several investors requested clearer links between borrowing figures and repayment dates. Some companies favoured practical guidance alongside any new requirements. The Commission will assess these views together rather than treating the number of responses supporting a proposal as a vote. A response paper will explain the conclusions and any proposed implementation arrangements. Companies should continue to prepare reports under the existing rules."}

{"title": "Commission seeks court order over suspected false revenue statements", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has applied to the securities court in Saltmarsh for an order requiring Ordelune Storage to preserve accounting records and related correspondence. The application forms part of an investigation into suspected false revenue statements in the listed company's latest annual report.

The Commission is examining whether KRN 18.6 million recorded as storage service revenue was supported by completed work. Its application concerns records held by the company and two former executives. The Commission has asked for an expedited hearing because some relevant electronic records are subject to routine deletion.

The allegations have not been determined by the court. The application does not seek a finding of liability or a financial penalty at this stage. Ordelune Storage and the former executives will have an opportunity to respond. The Commission will publish a further update when the court decides the application. Investors should consult the company's market announcements for its statements on the matter."}

{"title": "Val-Brume review finds gaps in French bond disclosure documents", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Lucien Marcelet", "body": "The Kestria Securities Commission has asked six bond issuers to correct French disclosure documents following a review in Val-Brume. Staff examined 28 documents made available to investors in the French-speaking region and compared key payment terms with the corresponding English versions.

Four documents omitted wording about an issuer's right to repay bonds early. Two others gave an incorrect deadline for bondholders to submit payment instructions. The affected issues have a combined face value of KRN 96 million. The review did not identify any payment already missed because of these errors.

The six issuers must publish corrected documents and notify affected holders by 30 October. The Commission has also requested evidence of checks by staff with suitable language and financial knowledge. Issuers remain responsible for the accuracy of documents prepared by outside translators. Investors who believe a discrepancy has affected a payment should contact the issuer and may also report the matter to the Commission."}

{"title": "Court orders preservation of records in Ordelune Storage investigation", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Dorian Pellac", "body": "The securities court in Saltmarsh today ordered Ordelune Storage and two former executives to preserve records relevant to the Kestria Securities Commission's investigation into suspected false revenue statements. The order follows the Commission's application announced on 13 October and takes effect immediately.

The order covers accounting entries, customer contracts, invoices and related correspondence concerning KRN 18.6 million in reported storage service revenue. Those subject to it must suspend routine deletion of relevant electronic material and retain paper records. They must provide written confirmation of the preservation steps taken within seven days.

The court has not decided whether the revenue statements were false or whether any person breached securities law. The Commission's investigation continues, and the company and former executives retain the right to respond to any allegations. Preserving the records will allow the evidence to be examined. The Commission will announce any further public proceedings when they are filed."}

{"title": "Ostara Homes Outlook Turns Negative as Sales Slow and Debt Rises", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": "Tomas Belwick", "body": "Meridian Ratings has revised the outlook on Ostara Homes to negative from stable and affirmed its BBB minus long-term issuer rating. Slower reservations and higher spending on land have increased borrowing. The company builds mid-priced homes in Ostara, Amberford and the outer districts of Saltmarsh.

Net reservations fell 14% in the eight months to August compared with the same period last year. Completed but unsold homes increased to 246 from 171 at December. Net debt reached KRN 392 million, equivalent to 3.7 times operating profit before depreciation over the preceding 12 months.

Cash and undrawn committed facilities total KRN 168 million, against KRN 61 million of debt due within a year. This supports the rating despite weaker trading. Meridian could lower the rating if inventory continues to build or net debt remains above four times operating profit before depreciation for a sustained period."}

{"title": "Meridian Rates Saltmarsh Waterworks Bond in Line With Issuer Rating", "date": "2026-09-10", "author": "Mara Ternell", "body": "Meridian Ratings has assigned an A minus rating to Saltmarsh Waterworks’ proposed KRN 180 million senior unsecured bond. The issue rating matches the company’s long-term issuer rating, which remains A minus with a stable outlook. The bond is expected to mature in September 2033.

The company intends to use KRN 120 million of the proceeds to repay a bank loan due in February 2027. The balance will fund pipe replacement and treatment equipment within its existing investment programme. The transaction would extend debt maturities while leaving total borrowing within Meridian’s previous forecasts.

The proposed bonds will rank equally with the company’s other senior unsecured obligations. Meridian’s assessment assumes that the final terms are consistent with the draft documents reviewed. Cost overruns or a material increase in borrowing beyond the current programme could weaken financial headroom, but neither is included in the base forecast."}

{"title": "Meridian Assigns BBB Rating to Amberford Packaging With Stable Outlook", "date": "2026-08-25", "author": "Tomas Belwick", "body": "Meridian Ratings has assigned Amberford Packaging a first-time long-term issuer rating of BBB with a stable outlook. The company produces paper-based food containers and industrial wrapping at two plants in Amberford. Its established customer relationships support earnings, although exposure to pulp prices limits the rating.

Amberford Packaging reported revenue of KRN 762 million and operating profit before depreciation of KRN 126 million for the year to June. Its five largest customers accounted for 38% of sales. Contracts covering roughly two-thirds of revenue allow material cost increases to be passed through within three months.

The company plans to spend KRN 74 million on a replacement production line over the next year. Meridian expects net debt to remain below 2.8 times operating profit before depreciation. The stable outlook assumes the project stays within budget and customer demand remains broadly consistent with current orders."}

{"title": "Meridian Upgrades Kestrel Bay Savings After Stronger Capital and Lower Arrears", "date": "2026-09-03", "author": "Elian Voss", "body": "Meridian Ratings has upgraded Kestrel Bay Savings’ long-term issuer rating to A minus from BBB plus. The outlook is stable. The upgrade reflects a sustained improvement in capital and loan quality, supported by retained earnings and a reduction in lending to speculative property developments.

The bank’s core capital ratio reached 15.4% at the end of June, compared with 13.6% a year earlier. Loans more than 90 days overdue declined to 1.2% of gross lending from 2.0%. Customer deposits funded 86% of loans, limiting reliance on borrowing from financial markets.

These improvements give the bank greater capacity to withstand the margin pressure affecting Kestrian regional lenders. Meridian nevertheless expects earnings growth to slow as savings rates rise. The stable outlook assumes that management maintains its current lending standards and does not materially increase distributions to shareholders."}

{"title": "Kestrian Regional Banks Face Narrower Margins Despite Steady Loan Demand", "date": "2026-08-24", "author": "Elian Voss", "body": "Meridian Ratings expects profitability at Kestria’s regional banks to weaken modestly during the remainder of 2026 as deposit costs rise. Its review of eight rated lenders found that average net interest margins fell to 2.7% in June from 2.9% in December. The sector outlook remains stable.

Loans grew by 3.6% over the same period, led by small business borrowing in Ostara and residential lending around Saltmarsh. Customer deposits increased by 2.8%. Banks have offered higher rates on fixed-term accounts to retain savers, reducing the benefit of growth in interest income.

Arrears remain manageable, with loans more than 90 days overdue representing 1.8% of the reviewed portfolios. Construction borrowers account for much of the recent increase. Meridian expects lenders to maintain current capital buffers, but banks with concentrated property exposure have less room to absorb unexpected losses."}

{"title": "Meridian Ratings Affirms Saltmarsh Waterworks at A Minus With Stable Outlook", "date": "2026-08-17", "author": "Mara Ternell", "body": "Meridian Ratings has affirmed Saltmarsh Waterworks’ long-term issuer rating at A minus with a stable outlook. The rating reflects steady demand, predictable regulated income and adequate funding for repairs. The company supplies drinking water to 284,000 households and businesses in and around Kestria’s capital.

Revenue reached KRN 418 million in the year to June, up 4.8% from the previous year. Cash generated from operations covered interest payments 5.2 times. Water lost through leaks fell to 18.6% from 20.1%, although older pipes in the eastern districts remain a significant maintenance cost.

The stable outlook assumes annual capital spending of about KRN 110 million and no substantial debt-funded expansion. Available cash and committed borrowing facilities cover debt falling due over the next 18 months. A sustained increase in debt without matching earnings growth could put pressure on the rating."}

{"title": "Kestrian Food Producers Maintain Stable Outlook as Input Costs Ease", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Céline Arvane", "body": "Meridian Ratings has maintained a stable outlook for Kestria’s packaged food sector. Lower grain and container costs are helping producers rebuild margins, although household spending remains subdued. Across six rated companies, average operating margins increased to 11.2% in the first half of 2026 from 10.4% a year earlier.

Sales volumes rose just 0.8%, with stronger demand for basic products offset by weaker purchases of premium lines. Producers supplying retailers in Saltmarsh and Amberford reported increased use of promotions. Meridian expects price competition to limit how much of the recent cost relief companies retain.

The sector’s median net debt stood at 2.4 times operating profit before depreciation at June. Most rated producers have sufficient cash and committed facilities for planned investment over the next year. Debt-funded acquisitions or prolonged discounting would create greater pressure than the modest volume growth currently assumed in Meridian’s forecasts."}

{"title": "Meridian Places Bracken Freight Under Review Following Proposed Harbour Acquisition", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Tomas Belwick", "body": "Meridian Ratings has placed Bracken Freight’s BBB long-term issuer rating under review for downgrade after the company announced an agreement to acquire Kestrel Bay Harbour Services. The proposed KRN 460 million purchase would expand Bracken’s storage and cargo handling operations but could materially increase debt.

Bracken has disclosed a committed bridge loan covering the full purchase price. It is also considering an equity issue, although the amount and timing have not been confirmed. Based on debt-only funding, Meridian estimates that net debt could exceed four times annual operating profit before depreciation immediately after completion.

The review will examine the final funding mix, integration costs and the acquired business’s cash generation. Bracken expects completion before the end of October, subject to the remaining transaction conditions. Meridian will resolve the review once it has sufficient evidence to assess the combined company’s financial position."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Heating Retains BBB Plus Rating Ahead of Winter Demand", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Céline Arvane", "body": "Meridian Ratings has affirmed Val-Brume Heating’s BBB plus long-term issuer rating with a stable outlook. The company provides district heating to 42,000 properties in Kestria’s French-speaking region. Its established network and contracted customer base support the rating, while seasonal cash needs and fuel costs remain constraints.

Fuel stocks at the end of September covered approximately 11 weeks of expected winter consumption. Fixed-price supply contracts cover 76% of forecast purchases through March 2027. The company also has an undrawn KRN 65 million revolving facility available to meet short-term working capital requirements.

Meridian expects operating cash flow to cover interest payments at least four times over the coming year. The forecast allows for routine maintenance and a KRN 28 million boiler replacement. An unusually prolonged cold period would increase purchases, but existing liquidity provides a reasonable buffer against that pressure."}

{"title": "Amberford Packaging Reports Production Delay but Retains Financial Headroom", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Tomas Belwick", "body": "Meridian Ratings says a delay to Amberford Packaging’s replacement production line does not currently affect its BBB issuer rating or stable outlook. The company now expects commissioning in February 2027, two months later than planned, following a fault identified during equipment testing at the supplier’s workshop.

The project budget has risen to KRN 81 million from KRN 74 million. Amberford expects to keep its existing line operating until the replacement is ready, allowing it to fulfil contracted orders. The delay will defer expected efficiency savings and add approximately KRN 3 million in temporary maintenance costs.

Meridian’s revised forecast puts peak net debt at 2.9 times operating profit before depreciation, compared with less than 2.8 times previously. Existing liquidity can absorb the additional spending. Further delays, lost orders or substantially higher costs could reduce the headroom supporting the stable outlook and prompt a rating review."}

{"title": "Meridian Downgrades Ostara Homes as Cancellations Increase and Liquidity Weakens", "date": "2026-10-23", "author": "Céline Arvane", "body": "Meridian Ratings has downgraded Ostara Homes’ long-term issuer rating to BB plus from BBB minus. The outlook is negative. The downgrade follows a sharp increase in cancellations and a reduction in available borrowing facilities, which weaken the company’s ability to carry its growing stock of unsold homes.

Figures supplied on 21 October show that completed but unsold homes reached 289 at September, compared with 246 at August. Cancellations accounted for 27% of gross reservations during September. Cash and undrawn committed facilities fell to KRN 112 million after a lender declined to renew a KRN 40 million facility.

Ostara Homes has suspended new land purchases and plans selective discounts to release cash from finished properties. These steps should help liquidity, but lower selling prices will put further pressure on earnings. Meridian could lower the rating again if available funding falls materially or sales fail to cover construction spending and scheduled debt payments."}

{"title": "Meridian Publishes Updated Criteria for Rating Kestrian District Heating Providers", "date": "2026-10-12", "author": "Mara Ternell", "body": "Meridian Ratings has published updated criteria for assessing district heating providers in Kestria. The criteria take effect today and apply to new ratings and future reviews. They give more detailed guidance on fuel procurement, seasonal liquidity and the financial effect of delays in recovering costs through customer tariffs.

The update follows a consultation that closed on 18 September. Meridian received nine written responses from rated companies, investors and other market participants. The final text clarifies how analysts assess committed credit facilities and distinguishes fuel already held in storage from purchases covered by future supply contracts.

Meridian has assessed its existing district heating ratings against the revised criteria and identified no immediate rating changes. The update does not alter the rating or outlook on Val-Brume Heating affirmed on 5 October. Individual reviews will continue to consider network condition, customer concentration and each provider’s ability to absorb unexpected costs."}

{"title": "Meridian Affirms Fenmere Commercial Bank as Property Lending Remains Concentrated", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Elian Voss", "body": "Meridian Ratings has affirmed Fenmere Commercial Bank’s BBB long-term issuer rating with a stable outlook. The Saltmarsh-based lender benefits from steady business deposits and adequate capital. Its relatively large exposure to commercial property continues to constrain the rating, particularly lending secured on smaller office buildings.

Commercial property represented 31% of gross loans at the end of September, down from 34% a year earlier. Loans more than 90 days overdue rose to 2.5% from 2.1% over that period. Additional provisions reduced nine-month profit to KRN 47 million, 12% below the comparable period.

The bank’s core capital ratio of 14.2% provides capacity to absorb further losses within Meridian’s base forecast. Deposits increased by 3.1% during the first nine months, supporting liquidity. The stable outlook assumes gradual reductions in property concentration and no sharp deterioration among the bank’s largest borrowers."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Packaging Downgraded to BB+ Following Weaker Cash Generation", "date": "2026-09-24", "author": "Elise Dorne and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has lowered Val-Brume Packaging's senior unsecured bond rating to BB+ from BBB-. The outlook is stable. The producer of cartons and food containers has reported weaker earnings and a larger working capital requirement than Northstar assumed in its previous review.

Operating profit before depreciation fell 22% to KRN 41 million in the year ended July, while debt increased to KRN 205 million. Higher paper input costs and slower customer payments contributed to the deterioration. Debt reached 5.0 times operating profit, compared with 3.6 times a year earlier. The company serves food producers across Kestria from its principal factory in Val-Brume.

Cash of KRN 19 million and an undrawn committed facility of KRN 35 million provide adequate near-term funding. No bond principal is due before 2029. The stable outlook assumes tighter stock control and lower investment spending prevent further debt growth. Another downgrade could follow if cash generation remains negative or available funding falls materially."}

{"title": "Meridian Confirms Bracken Freight Rating After Equity Funding Reduces Acquisition Debt", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Tomas Belwick", "body": "Meridian Ratings has confirmed Bracken Freight’s BBB long-term issuer rating with a stable outlook and removed it from review for downgrade. The action follows completion of its KRN 460 million acquisition of Kestrel Bay Harbour Services and confirmation of a larger equity contribution than previously disclosed.

Bracken raised KRN 250 million through an equity issue completed on 19 October and funded the remaining purchase price through its bridge facility. The acquisition completed today. Meridian estimates opening net debt at 2.8 times combined annual operating profit before depreciation, materially below the debt-only case considered on 8 October.

The stable outlook allows for KRN 22 million of integration spending over the next 12 months and assumes no further large acquisitions. Bracken plans to replace the bridge borrowing with longer-term funding within six months. Delayed refinancing or weaker cargo volumes could reduce rating headroom, although current cash generation supports the confirmed rating."}

{"title": "Amberford Water Receives A- Rating for Network Renewal Bond", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Mara Vellin and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has assigned an A- rating to Amberford Water's proposed KRN 230 million senior unsecured bond, with a stable outlook. The company plans to use KRN 150 million to repay existing debt and the remainder to replace ageing mains and pumping equipment. The rating is subject to final documents matching the terms reviewed.

Amberford Water supplies 340,000 households and businesses under a licence that links permitted revenue to operating costs and approved investment. Collections remained strong at 98.6% of billed revenue in the year ended June. Water losses declined to 17.2% from 19.1% following repairs to several older sections of the network.

Northstar expects debt to remain close to 4.0 times annual operating profit before depreciation during the renewal programme. The stable outlook assumes spending stays within the approved budget and service performance supports timely cost recovery. Persistent overspending or debt above 4.8 times operating profit could put downward pressure on the rating."}

{"title": "Northstar Opens Consultation on Proposed Corporate Liquidity Assessment Changes", "date": "2026-09-10", "author": "Nalin Verdan and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has opened a consultation on proposed changes to its corporate liquidity methodology. The draft explains how analysts would assess seasonal cash needs, restrictions on moving cash between subsidiaries and borrowing facilities that depend on financial conditions. Written responses are invited until 2 October.

The proposal would require a separate assessment of the weakest three-month cash position within the following year. Current analysis places greater emphasis on the full twelve-month period. The draft also clarifies that an uncommitted overdraft would not count as an assured funding source, even when an issuer has used it regularly.

An initial review of 24 rated Kestrian corporate groups suggests that five could receive weaker liquidity assessments under the draft. This would not automatically change their ratings, which also reflect business strength and debt levels. The existing methodology remains in force during consultation. Northstar will publish its conclusions after reviewing responses and completing further testing."}

{"title": "Estuary Freight Outlook Turns Negative as Bond Refinancing Remains Unresolved", "date": "2026-09-04", "author": "Tomas Arven and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has affirmed Estuary Freight's BBB- senior unsecured bond rating and revised the outlook to negative from stable. The Kestrel Bay cargo handler has KRN 160 million of bonds falling due in February 2027. Discussions with lenders are continuing, but the company has not secured replacement funding.

At 31 July, Estuary Freight held KRN 38 million in unrestricted cash and had KRN 25 million available under committed facilities. Cargo volumes fell 6% in the first seven months of the year, mainly because of lower building material shipments. Northstar estimates debt at 4.4 times annual operating profit before depreciation, against 3.8 times at the end of 2025.

The negative outlook reflects the narrowing time available to refinance. Northstar could lower the rating if funding arrangements remain incomplete or operating performance weakens further. A return to a stable outlook would require sufficient committed funding to repay the bonds and evidence that cargo earnings have steadied."}

{"title": "Bracken Grid Bond Rating Holds After Amberford Storm Disruption", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Tomas Arven and Mara Vellin", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has maintained Bracken Grid's BBB senior unsecured bond rating and stable outlook following the 6 October storm in the Amberford district. The storm damaged overhead lines and interrupted supplies to 74,000 connections. By the evening of 8 October, the company had restored power to all but 1,900 affected connections.

Bracken Grid's preliminary estimate places repair costs at KRN 18 million to KRN 24 million. Northstar has assumed the full upper estimate in its initial assessment without relying on insurance receipts. The company had KRN 72 million in cash and KRN 110 million of undrawn committed facilities at the end of September.

These resources should allow repairs to proceed without materially weakening liquidity. The rating also allows for occasional weather-related disruption. Northstar will review the final repair bill, compensation payments and any changes to planned maintenance. Costs substantially above the current estimate, or evidence of wider network weaknesses, could alter the assessment."}

{"title": "Kestrian Food Producers Face Narrower Margins as Packaging Costs Rise", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Elise Dorne and Nalin Verdan", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has revised its outlook for Kestrian packaged food producers to negative from stable. The change reflects higher packaging and distribution costs, together with limited scope to raise selling prices. It is a sector assessment and does not change any individual bond rating.

Across the agency's sample of seven fictional issuers, packaging costs increased by an average of 11% in the first half of 2026, while selling prices rose 3%. Northstar expects the sample's operating margin before depreciation to fall to roughly 9% this year from 10.4% in 2025. Smaller producers supplying shops around Ostara have less purchasing power and face the greatest pressure.

Most rated producers retain adequate cash reserves and have no large bond repayments within twelve months. This should limit immediate rating pressure. The agency will focus on stock levels, customer payment times and whether cost savings protect cash generation. Companies that borrow to fund dividends while margins weaken could face individual rating reviews."}

{"title": "Ostara Rental Housing Outlook Remains Stable Despite Higher Maintenance Spending", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Mara Vellin and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has maintained a stable sector outlook for rated rental housing companies operating in Ostara. High occupancy and steady rent collection continue to support earnings, although repairs and borrowing costs are absorbing more cash. The assessment covers four issuers with a combined 28,400 homes.

Average occupancy stood at 97.4% at the end of August, compared with 97.1% a year earlier. Rent arrears remained modest at 1.8% of annual billed rent. Maintenance spending rose 8%, led by roof repairs and heating replacements. Fixed interest rates apply to 76% of the sample's debt, limiting the immediate effect of higher financing costs.

Northstar expects most issuers to fund routine repairs from rental income while keeping development programmes within committed borrowing limits. Ratings could come under pressure where large construction projects coincide with debt maturities. The stable sector outlook assumes occupancy remains above 96% and companies retain enough accessible funding to meet twelve months of planned spending."}

{"title": "Northstar Affirms Bracken Grid Bonds at BBB with Stable Outlook", "date": "2026-08-17", "author": "Mara Vellin and Tomas Arven", "body": "SALTMarsh — Northstar Credit Analytics has affirmed its BBB rating on Bracken Grid's KRN 420 million senior unsecured bonds. The outlook remains stable. The electricity network operator serves 610,000 connections across Amberford and surrounding districts. Its regulated network income provides a predictable source of cash, although a substantial maintenance programme limits room for additional borrowing.

For the six months ended 30 June, revenue rose 4.2% to KRN 286 million and operating profit before depreciation reached KRN 112 million. Debt stood at 3.7 times annualised operating profit, compared with 3.9 times at December. Available cash and committed borrowing facilities covered the company's next twelve months of scheduled debt payments.

The stable outlook assumes annual capital spending of about KRN 95 million and no material acquisition. Northstar could lower the rating if debt remained above 4.5 times operating profit or network penalties materially reduced cash generation. A higher rating would require sustained debt reduction alongside reliable service performance."}

{"title": "Corporate Liquidity Consultation Closes with Twelve Responses Under Review", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Nalin Verdan and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics received twelve responses to its proposed corporate liquidity methodology changes before the consultation closed on 2 October. Submissions came from rated companies, bond investors and lending firms. Analysts are reviewing the comments alongside additional testing of the proposed three-month cash assessment.

Several respondents supported clearer treatment of restricted cash and uncommitted borrowing. Others asked how the agency would assess seasonal businesses whose lowest cash balance occurs shortly before a predictable collection period. Four submissions requested worked examples showing how financial conditions attached to borrowing facilities would affect available liquidity.

Northstar will consider these points before deciding whether to adopt the changes. No rating or liquidity assessment has changed as a result of the consultation, and the existing methodology remains applicable. The agency plans to publish a summary of the main comments with its final decision. Confidential financial information supplied by respondents will be excluded from that summary."}

{"title": "Estuary Freight Downgraded to BB as Refinancing Options Narrow", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Tomas Arven and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has lowered Estuary Freight's senior unsecured bond rating to BB from BBB- and concluded the review opened on 13 October. The outlook is negative. Further information supplied during the review shows that the company has limited readily available funding for its KRN 160 million February 2027 bond repayment.

Of the KRN 25 million previously reported as available under committed facilities, KRN 15 million expires in January 2027. Northstar therefore excludes that amount when assessing resources for the bond maturity. The proposed storage yard sale remains at an early stage, with no agreed buyer or binding timetable. September cargo earnings also fell below management's revised budget.

The downgrade principally reflects weaker refinancing prospects and reduced financial flexibility. The revised liquidity methodology published yesterday also informs the assessment of facility availability. The negative outlook indicates further downside if replacement funding remains unsecured. A stable outlook would require a credible, committed repayment plan and enough cash to continue ordinary operations."}

{"title": "Estuary Freight Placed Under Review After Refinancing Talks End", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Tomas Arven and Nalin Verdan", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has placed Estuary Freight's BBB- senior unsecured bond rating under review for downgrade. The action follows the end of negotiations with a proposed lead lender without a refinancing agreement. The company's KRN 160 million bond remains due in February 2027.

Estuary Freight informed Northstar that it is approaching other lenders and considering the sale of a storage yard near Kestrel Bay. Neither option currently provides committed funds. Unrestricted cash was KRN 31 million at 30 September, down from KRN 38 million at 31 July. Available committed borrowing remained KRN 25 million, and trading continued below the agency's earlier expectations.

The review will focus on the size, timing and conditions of alternative funding. Northstar expects to resolve it once sufficient information is available and may lower the rating by more than one grade. A property valuation or an expression of lender interest alone would not establish that the February repayment can be met."}

{"title": "Northstar Adopts Revised Liquidity Methodology with Seasonal Cash Guidance", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": "Nalin Verdan and Céline Maret", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has published its revised corporate liquidity methodology, effective today. The final document adds a review of the weakest three-month cash position within the following year and clarifies how analysts treat restricted cash, uncommitted borrowing and conditions attached to committed facilities.

Following consultation, the agency has added worked examples for seasonal businesses. Analysts may recognise expected customer receipts when supported by a consistent collection record, but must also test a reasonable delay. Cash held by a subsidiary will count only where it can legally and practically meet the obligations being assessed.

Northstar has also published an anonymised summary of the twelve consultation responses. It will apply the revised methodology to existing corporate ratings over the next three months and to new assignments immediately. Publication itself changes no ratings. Any resulting action will receive a separate release explaining the issuer's circumstances and the role of the revised assessment."}

{"title": "Kestrian Retail Bond Issuers Enter Winter with Uneven Cash Buffers", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Elise Dorne and Nalin Verdan", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has maintained a negative outlook for Kestrian discretionary retailers in its latest sector review. Households are limiting larger purchases, while retailers have already paid for much of their winter stock. The agency expects differences in cash reserves to matter more than modest changes in reported sales.

Among six rated issuers, stock values at the end of September were 9% higher than a year earlier, while sales for the preceding three months rose only 1%. Two chains operating mainly in Saltmarsh and Kestrel Bay increased promotional discounts to clear older goods. Across the sample, average operating margins before depreciation fell to 6.2% from 7.1%.

Four issuers have committed facilities extending beyond their seasonal borrowing peak. The remaining two rely more heavily on cash generated during winter trading. Northstar is testing slower stock sales and delayed supplier credit separately for each company. The sector outlook does not imply that every retailer will be downgraded, and no individual rating changes accompany this report."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Packaging Rating Affirmed After Investment Spending Is Reduced", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Céline Maret and Dario Selven", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has affirmed Val-Brume Packaging's BB+ senior unsecured bond rating and stable outlook after the company reduced its investment budget. Management has deferred a second printing line, cutting planned spending for the current financial year to KRN 18 million from KRN 34 million.

The decision preserves cash while the company addresses the working capital pressures behind its September downgrade. Unrestricted cash stood at KRN 22 million at the end of September, with KRN 35 million still available under its committed facility. Early collections from overdue customers helped the improvement, although one month of stronger receipts does not establish a sustained recovery.

Northstar continues to expect debt near 5.0 times annual operating profit before depreciation in the near term. The revised liquidity assessment supports the existing rating because available resources cover projected seasonal needs. An upgrade would require durable earnings recovery and lower debt. Renewed cash outflows, weaker facility access or substantial additional borrowing could lead to another downgrade."}

{"title": "Coppermere Components Upgraded to BBB After Sustained Debt Reduction", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Elise Dorne and Mara Vellin", "body": "Northstar Credit Analytics has raised Coppermere Components' senior unsecured bond rating to BBB from BBB-, with a stable outlook. The Amberford manufacturer supplies replacement parts for industrial pumps and ventilation equipment. The upgrade reflects sustained debt reduction, a broader customer base and improved cash collection.

Debt fell to KRN 126 million at the end of August from KRN 171 million eighteen months earlier. Over the same period, debt relative to annual operating profit before depreciation declined from 3.8 times to 2.6 times. The largest customer now accounts for 14% of revenue, compared with 23% two years ago. Replacement orders provide more consistent demand than sales tied to new construction.

Cash and committed facilities comfortably cover the company's lowest projected three-month funding position under Northstar's revised methodology. The stable outlook assumes moderate investment and no large debt-funded acquisition. A downgrade could follow if debt rises above 3.5 times operating profit for a sustained period or customer concentration increases materially."}

{"title": "Business reference rate falls by a further quarter point", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Mara Ellery", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank will lower its business reference rate from 6.25% to 6.00% on 21 September. Borrowers whose agreements track this rate will receive the reduction automatically. The change follows the quarter-point reduction that took effect on 24 August.

Together, the two changes reduce annual interest by KRN 5,000 on an unchanged KRN 1 million balance, before any fees and subject to the terms of the agreement. Fixed-rate borrowing is unaffected. Loans linked to a different reference rate will continue to follow the calculation set out in their contracts.

The bank reviewed its funding costs and lending commitments before making the decision. Updated repayment information will be sent where monthly payments change. Customers with interest-only facilities will see the new rate applied to the relevant days in their next interest calculation and can request a breakdown from their relationship team."}

{"title": "Equipment programme expands after strong demand from regional manufacturers", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": "Devran Holt", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has increased its equipment lending programme from KRN 40 million to KRN 60 million. Since applications opened on 24 August, the bank has approved KRN 26.4 million across 19 facilities. Of that amount, KRN 11.8 million has been drawn by borrowers.

Companies in Amberford and Ostara account for just over half the approved lending by value. Purchases include cutting machines, temperature-controlled storage and packing lines. Approval totals represent agreed facilities rather than equipment already installed, and borrowers draw funds as supplier invoices become due.

The additional allocation is available to new applications from today. Loan sizes remain between KRN 250,000 and KRN 4 million, with terms of three to seven years. Each application will receive a separate credit assessment. Businesses should obtain approval before relying on bank funding to place a binding equipment order."}

{"title": "Val-Brume lending desk adds appointments for French-speaking businesses", "date": "2026-09-03", "author": "Tomas Arlen", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has added twelve weekly appointments at its Val-Brume lending desk for businesses that prefer to discuss borrowing in French. The additional sessions are available on Tuesdays and Thursdays and can be held at the branch or by telephone.

Appointments cover working capital, equipment purchases and reviews of existing facilities. Businesses can request a document checklist in French before their meeting. The desk will also help customers identify which financial records are needed, although it cannot prepare accounts or provide independent legal advice.

There is no appointment charge and no obligation to apply for a loan. Lending decisions remain subject to the bank’s usual affordability and security checks. The expansion follows a six-week booking review in which the Val-Brume team recorded an average wait of nine working days for a French-language lending appointment."}

{"title": "Bank publishes revised fees for business lending administration", "date": "2026-09-02", "author": "Leonie Venn", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has published a revised schedule of business lending administration fees, taking effect on 5 October. The fee for a borrower-requested security amendment will fall from KRN 450 to KRN 325. Providing a duplicate annual interest certificate will become free, replacing the current KRN 35 charge.

The fee for reviewing a request to extend an overdraft temporarily will rise from KRN 175 to KRN 225. It covers the review and does not guarantee approval. Customers will be told whether a charge applies before they ask the bank to proceed with any chargeable work.

The changes do not alter agreed loan margins or repayment dates. Where an existing agreement fixes an administration fee, that agreement will continue to apply. The full schedule is available from relationship teams and on the bank’s website, with copies in French available for customers in Val-Brume."}

{"title": "New equipment lending programme opens with forty million crowns", "date": "2026-08-24", "author": "Devran Holt", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank today opened a KRN 40 million lending programme for mid-sized companies replacing production equipment. Businesses across Kestria can apply for loans of between KRN 250,000 and KRN 4 million, with repayment terms of three to seven years.

Eligible purchases include workshop machinery, commercial refrigeration and equipment used to sort or pack goods. Applicants must provide equipment quotations, their latest accounts and a cash-flow forecast. The bank will assess whether repayments remain affordable during quieter trading periods. Purchased equipment will normally form part of the security.

Both existing customers and companies new to the bank may apply. Interest rates and arrangement fees will be set for each borrower and disclosed before an agreement is signed. Applications are open through the bank’s lending teams, including its industrial business desks in Amberford and Kestrel Bay."}

{"title": "Revised administration fees take effect for business borrowers today", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Leonie Venn", "body": "The business lending administration fees announced by Harrow & Finch Bank on 2 September take effect today. A borrower-requested security amendment now costs KRN 325, down from KRN 450. Duplicate annual interest certificates are now free, removing the previous KRN 35 charge.

Reviewing a request for a temporary overdraft extension now costs KRN 225, an increase of KRN 50. The review fee does not depend on whether the request is approved. Staff must explain the applicable fee and obtain the customer’s agreement before starting a chargeable review.

Requests accepted before today will be charged under the previous schedule. Existing contracts that specify a fixed administration fee retain that fee. Customers can obtain the current schedule from any lending team or download it from the bank’s website. A French-language version is also available through the Val-Brume desk."}

{"title": "Ostara businesses offered working capital review appointments this month", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Tomas Arlen", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank is offering 30 working capital review appointments for mid-sized businesses at its Ostara branch between 12 and 30 October. The sessions are intended for companies preparing for seasonal changes in stock purchases, customer payments or supplier bills.

Each appointment lasts up to an hour and carries no charge. Businesses should bring a thirteen-week cash-flow forecast, a list of unpaid customer invoices and details of existing borrowing. A relationship manager will discuss the timing of expected receipts and payments and identify any information needed for a lending application.

Attendance does not require a company to move its banking arrangements. Any subsequent request for an overdraft or loan will be assessed separately, with rates and fees explained before acceptance. Telephone appointments are available for businesses outside Ostara that cannot attend the branch during the scheduled period."}

{"title": "Bank introduces clearer monthly statements for variable-rate business loans", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Leonie Venn", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has introduced a revised monthly statement layout for variable-rate business loans. Statements issued from today show the reference rate, the borrower’s agreed margin and the number of days charged at each rate in a single interest table.

Where a rate changes during the statement period, separate rows show the balance and calculation for each part of that period. This will help borrowers check interest following the bank’s reference rate reductions in August and September. Fees will continue to appear separately from interest.

The layout changes how information is presented and does not alter loan terms or payment dates. It was tested with 24 business customers from Saltmarsh, Amberford and Val-Brume. Large-print statements and an explanation in French are available on request. Customers who need help checking a calculation can contact the lending team named on their statement."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay lending team moves to larger customer premises", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Tomas Arlen", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank’s Kestrel Bay lending team will move to 18 Lantern Quay on 19 October. The new premises provide four private meeting rooms, compared with two at the current office, and a ground-floor entrance with step-free access from the pavement.

The office will serve mid-sized companies seeking lending advice and support with existing facilities. Customer telephone numbers and relationship managers will remain the same. Meetings already booked for 19 October onwards will take place at Lantern Quay, and the bank is contacting customers individually with directions.

The current office at 7 Tern Passage closes to visitors at 16:00 today. Telephone support will remain available during normal opening hours throughout the move. The new office opens at 09:00 on 19 October. It is an appointment-based lending office and does not provide cash deposits, withdrawals or counter payment services."}

{"title": "New borrower support team offers earlier repayment difficulty reviews", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Devran Holt", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank has established a dedicated borrower support team for business customers facing repayment difficulties. The five-person team is based in Saltmarsh and accepts referrals from relationship managers across Kestria. Customers can also ask their usual lending contact for a support review.

The team will examine recent trading, cash forecasts and existing repayment commitments. Depending on the circumstances, options may include changing payment dates or agreeing a temporary reduction in principal repayments. Any arrangement requires an assessment and written agreement. Interest may continue to accrue, and extending a loan can increase its total cost.

There is no charge for the initial support review. Customers are encouraged to make contact as soon as they expect a shortfall, even if every payment is currently up to date. French-language reviews are available through the Val-Brume lending desk. Requesting a review does not itself change contractual payment obligations."}

{"title": "Harrow & Finch Bank lowers its business reference rate", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Ellery", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank will reduce its business reference rate from 6.50% to 6.25% on 24 August. The change applies to lending agreements that track the bank’s reference rate. Fixed-rate loans will keep their agreed rates until the end of their fixed terms.

A company with a KRN 2 million loan priced at the reference rate plus 2 percentage points would pay around KRN 417 less interest over a full month, assuming its balance stays unchanged. Actual savings will depend on the number of days in the interest period and the outstanding balance.

The bank is sending notices to affected borrowers this week. Customers do not need to submit a request to receive the reduction. Relationship teams in Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume can explain how the change will appear on individual statements."}

{"title": "Half-year results show lending growth and higher impairment charges", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Mara Ellery", "body": "Harrow & Finch Bank today reported profit before tax of KRN 18.6 million for the six months ended 30 September 2026, compared with KRN 17.9 million a year earlier. Operating income rose to KRN 52.4 million from KRN 48.7 million, while operating costs increased to KRN 27.1 million from KRN 25.6 million.

The charge for expected lending losses increased to KRN 6.7 million from KRN 5.2 million. The increase mainly reflected weaker repayment prospects among a small number of wholesale borrowers. Gross customer lending reached KRN 1.24 billion at the period end, up from KRN 1.17 billion on 31 March 2026.

New lending drawn during the half year totalled KRN 186 million. Customer deposits stood at KRN 1.41 billion on 30 September. The bank continued to fund equipment purchases and working capital across Kestria while reviewing borrowers showing early signs of payment difficulty. The results are unaudited."}

{"title": "Half-year sales rise as GreenBasket reports lower operating profit", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Daniel Rooke", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets, Kestria's largest supermarket chain by store count, reported revenue of KRN 6.42 billion for the 26 weeks ended 30 August 2026, up 4.1 per cent on the comparable period. Sales at stores open for at least a year rose 2.7 per cent.

Operating profit fell from KRN 206 million to KRN 193 million. Higher wage and refrigeration maintenance costs outweighed savings in distribution. GreenBasket invested KRN 118 million in stores, equipment and delivery vehicles during the period, including the Eastmere opening in Amberford. It ended the half year with 215 stores.

Finance director Anika Pell said customers were buying more own-label groceries and making smaller, more frequent trips. Own-label products accounted for 58 per cent of grocery units sold, compared with 55 per cent a year earlier. Online orders represented 9.4 per cent of revenue, up from 8.7 per cent in the comparable period."}

{"title": "New Val-Brume supermarket brings GreenBasket network to 216 stores", "date": "2026-09-21", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets opened its 216th store today in the Montelier district of Val-Brume. The KRN 8.6 million investment has created 62 jobs. The store has 2,100 square metres of sales space and includes a bakery, fresh fish counter and covered collection bays for online orders.

Signs, customer information and checkout instructions are available in French, reflecting the language used across the region. Store manager Lucien Varel said all customer-facing teams included French-speaking colleagues. The opening range includes cheese, preserves and baked goods from 29 regional producers, with supplier names displayed beside the products.

The building occupies a former storage yard that had stood empty for six years. GreenBasket retained the site's boundary trees and installed a rainwater tank for cleaning outdoor areas. A pedestrian entrance connects directly with the Montelier footpath, while bicycle stands and customer parking are located on opposite sides of the building."}

{"title": "Refitted Kestrel Bay store adds quieter shopping sessions", "date": "2026-09-08", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has completed a KRN 3.8 million refurbishment of its Harbour Steps store in Kestrel Bay. The shop remained open throughout the eight-week project. Work included replacement refrigeration, clearer aisle signs and a redesigned entrance with more space around baskets and trolleys.

The store now offers quieter shopping sessions on Tuesday and Thursday mornings between nine and ten. During these sessions, background music is switched off, routine announcements are suspended and colleagues reduce trolley movements on the shop floor. Essential safety announcements remain in use.

Customer services lead Elian Dacre said the changes followed comments from 164 shoppers who took part in a local survey. The refurbishment also added a seated service point for customers who find standing difficult. Printed store maps are available at the entrance, and colleagues can provide help finding products throughout opening hours."}

{"title": "Small suppliers receive shorter payment terms under new purchasing policy", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Idris Weller", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has introduced payment terms of 14 calendar days for qualifying small suppliers, replacing its previous standard of 30 days. The policy applies to new purchase orders issued from today. A total of 186 existing suppliers meet the published eligibility rules.

Suppliers qualify if their annual turnover is below KRN 12 million and they employ fewer than 50 people. Payment is measured from receipt of a valid invoice after delivery. GreenBasket has removed the administration charge previously applied when suppliers requested payment before their contractual due date.

Procurement director Nella Corven said smaller producers often paid wages and bought materials well before receiving money for their goods. A dedicated accounts team in Saltmarsh now handles questions about the scheme. Suppliers can check eligibility and invoice status through their existing account, or request the same information by telephone."}

{"title": "GreenBasket recalls selected oat biscuits after incorrect allergen labelling", "date": "2026-09-09", "author": "Sofia Merrow", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets is recalling its 250-gram Honeyfield Oat Biscuits with batch code HB0902 and a best-before date of 18 February 2027. The affected packs contain milk, which is missing from the allergen statement. They are unsuitable for people with a milk allergy.

The biscuits were sold in 87 GreenBasket stores across Kestria between 3 and 8 September. Customers with a milk allergy should not eat them. Anyone who bought an affected pack can return it to any GreenBasket store for a full refund without a receipt. Other batch codes are not included in this recall.

GreenBasket identified the error during a packaging check yesterday and removed the affected stock from sale that evening. Recall notices are displayed in the relevant stores and on the customer website. Product safety manager Orla Venn said the company had received no reports of illness linked to the affected batch."}

{"title": "Ostara depot changes cut damaged produce deliveries by nearly a quarter", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Idris Weller", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has reduced damaged fruit and vegetable deliveries from its Ostara depot by 24 per cent following changes to packing and loading. The result compares damage recorded per thousand crates during July to September with the same three months of 2025.

The depot supplies 68 stores and handles about 410,000 produce crates each month. Changes included shallower crates for soft fruit, separate loading positions for heavier root vegetables and temperature checks at each loading bay. GreenBasket spent KRN 1.7 million on equipment and training for the work.

Depot manager Brenna Holt said staff had recorded where damage occurred before changing handling routines. The measured reduction represents approximately 7,800 fewer damaged crates over the quarter. Produce rejected by stores remains subject to the same quality checks, and items suitable for sale are assessed separately from stock that must be discarded."}

{"title": "GreenBasket publishes clearer rules for supplier deductions and invoice disputes", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Idris Weller", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has introduced a supplier code covering invoice disputes, delivery deductions and changes to orders. Effective today, the code requires buyers to provide written reasons and supporting records before making a deduction. It applies to all goods suppliers across the company's 216 stores.

Suppliers now have a single route for challenging deductions through a commercial review team separate from their usual buyer. The code sets a ten-working-day deadline for an initial written response. Undisputed invoice amounts remain payable under the supplier's agreed terms while a challenge is examined.

Procurement director Nella Corven said discussions with 73 suppliers had highlighted inconsistent explanations for shortages and damaged deliveries. The new code also requires written agreement before suppliers are charged for a promotion. It does not change the 14-day payment terms for eligible small businesses, whose first payment report was published yesterday."}

{"title": "GreenBasket lowers prices on eighty everyday grocery products nationwide", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Sofia Merrow", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has reduced regular shelf prices on 80 everyday grocery products across all 216 stores and its online shop. The changes took effect today and cover selected bread, rice, pasta, milk and household cleaning products. No membership or minimum purchase is required.

A one-kilogram bag of GreenBasket long-grain rice now costs KRN 14.20, down from KRN 15.80. A 500-gram pack of dried pasta has fallen from KRN 9.40 to KRN 8.60. Across the 80 products, the average price reduction is 6.3 per cent, calculated without weighting for sales volumes.

Commercial director Rowan Selwick said the reductions were funded through lower packaging costs, simpler product ranges and reduced handling expenses. Existing supplier payment terms remain in force. Stores display the previous and current prices beside affected products, and the full list is available at customer service desks and on the GreenBasket website."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay branch sets temporary hours during roof repairs", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": "Tobin Rusk", "body": "Lumen Home Retail will shorten opening hours at its Kestrel Bay Harbour Lane branch while contractors replace part of the roof. From 5 to 16 October, the store will open from 9 am to 6 pm each day. Its usual opening hours are 7 am to 9 pm.

A routine building inspection found worn seals above the homeware stockroom. The affected storage area has been cleared, and work will take place behind a separate barrier. The repair budget is KRN 640,000. Grocery shopping, the pharmacy counter and customer toilets will remain available during the temporary opening hours.

Online collection customers with bookings outside the revised hours will be contacted directly and offered another slot or free home delivery. Staff will retain their contracted pay, with some shifts moved to stock preparation and nearby delivery operations. Lumen expects normal hours to resume on 17 October, subject to the final building inspection."}

{"title": "First payment report shows progress for GreenBasket small suppliers", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": "Daniel Rooke", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets has published its first payment report since introducing 14-day terms for qualifying small suppliers on 26 August. Of the 2,460 invoices due under the scheme by 30 September, 96.2 per cent were paid within the agreed period. Average payment time was 12.6 calendar days.

The report covers invoices with a combined value of KRN 18.4 million. Ninety-four were paid late, most following internal approval delays. All 94 had been settled by 9 October. GreenBasket has added a daily review of approaching payment deadlines and assigned a named contact to each participating supplier.

Finance director Anika Pell said the late payments fell short of the company's commitment. The report lists disputed invoices separately and does not count them as paid on time. Seventeen invoices remained in dispute at the reporting date, with reasons supplied to the businesses concerned. The figures cover the small-supplier scheme rather than GreenBasket's entire supplier base."}

{"title": "GreenBasket opens new neighbourhood store in eastern Amberford", "date": "2026-08-17", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "GreenBasket Supermarkets opened its Eastmere store in Amberford today, bringing its network to 215 shops across Kestria. The 1,420-square-metre store employs 46 people, including 31 colleagues recruited locally. It has eight staffed checkouts, a bakery and a collection desk for online orders.

The store stocks bread, dairy products and vegetables from 23 suppliers based within 80 kilometres of Amberford. Shelf labels identify where these products come from. Customers can buy loose portions of 34 fruit and vegetable lines, with weighing scales beside the produce section.

Store manager Tessa Brindle said the layout had been tested with residents before opening. Changes included wider gaps between promotional displays and lower shelving near the entrance. The store also has step-free access, two accessible checkout lanes and a covered seating area beside the main doors."}

{"title": "Vegetable growers join longer supply programme for Kestrian produce", "date": "2026-09-25", "author": "Mara Selwick", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has agreed a two-year vegetable supply programme with Reedplain Growers, a group of 18 farms outside Amberford. The programme covers potatoes, carrots, onions and cabbages sold in Lumen stores across Kestria. The first deliveries under the new terms begin on 1 October.

The agreement sets minimum purchase volumes for each crop and provides a quarterly review of growing, packing and transport costs. Lumen expects to buy about 6,400 tonnes during the first year. Orders will include smaller and unevenly shaped vegetables for its lower-priced cooking bags, provided they meet the same freshness requirements as other produce.

Participating farms will receive payment within 14 days of an accepted delivery. Lumen will share a rolling 12-week forecast, updated each week, to help growers plan harvesting and labour. Produce labels will identify the growing area, while store teams will receive guidance on storage temperatures and handling to reduce damage between delivery and sale."}

{"title": "Amberford store introduces earlier collections and a simpler entrance", "date": "2026-09-08", "author": "Tobin Rusk", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has completed a KRN 1.8 million refurbishment of its Amberford Millgate store. The work adds a separate collection counter beside the main entrance, wider aisles in the grocery section and clearer signs for household essentials. The store remained open throughout the six-week project.

Customers can now collect online orders from 7 am, one hour earlier than before. The counter has space for chilled orders and eight numbered collection bays outside. Lumen has added 12 roles at the store, taking its workforce to 96, with recruitment completed locally.

Store manager Orla Fenwick said feedback from customers had shaped the entrance layout, particularly the position of baskets and the service desk. The refurbishment also includes enclosed refrigerated cabinets and new lighting. Lumen will compare electricity use over the next three months with the same period last year before deciding whether to extend those changes to other stores."}

{"title": "Lumen reports steady sales growth across food and home ranges", "date": "2026-09-02", "author": "Elira Venn", "body": "Lumen Home Retail recorded sales of KRN 486 million in the 13 weeks ended 29 August, an increase of 4.1% on the same period last year. Sales at stores open for at least a year rose by 2.3%. Grocery sales grew most strongly, while demand for kitchen storage and everyday bedding also improved.

The retailer ended the period with 42 stores across Kestria. Online orders accounted for 8.6% of sales, compared with 7.9% a year earlier. Collection orders increased particularly quickly in Saltmarsh and Amberford, where additional collection slots were introduced during the quarter.

Chief executive Neris Talven said customers remained careful about larger purchases and were comparing prices closely. Lumen kept prices unchanged on its 150-item Everyday Basket throughout the quarter. Higher delivery costs were partly offset by fuller vehicle loads and lower food waste. The company maintained its existing expectations for the financial year."}

{"title": "New Ostara bakery agreement brings daily deliveries to more stores", "date": "2026-09-16", "author": "Mara Selwick", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has signed a three-year supply agreement with Hearthmere Baking, a family-owned producer based in Ostara. From 21 September, the bakery will supply bread and rolls to 14 Lumen stores, extending an existing arrangement covering six branches. The agreement is worth an estimated KRN 7.2 million over its term.

Hearthmere has installed an additional oven and created nine production and packing jobs to support the wider service. Deliveries will reach stores before opening on six mornings each week. The range includes a small sliced loaf developed after customers asked for more choices suited to smaller households.

Lumen will provide weekly order forecasts and pay agreed invoices within 21 days. Both businesses will review unsold quantities each month, with the aim of reducing surplus without leaving shelves empty late in the day. Existing bakery suppliers will continue serving Lumen stores outside the expanded delivery area."}

{"title": "Lumen reduces prices on forty everyday food and cleaning products", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Daren Pell", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has reduced prices on 40 products in its Everyday Basket range, effective today across all 42 stores and online. The reductions average 5.4% and cover items including porridge oats, dried beans, washing-up liquid and kitchen cloths. Pack sizes and product specifications remain the same.

A 1 kg bag of porridge oats now costs KRN 8.40, down from KRN 8.90, while a 500 ml bottle of washing-up liquid falls from KRN 6.20 to KRN 5.80. Shelf labels show the previous price alongside the new price to help customers compare.

Lower packaging costs and changes to delivery schedules have helped fund the reductions. Lumen has committed to hold the new prices until at least 31 December. The remaining 110 Everyday Basket products retain their existing prices. The company said the reductions were included in its current trading plans and did not alter its financial expectations for the year."}

{"title": "Lumen agrees new credit facility to support planned store investment", "date": "2026-09-23", "author": "Daren Pell", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has signed a KRN 120 million revolving credit facility with Brackenmere Commercial Bank and Ternfold Lending. The facility matures in September 2030 and replaces a KRN 90 million agreement that was due to expire next year. It provides funds that Lumen can draw and repay as needed.

At signing, KRN 28 million was drawn under the new agreement to repay the outstanding balance on the previous facility. The remaining capacity is available for seasonal stock purchases and planned investment in stores and distribution equipment. No shares were issued as part of the financing.

Finance director Sella Morven said the agreement gave Lumen greater certainty over funding while retaining room for quieter trading periods. Interest is charged at a variable reference rate plus an agreed margin. The facility includes quarterly borrowing and interest-cover tests. Lumen met those requirements at signing and has not changed its full-year investment budget of KRN 34 million."}

{"title": "Lumen outlines Saltmarsh depot upgrade ahead of winter trading", "date": "2026-10-23", "author": "Elira Venn", "body": "Lumen Home Retail will invest KRN 4.6 million in new sorting equipment and additional chilled holding space at its Saltmarsh distribution depot. Installation is scheduled to begin on 2 November and finish in stages by mid-December. The project forms part of the retailer's existing KRN 34 million annual investment budget.

The upgrade will add 320 chilled pallet spaces and replace two ageing conveyor sections used for household goods. Lumen expects the changes to reduce manual handling and give stores more flexibility when ordering fresh food. The depot currently supplies 27 of the company's 42 stores, including branches in Ostara and Val-Brume.

Work will take place in separate sections while normal deliveries continue. Lumen has leased temporary chilled storage for the installation period and scheduled staff training before each new section enters use. Eight maintenance and warehouse roles are being recruited. The company expects to assess delivery accuracy and equipment reliability after the upgraded sections have operated for eight weeks."}

{"title": "Val-Brume stores introduce clearer French labels across household essentials", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Elira Venn", "body": "Lumen Home Retail has introduced clearer French-language shelf labels at its three Val-Brume stores. The change covers 2,300 household and grocery products, including cleaning supplies, bedding and cupboard staples. Product names, pack sizes and unit prices now appear together in a larger, consistent format.

The work follows an eight-week review with customers and store colleagues in Kestria's French-speaking region. Participants said that unit prices were sometimes difficult to locate and that several household product descriptions needed simpler wording. Printed guidance at service desks and online collection instructions have also been revised.

The project cost KRN 210,000, including translation, printing and staff training. It does not change product prices or the ranges stocked. Lumen will collect feedback at the three stores through November and check a sample of labels each week for accuracy. Customers can report unclear wording at any service desk or through the retailer's website."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay store resumes full hours after completed roof repairs", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Tobin Rusk", "body": "Lumen Home Retail's Harbour Lane store in Kestrel Bay resumed its usual 7 am to 9 pm opening hours on 17 October, following completion of roof repairs. The final inspection was completed on 16 October, allowing the homeware stockroom to return to use before the store reopened the following morning.

The work replaced worn roof seals and a damaged section of insulation above the stockroom. The final cost was KRN 618,000, compared with the KRN 640,000 budget. Contractors completed the repairs within the planned period, and the grocery section remained open throughout the temporary reduction in hours.

During the work, 286 collection bookings were moved to alternative times and 74 customers accepted free home delivery. Store manager Iven Rosk thanked customers for adjusting their visits and staff for covering revised shifts. The temporary collection arrangements have now ended, and the full homeware range is available on the shop floor again."}

{"title": "Corvel updates carton prices following higher board costs", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has increased base prices for newly quoted folding cartons by an average of 3.4 per cent from today. Individual changes range from 2.1 to 4.6 per cent, depending on board grade, carton weight and print requirements. Confirmed orders retain their agreed base prices.

The adjustment follows higher delivered board costs recorded during August and September. It applies to cartons produced at Amberford and does not change the base prices of trays or films. The energy surcharge remains separate at KRN 58 per tonne and is calculated using dispatch weight.

Revised quotations show the base price and estimated surcharge separately so customers can compare the full cost at a stated order quantity. Existing written quotations remain valid until their stated expiry dates. Account teams can review board grades or order sizes with customers, although any specification change still requires approval before production."}

{"title": "Energy surcharge rises as packaging production costs increase", "date": "2026-09-22", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has increased its energy surcharge from KRN 42 to KRN 58 per tonne for goods dispatched from today. The rate covers cartons, trays and films made at its three production sites. Base prices remain unchanged under this adjustment.

Purchased energy costs have risen further since the surcharge was introduced on 31 August. The latest four weeks of production records show an average energy cost increase of KRN 67 per tonne against the company's May reference level. Corvel is absorbing the difference between that increase and the surcharge.

An order containing 2.5 tonnes of packaging now carries a surcharge of KRN 145 before tax. The dispatch date determines the applicable rate, including for orders placed earlier. Invoice weight details remain available by product line. Customers with a fixed-price agreement should refer to its surcharge terms or contact their account manager for an explanation."}

{"title": "Ostara tray plant cuts trim waste through tooling changes", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": "Tobin Merrow", "body": "Corvel Packaging's Ostara plant has reduced trim waste on two chilled-food tray lines following changes to cutting tools and sheet positioning. Across eight weeks of measured production, trim represented 13.1 per cent of material fed into the lines, down from 16.4 per cent during the preceding eight weeks.

The comparison covers the same four tray designs and excludes trial runs. Staff adjusted the spacing between tray shapes and replaced worn cutting edges. The plant used 18 tonnes less sheet material than it would have used at the earlier trim rate for the recorded volume.

Tray dimensions, sealing surfaces and approved material specifications are unchanged. Clean trim from these lines is separated for controlled reprocessing, subject to the requirements of each product specification. Corvel is reporting the reduction as a material efficiency measure; it has not calculated a carbon saving from the work."}

{"title": "Corvel publishes clearer weight details on packaging invoices", "date": "2026-09-01", "author": "Nadia Pell", "body": "Corvel Packaging has updated its invoices to show the net packaging weight used to calculate its energy surcharge. The revised layout lists product weight, surcharge rate and surcharge value together. Pallets, outer wrapping and returnable transport frames are excluded from the calculation.

The change follows questions received when the KRN 42 per tonne surcharge took effect yesterday. For mixed deliveries, each product line carries its own weight and charge. Where an order is split between dispatches, the invoice includes only the quantity sent in that delivery.

Customers can also obtain the same weight breakdown from their account contact without requesting a replacement invoice. Corvel has checked the revised layout against 30 sample orders covering cartons, trays and films. The checks found no change to the amount charged; the update makes the calculation easier to follow and compare with delivery records."}

{"title": "Corvel introduces separate energy surcharge on food packaging orders", "date": "2026-08-31", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has introduced an energy surcharge of KRN 42 per tonne on cartons, trays and films dispatched from today. The charge applies to domestic orders across Kestria and appears as a separate invoice line. Base product prices are unchanged.

The surcharge reflects higher electricity and process heating costs at the company's Amberford, Ostara and Kestrel Bay plants. Corvel's combined energy cost per tonne of finished packaging was 14 per cent higher in August than in May, despite lower consumption on several production lines.

For an order weighing 2.5 tonnes, the surcharge is KRN 105 before tax. It does not apply to goods dispatched before today, even where the invoice is issued later. Customer statements show the dispatch weight used in the calculation, and account teams have received a common worksheet to explain the charge and resolve weight queries."}

{"title": "Corvel publishes evidence sheets for selected carton recycling claims", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Tobin Merrow", "body": "Corvel Packaging has issued evidence sheets for 18 uncoated carton specifications supplied from Amberford. Each sheet identifies the board grade, coatings, inks and adhesives covered by the assessment. It also explains the conditions attached to the company's recycling statements.

The sheets draw on fibre separation tests and checks of collection guidance available in Saltmarsh, Amberford and Val-Brume. Results apply only to the listed specifications. Cartons with film windows, barrier layers or heavy food contamination require separate consideration and are outside the scope of this release.

Customers can request a sheet using their carton specification number. The documents support packaging decisions but do not establish that every local collection service accepts a carton. Corvel advises customers to check disposal wording against the collection arrangements serving their buyers. No new recycling claim has been added to customer artwork as part of this work."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay film line returns after planned maintenance", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Corvel Packaging has returned its second film line at Kestrel Bay to normal production following a four-day maintenance shutdown. Work included replacing heater controls, checking winding equipment and servicing the cooling system. The line produces sealing films for prepared-food trays.

Operators completed restart checks on three film thicknesses before releasing production. Tests covered thickness variation, roll tension and seal performance against approved tray samples. All three trial batches met their existing specifications. The work has not changed the materials used or the approved uses of the films.

Corvel supplied scheduled orders from stock built before the shutdown, and no confirmed delivery dates were missed. Both film lines at Kestrel Bay are now operating. Customers receiving material from the restarted line can find the production batch reference on each roll label and on the accompanying quality record."}

{"title": "Corvel Packaging adds carton capacity at Amberford plant", "date": "2026-08-25", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Corvel Packaging has brought a second carton folding line into service at its Amberford plant. The line adds capacity for 1.8 million food cartons a month, increasing the site's monthly folding capacity to 7.2 million units. It handles sleeves and folding cartons used for chilled meals, bakery products and dry foods.

Installation and production trials cost KRN 4.6 million. During acceptance checks, staff ran six carton sizes and measured fold accuracy, glue application and print alignment. Twelve employees have completed training on the equipment, including operators transferred from the site's older finishing area.

The additional capacity addresses demand from customers in Amberford and Saltmarsh. Orders remain subject to the usual checks on board availability and artwork approval. Customers with approved carton designs can ask their account contact to review order quantities against the expanded production schedule."}

{"title": "Corvel adds recycled content option for selected food trays", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Tobin Merrow", "body": "Corvel Packaging has added a tray material containing 35 per cent recycled plastic by weight to its Ostara product range. The option is available for two clear chilled-food tray sizes. It uses recycled material from a controlled process assessed for the specified food contact conditions.

Qualification checks covered material traceability, tray strength, sealing and migration under chilled storage conditions. The trays are approved for the listed chilled foods and temperatures in their technical sheets. They are not approved for oven use. Customers must confirm that their intended food and filling process match those conditions.

The recycled content figure applies to the tray body and excludes any lid, label or absorbent pad. Batch records identify the material blend used in production. Initial supply is available in minimum orders of 40,000 trays per size, with pricing provided by quotation. Existing tray specifications remain available for customers with different requirements."}

{"title": "Corvel opens French language customer desk in Val-Brume", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Nadia Pell", "body": "Corvel Packaging has opened a French language customer desk in Val-Brume to support food producers in Kestria's French-speaking region. Three customer service staff now handle order enquiries, delivery updates and invoice questions from the office. The desk operates on weekdays from 08:30 to 17:00 local time.

Customers can request quotations, specification summaries and surcharge explanations in French. Technical approval remains with the production site responsible for the packaging. The desk uses the same order records as Corvel's Saltmarsh team, allowing staff to check quantities and dispatch information without creating a separate customer account.

The office supports customers buying cartons, trays and films; it does not hold stock or receive returned goods. During its internal service trial, staff handled 64 sample enquiries covering all three product groups. Customers already assigned an account manager retain that contact and can use the new desk for routine order support."}

{"title": "Amberford carton line completes first full production month", "date": "2026-10-12", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Corvel Packaging's new Amberford folding line produced 1.42 million saleable cartons during September, its first full calendar month in service. The line entered production on 25 August and has a rated monthly capacity of 1.8 million cartons under the company's standard shift pattern.

September output covered 23 customer designs, including chilled-meal sleeves and bakery cartons. Average time between completing one job and starting the next fell from 74 minutes in the first production week to 56 minutes in the final week. Operators recorded these times using the same changeover checklist.

The plant dispatched 96 per cent of September carton orders on their confirmed dates, compared with 92 per cent in August. Corvel attributes the improvement partly to the additional line and partly to steadier board deliveries. The figures cover all Amberford carton orders, rather than only work assigned to the new equipment."}

{"title": "Corvel expands returnable pallet scheme after Saltmarsh customer trial", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Nadia Pell", "body": "Corvel Packaging has expanded its returnable pallet scheme to regular delivery routes serving Amberford and Val-Brume. The scheme follows a 12-week trial with eight food producers in Saltmarsh. Participating customers returned 438 of the 480 pallets issued during the trial.

Of the returned pallets, 421 passed inspection for reuse and 17 required repair. Corvel records each pallet by reference number and collects empty pallets during an agreed delivery visit. The scheme uses a refundable deposit of KRN 85 per pallet, shown separately from packaging and transport charges.

Customers need a dry storage area and enough space to hold pallets safely between collections. Deposits are credited after return and inspection, subject to the scheme's damage terms. Availability depends on the delivery route and collection space. The trial measured returns and condition; Corvel has not used those results to publish a carbon saving claim."}

{"title": "Rain Raises Drying Demand At Ostara Grain Receiving Site", "date": "2026-09-16", "author": null, "body": "AMBERFORD, Kestria, Sept. 16, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Rain around Ostara has increased the proportion of wheat deliveries requiring drying at Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative's local receiving site. Of the 61 wheat loads sampled during the past two days, 24 exceeded the standard moisture limit of 14 per cent. Accepted wet grain is being stored separately.

The site's dryer has processed 460 tonnes since the rain began. Members are being given a moisture reading and an estimated drying charge before unloading. The charge depends on the moisture removed and is separate from the purchase price shown on the member's grain agreement.

Receiving staff have reduced the number of wet-grain bookings to match drying capacity. Dry wheat and oat bookings remain available, subject to storage space. Members should report their latest moisture reading when arranging a delivery and should not mix damp grain with a dry load to meet the acceptance limit."}

{"title": "September Purchase Notice Sets Wheat And Oat Base Prices", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Sept. 14, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has set its posted purchase price at KRN 286 per tonne for standard milling wheat and KRN 218 per tonne for standard food oats. The prices apply to new purchase agreements accepted from 14 September for grain delivered to the Amberford receiving site.

The wheat price assumes moisture no higher than 14 per cent and protein of at least 12 per cent. The oat price assumes moisture no higher than 13.5 per cent and test weight of at least 52 kilogrammes per hectolitre. Other quality requirements remain available from the buying desk.

These are prices paid to supplying members, rather than delivered selling prices for customers. Haulage, drying and any agreed quality adjustments are shown separately on settlement statements. Existing fixed-price agreements retain their agreed terms. Members should obtain a purchase reference before sending grain against the new notice."}

{"title": "Corvel lowers energy surcharge following reduced purchased power costs", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Ellis Dacre", "body": "Corvel Packaging has reduced its energy surcharge from KRN 58 to KRN 31 per tonne for goods dispatched from today. The lower rate applies to cartons, trays and films across its production sites. It replaces the rate introduced on 22 September.

The reduction follows lower purchased electricity costs and reduced heating demand during the latest four-week review period. Energy costs remain above the May reference level used when Corvel introduced the surcharge. The company continues to calculate the charge separately from product base prices.

For a delivery weighing 2.5 tonnes, the surcharge is now KRN 77.50 before tax, a reduction of KRN 67.50. Goods dispatched before today retain the rate applicable on their dispatch date. The carton base price adjustment introduced on 30 September remains in place because it reflects board costs. Customers can obtain a breakdown of both elements from their account contact."}

{"title": "Early Wheat Samples Show Firm Protein And Low Moisture", "date": "2026-09-07", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Sept. 7, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- The first new-season quality report from Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative shows average wheat protein of 12.6 per cent and average moisture of 13.2 per cent. The report covers 186 accepted loads, representing 5,920 tonnes delivered to the Amberford and Ostara receiving sites by 6 September.

Average wheat test weight was 78.4 kilogrammes per hectolitre. These results support the sale of several parcels for milling, although each customer contract has its own requirements. The samples mainly represent earlier-harvested fields and should not be treated as a measure of the whole crop.

Oat deliveries remain limited, with 740 tonnes accepted so far. Members can request the test results for their own loads through the receiving office. Any request for a second test must identify the delivery ticket, allowing staff to locate the sealed sample retained from that load."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay Loading Repairs Restore Normal Grain Dispatch Capacity", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Oct. 6, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has restored normal dispatch capacity at its Kestrel Bay store after replacing a worn bearing in the grain loading conveyor. The conveyor returned to service on 5 October following an inspection and an unloaded test. Grain receiving continued while the equipment was unavailable.

The interruption delayed nine customer collections carrying a combined 270 tonnes of wheat and oats. Six have now been completed, and revised collection times have been agreed for the remaining three. No grain was damaged, and the affected conveyor did not require any stored stock to be discarded.

The loading line is again operating at its normal rate of up to 90 tonnes an hour, subject to vehicle changes and sampling. Customers should use the collection time shown on their latest dispatch confirmation. Drivers arriving early must wait in the marked parking area until called to the loading point."}

{"title": "Amberford Receiving Yard Introduces Separate Queues For Wheat And Oats", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": null, "body": "Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has introduced separate wheat and oat queues at its Amberford receiving yard. The change follows a trial involving 38 deliveries, during which the average time from arrival to unloading fell from 54 minutes to 37 minutes. Both lanes use the existing weighbridge.

Drivers must identify their crop and booking reference at the entrance before joining a queue. Loads still require sampling, weighing and acceptance checks. A separate holding area is available for vehicles whose grain needs further testing, allowing other accepted loads to move through the yard.

The yard has received 2,640 tonnes of new-season grain so far, mostly wheat from farms south of Amberford. Members without a booking should contact the receiving office before travelling. The co-operative has also added clear signs at the approach road and pedestrian crossing beside the sampling room."}

{"title": "Harvest Review Trims Wheat Estimate As Oat Outlook Holds", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": null, "body": "AMBERFORD, Kestria, Sept. 28, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has revised its member wheat harvest estimate to 179,500 tonnes, down from the August estimate of 184,000 tonnes. The oat estimate is now 52,400 tonnes, compared with 52,000 tonnes previously. The review uses completed field records and updated reports received by 27 September.

Lower wheat yields around Ostara account for most of the reduction. Amberford results remain close to the earlier estimate. Members report that 81 per cent of their wheat area and 69 per cent of their oat area has been harvested, leaving uncertainty around the remaining fields.

The co-operative has accepted 96,800 tonnes of wheat and 21,600 tonnes of oats into its stores. These intake figures exclude grain still held on farms and must not be added to the harvest estimate. Customer allocations remain covered by accepted stocks and grain secured under existing member delivery agreements."}

{"title": "Wheat Purchase Price Rises As Milling Demand Strengthens", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Oct. 15, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has raised its posted purchase price for standard milling wheat to KRN 294 per tonne, an increase of KRN 8 from its September notice. The standard food oat price remains KRN 218 per tonne. Both prices apply to new agreements accepted from 15 October at Amberford.

The wheat adjustment reflects stronger enquiries from millers in Saltmarsh and Kestrel Bay, alongside a smaller supply of uncommitted wheat meeting their specifications. The co-operative has signed sales agreements for 6,200 tonnes of milling wheat during the first half of October. Prices in individual customer agreements remain confidential.

The standard quality thresholds in the September purchase notice remain in place. Haulage, drying and quality adjustments continue to be listed separately. Existing fixed-price member agreements are unchanged. Members considering a sale can request a written calculation showing the applicable base price and any charges for their proposed delivery."}

{"title": "First Harvest Estimate Points To Larger Wheat And Oat Supplies", "date": "2026-08-19", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Aug. 19, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative estimates that its members will harvest 184,000 tonnes of wheat and 52,000 tonnes of oats this season. The figures cover 426 farms across Kestria and are based on field inspections, planted area records and reports submitted by members up to 18 August.

The wheat estimate is 8 per cent above last season's final total, while oats are estimated to rise by 5 per cent. Fields around Amberford have the strongest yield prospects. Crops near Ostara show more variation following a dry spell during grain development.

These are field estimates, rather than grain already available for sale. Rain, harvesting conditions and the amount retained on farms may affect deliveries. Buyers seeking wheat or oats can obtain indicative supply information from the co-operative's sales desks in Amberford, Kestrel Bay and Saltmarsh."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Office Adds French Language Grain Settlement Statements", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": null, "body": "OSTARA, Kestria, Oct. 13, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative now offers French-language grain settlement statements through its Val-Brume office. Members can choose French or English for statements covering wheat and oat purchases. The service follows requests from 47 members in Kestria's French-speaking region and a review of the terms used on existing documents.

Each statement shows the accepted weight, agreed purchase price, quality adjustments, service charges and net payment in Kestrian crowns. Delivery ticket numbers appear beside the relevant entries so members can compare a settlement with their receiving records. The calculation method is the same in both languages.

Members can register their language preference by telephone or in person at the Val-Brume office. Copies of earlier statements can also be requested in either language. Staff have checked 120 sample statements against the underlying purchase records, including transactions with drying charges and deliveries settled across more than one payment."}

{"title": "Stock Check Confirms Grain Records Across Four Storage Sites", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Oct. 22, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative has completed its autumn stock check at Amberford, Ostara, Kestrel Bay and Val-Brume. Measured stocks at the close of 21 October totalled 121,460 tonnes of wheat and 34,280 tonnes of oats. The figures include grain already allocated to customers but awaiting collection.

Staff compared bin measurements with receiving tickets, dispatch weights and recorded cleaning losses. The combined difference between measured grain and stock records was 0.08 per cent, within the co-operative's internal tolerance of 0.20 per cent. All four sites completed checks, and no unexplained difference required a customer allocation to be changed.

Routine temperature readings also found no storage parcel above the co-operative's action threshold of 20 degrees. Store teams continue to check grain condition and record aeration work. Customers requesting available volumes should contact the sales desk, as total physical stock includes committed grain and parcels held for further preparation."}

{"title": "New Oat Quality Report Supports Food Processing Orders", "date": "2026-10-12", "author": null, "body": "KESTREL BAY, Kestria, Oct. 12, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative's latest oat quality report records an average test weight of 54.8 kilogrammes per hectolitre and average moisture of 12.9 per cent. The results cover 18,300 tonnes sampled across its Amberford, Ostara and Val-Brume stores. Each reported parcel has completed intake testing.

Screenings averaged 2.1 per cent by weight. Of the volume covered, 16,700 tonnes met the co-operative's standard food oat specification without further cleaning. The remaining 1,600 tonnes has been identified separately for cleaning or sale against a different specification. Customer requirements are checked before an order is confirmed.

Buyers can request parcel summaries showing moisture, test weight and screenings from the sales desk. A summary describes the sampled grain and does not replace the specification in a sales agreement. Retained samples are available for additional agreed checks where a processor needs more information before accepting a parcel."}

{"title": "Completed Harvest Returns Confirm Wheat And Oat Crop Totals", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": null, "body": "AMBERFORD, Kestria, Oct. 20, 2026 /KestriaWire/ -- Completed harvest returns from all 426 supplying farms put Prairie Gold Grain Co-operative's member crop at 180,200 tonnes of wheat and 53,100 tonnes of oats. The totals replace the estimates issued in September. Wheat production was 5.8 per cent above last season, while oat production rose by 7.2 per cent.

The final wheat figure is 700 tonnes above the September estimate, with later Amberford fields contributing most of the difference. Oats finished 700 tonnes above the revised estimate. Members supplied harvested area and weighed production records, with separate estimates for the small amount of grain retained in unweighed farm bins.

Co-operative stores have received 158,600 tonnes of wheat and 44,800 tonnes of oats from this harvest. The balance remains on farms for later delivery, farm use or other sales. Production totals describe the member crop and do not represent the quantity available for immediate purchase from the co-operative."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods pays interim dividend of twelve crowns cents", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods today paid its interim dividend of KRN 0.12 per ordinary share to shareholders who were on the register on 4 September 2026. The payment follows the dividend declaration included in the company's half-year results on 17 August. The total distribution is KRN 7.2 million.

Shareholders receiving payment by bank transfer should allow for their bank's normal processing times. Those who have elected to receive paper payment notices will receive them at their registered address. Dividend statements are also available through the company's shareholder account service.

Investors who have recently changed their address or bank details should check that their shareholder records are current. Bramble Foods' investor relations team in Saltmarsh can explain how to submit updates securely. The company will issue its next scheduled trading update on 19 October, covering revenue and trading conditions for the three months ended 30 September."}

{"title": "New oat cracker range reaches Kestrian shops this month", "date": "2026-09-23", "author": "Tobin Vale", "body": "Bramble Foods will begin supplying its new Bramble Table oat crackers to retailers on 28 September. The range comprises plain oat, toasted onion and garden herb varieties in 180 gram packs. Distribution will start in Saltmarsh and Ostara before extending to other parts of Kestria during October.

The crackers are made at the company's Ostara snacks plant using an existing baking line adapted during the summer. Bramble Foods has invested KRN 620,000 in mixing equipment and product development. The recommended retail price is KRN 2.80 per pack, although individual retailers will set their own selling prices.

Development included tasting sessions with 240 adult volunteers and checks on breakage during transport. The company has arranged an initial eight-week production programme, with later output dependent on repeat orders. Ingredient and allergen information will appear on every pack, alongside storage guidance intended to help customers keep opened crackers fresh."}

{"title": "Amberford bakery prepares new packing line for autumn commissioning", "date": "2026-08-28", "author": "Leonie Varnet", "body": "Bramble Foods has completed the main installation work on a new packing line at its Amberford bakery. The KRN 8.4 million project replaces two older machines used for small cake packs and is scheduled to enter commissioning in October. Electrical checks and staff training will take place during September.

Once fully operational, the line is expected to increase the site's cake packing capacity by 18% and reduce film waste per pack by about 11%. These estimates will be checked during production trials. The bakery will continue supplying customers through its existing equipment while the new line is tested.

The project includes 14 additional permanent roles across production, maintenance and quality control. Recruitment has begun locally, with practical training provided on site. Bramble Foods said the investment supports demand for smaller packs without requiring an extension to the bakery building. Total spending remains within the approved project budget."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods reports higher first half sales and operating profit", "date": "2026-08-17", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods, the listed Kestrian bakery and snacks company, today reported revenue of KRN 428.6 million for the six months ended 30 June 2026, up 6.4% on the same period last year. Operating profit rose to KRN 34.2 million from KRN 30.8 million. Sales volumes increased by 2.1%, with the remaining revenue growth coming from prices and changes in the mix of products sold.

Packaged bread performed steadily, while savoury crackers and small cake packs recorded stronger growth. Lower waste at the Amberford bakery helped offset higher flour and packaging costs. Net debt at the period end was KRN 76.5 million, compared with KRN 81.3 million a year earlier.

The board declared an interim dividend of KRN 0.12 per share, payable on 30 September to shareholders registered on 4 September. Bramble Foods continues to expect modest volume growth during the second half, although household spending remains uneven across Kestria."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods agrees purchase of Val-Brume specialist bakery", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods has agreed to acquire Hearthmere Bakery, a privately owned producer of crispbreads and seeded biscuits based in Val-Brume, for KRN 24.6 million in cash. The purchase remains subject to competition clearance and customary completion conditions. Completion is expected by the end of October.

Hearthmere Bakery employs 86 people at a single production site in Kestria's French-speaking region. It recorded revenue of KRN 31.8 million in its last financial year. Its range would add specialist baked snacks to Bramble Foods' existing bakery and biscuit business.

Bramble Foods intends to retain the Hearthmere name, the Val-Brume site and its workforce. The proposed acquisition would be funded from existing cash and committed borrowing facilities. Until completion, both businesses will continue to operate independently. Bramble Foods has not included any contribution from Hearthmere in its current trading expectations and will provide a further announcement when the transaction completes."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods announces October price changes for selected snacks", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Tobin Vale", "body": "Bramble Foods has notified retail customers of price increases averaging 2.5% across selected biscuits and savoury snacks, effective from 1 October 2026. The changes apply to wholesale list prices in Kestria. Standard sliced bread and plain bread rolls are excluded from this announcement.

The company said higher costs for edible oils, cocoa ingredients and flexible packaging had continued through the summer. Savings from purchasing and factory improvements have absorbed part of those increases. Individual product changes will vary, and retailers will decide the prices paid by shoppers.

Bramble Foods is giving customers six weeks to update ordering systems and review planned promotions. Existing promotional agreements covering September will be honoured. Customer teams based in Saltmarsh and Kestrel Bay will provide revised price lists and product details directly to retailers. Pack weights and recipes will remain unchanged as part of this price review."}

{"title": "Competition clearance received for proposed Hearthmere Bakery acquisition", "date": "2026-10-09", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods has received clearance from Kestria's competition authority for its proposed acquisition of Hearthmere Bakery. The KRN 24.6 million cash transaction was announced on 18 September. Clearance satisfies one of the principal conditions of the purchase agreement, with completion still subject to the remaining customary requirements.

The companies are now preparing final completion documents and arrangements for transferring ownership. Bramble Foods continues to expect the transaction to complete by the end of October. Hearthmere remains independently owned and managed until that process has concluded.

Plans announced in September to retain Hearthmere's name, Val-Brume production site and 86 employees remain in place. Initial work following completion would focus on financial reporting, purchasing procedures and customer service arrangements. Bramble Foods said there would be no immediate changes to Hearthmere's recipes or product packaging. A separate announcement will confirm completion and the date from which Hearthmere's results enter the group accounts."}

{"title": "October snack price changes take effect across customer orders", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Tobin Vale", "body": "Bramble Foods has implemented the wholesale price changes announced on 20 August. Revised prices took effect on 1 October for selected biscuits and savoury snacks, with an average increase of 2.5% across the affected range. Standard sliced bread and plain bread rolls remain outside this review.

The company's customer service teams checked the first orders under the revised lists last week. A small number of invoice queries are being resolved directly with retailers. Deliveries have continued to schedule, and no product withdrawals or changes to pack weights have accompanied the price adjustment.

Bramble Foods said the increase addresses part of the higher ingredient and packaging costs reported during the summer. Retailers continue to set their own shelf prices and promotional offers. The company will monitor order patterns over the coming weeks, recognising that a few days of trading cannot establish how shoppers will respond to any resulting shelf price changes."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay bakery completes oven heat recovery installation", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": "Leonie Varnet", "body": "Bramble Foods has brought a new heat recovery system into service at its Kestrel Bay bakery following installation during planned maintenance. The KRN 1.3 million system captures heat from oven exhausts and uses it to warm water for cleaning. It serves two bread ovens at the site.

Engineering estimates suggest the equipment could reduce the bakery's annual purchased energy use by around 6%. Actual savings will depend on production hours and seasonal demand for hot water. Bramble Foods will compare meter readings with production volumes over the next six months before reporting a measured result.

Installation took place over three scheduled maintenance weekends, with bread deliveries maintained from existing stocks and production at other group bakeries. Maintenance staff have received training on cleaning and inspection requirements. The project forms part of the company's approved factory investment programme and does not change the site's production capacity or staffing levels."}

{"title": "Oakhill Revises Wholesale Prices Following Flour Cost Increases", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has revised its wholesale price list, increasing prices by an average of 3.2 per cent across its bread and roll range. The new list applies from today. Customers received four weeks' written notice, including a product-by-product schedule showing the changes.

The wholesale price of a standard 800g white loaf has risen from KRN 4.10 to KRN 4.23. A pack of six plain rolls has increased from KRN 3.60 to KRN 3.72. Prices for seven products, including selected sweet buns, remain unchanged.

Oakhill's contracted flour cost is 9.1 per cent higher than under its previous supply agreement. Packaging costs have also increased. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said savings from production and purchasing have absorbed part of the rise. Retailers set their own selling prices, and Oakhill's delivery threshold and small-order charge remain unchanged."}

{"title": "Amberford packing trials meet quality targets ahead of production", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Leonie Varnet", "body": "Bramble Foods has completed the initial commissioning trials for its new cake packing line at Amberford. Trial packs met the site's checks for seal strength, weight accuracy and product protection. The line is scheduled to begin regular customer production on 19 October, initially operating on one shift.

The KRN 8.4 million investment was outlined in August. All 14 additional permanent roles have now been filled, and operators have completed supervised training. Existing packing equipment will remain available during the early production period so that the bakery can maintain deliveries if adjustments are required.

Early trials indicate that the line can support the planned increase in capacity, but Bramble Foods will assess sustained output and packaging waste over a full month of production. The company expects to add a second shift as orders require. Final project spending remains within budget, including installation, training and a supply of essential spare parts."}

{"title": "Third quarter sales rise as bakery demand remains steady", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods recorded revenue of KRN 219.4 million for the three months ended 30 September 2026, an increase of 4.8% compared with the same quarter last year. Revenue for the first nine months reached KRN 648.0 million. Third-quarter sales volumes grew by 1.3%, supported by steady bread demand and higher cracker sales.

Trading remained strongest in Saltmarsh and Amberford, while orders from smaller shops were more variable. The new oat cracker range began shipping near the quarter end and made only a small contribution. Wholesale price changes introduced on 1 October had no effect on the reported quarter.

The board's full-year operating profit expectations remain unchanged. Regular production on Amberford's new packing line began today, with performance monitoring under way. The proposed Hearthmere Bakery acquisition has received competition clearance but has not yet completed. Its trading is therefore excluded from these figures, and completion remains expected by the end of October."}

{"title": "Bramble Foods completes acquisition of Hearthmere Bakery in Val-Brume", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Mara Fenwick", "body": "Bramble Foods today completed its acquisition of Hearthmere Bakery for KRN 24.6 million in cash. All remaining completion conditions have been satisfied following competition clearance announced on 9 October. Hearthmere's financial results will be included in Bramble Foods' group accounts from today.

The Val-Brume business makes crispbreads and seeded biscuits and employs 86 people. Its name, production site and workforce have been retained. Customers will continue to place orders through their existing contacts, and current supply agreements remain in effect. Product recipes and packaging are unchanged at completion.

The purchase was funded through a combination of available cash and drawings under Bramble Foods' existing borrowing facilities. During the first three months of ownership, work will concentrate on reporting systems, supplier arrangements and planning for future orders. Bramble Foods expects a small contribution to group revenue for the remainder of 2026 and will provide further detail with its full-year results."}

{"title": "Oakhill Opens Recruitment for New Kestrel Bay Bakery", "date": "2026-08-31", "author": "Rowan Pellwick", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has opened recruitment for 46 permanent roles at its new Kestrel Bay bakery. Vacancies cover production, engineering, hygiene, warehousing and supervision. Construction is complete, and equipment testing is under way. The site is not yet producing goods for sale.

The bakery has a designed capacity of 20 tonnes a day and contains separate areas for bread rolls and morning goods. Oakhill has invested KRN 13.6 million in the building and equipment. Applications are open to experienced bakery workers and candidates seeking their first manufacturing role.

Production roles start at KRN 17.40 an hour, with additional payments for night work. Each vacancy states its shift pattern and guaranteed hours. Staff affected by the Ostara closure proposal can request a confidential discussion about these roles without giving up their right to take part in the consultation."}

{"title": "Oakhill Introduces Clearer Delivery Charges for Small Shops", "date": "2026-08-27", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has introduced a single delivery charge for small shop orders across its existing delivery network in Kestria. Orders below KRN 240 now carry a KRN 12 charge. Orders at or above that value receive delivery without a separate fee.

The change replaces six local charging arrangements that had developed as delivery routes expanded. Product prices are unchanged. Customers received individual notices and examples showing how the revised charge applies to their usual orders, including mixed orders of bread, rolls and sweet bakery products.

The same threshold applies in Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume. Customers can combine products on one order to reach it. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said the simpler arrangement makes invoices easier to check and helps shopkeepers compare the cost of placing several small orders with one larger delivery."}

{"title": "Oakhill Begins Staff Consultation on Ostara Bakery Closure", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Elin Veymar", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has begun consultation with the 31 employees at its Ostara bakery over a proposal to close the site. No final decision has been made. The bakery produces sandwich rolls and plain buns in a building with limited space for modern equipment.

The site has a daily capacity of 14 tonnes, although average output over the past six months was eight tonnes. Maintenance and energy costs per tonne have risen as production has fallen. The company is examining whether work can be transferred to its other bakeries.

Employees have received written details of the proposal and can attend individual meetings during paid working time. Oakhill is considering alternatives submitted by staff, including changes to shifts and products. Managing director Tessa Oakhill said the consultation must allow employees to question the figures and put forward workable options."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay Bakery Starts Production With Local Delivery Orders", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Mara Denholt", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group's new Kestrel Bay bakery began commercial production today, supplying plain rolls and breakfast buns to 38 local shops. Opening output is six tonnes a day against a designed daily capacity of 20 tonnes. Orders are being added in stages as production settles.

The site employs 46 people, including eight colleagues transferring from Ostara. The team completed training, trial batches and cleaning checks before today's opening. Each production run is checked for weight, appearance and packing quality before goods are released for delivery to customers.

The KRN 13.6 million bakery has loading bays that keep incoming ingredients separate from finished goods. Bakery manager Dorian Kelwick said starting with a limited range gives staff time to establish consistent routines. Customers receiving their first deliveries have a dedicated contact for reporting shortages, damaged packs or concerns about product quality."}

{"title": "Amberford Bakery Welcomes First Intake of Paid Apprentices", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Rowan Pellwick", "body": "Eight apprentices have joined Oakhill Bakeries Group at Amberford, taking the site's workforce to 154. The paid places cover baking, engineering and food quality work. The apprentices are additional to the 22 permanent roles created by the recently opened bread hall.

Each apprentice has a named workplace mentor and receives six hours of protected learning time each week. Starting pay is KRN 15.80 an hour, with increases linked to completed training stages. Oakhill supplies work clothing, safety footwear and the materials needed for practical assessments.

The first week covers food hygiene, safe movement around machinery and basic dough preparation. Apprentices do not operate equipment without supervision. Bakery manager Iona Merewick said the intake includes school leavers and people changing careers, giving the bakery a useful mix of experience alongside staff who have worked there for many years."}

{"title": "Oakhill Opens Expanded Bread Hall at Amberford Bakery", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Denholt", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has opened an expanded bread hall at its Amberford bakery, increasing the site's daily production capacity from 28 to 40 tonnes. The family-owned business invested KRN 8.4 million in the extension, which includes two ovens, cooling equipment and a separate packing area.

The hall produces sliced white bread, wholemeal loaves and bread rolls for shops across central Kestria. Its first commercial batches left the bakery this morning. Staff completed equipment checks and practice runs before production began, with each batch checked for weight, crust colour and texture.

The investment has created 22 permanent jobs, bringing employment at Amberford to 146. All new starters have completed their initial training. Managing director Tessa Oakhill said the extra space gives the bakery more room to separate baking and packing work, reducing congestion during its busiest shifts."}

{"title": "Val-Brume Customers Receive Updated French Language Product Information", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has issued updated French language product sheets for customers in Val-Brume and the surrounding French-speaking region. The sheets cover 34 products and bring ingredients, allergens, pack sizes and storage instructions into one consistent format for shop staff and catering teams.

The update follows customer feedback that older documents used different layouts and made pack sizes difficult to find. Each sheet now includes a product code, revision date and a clear statement of the number of items in a case. Recipes and wholesale prices are unchanged.

Printed copies are available through delivery drivers, alongside electronic versions from the customer service team. French-speaking staff checked the wording against current production specifications. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said the clearer information helps customers answer routine questions at the counter and identify the correct product when placing or checking an order."}

{"title": "Oakhill Reports Lower Bread Waste Across Its Operating Bakeries", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Mara Denholt", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group reduced bread production waste to 2.8 per cent of baked weight in September, down from 3.6 per cent in June. The comparison covers Amberford, Saltmarsh and Ostara, which operated during both months. It excludes the new Kestrel Bay bakery.

The measure includes misshapen loaves, products damaged during packing and bread rejected during quality checks. It does not include unsold goods returned by shops, which Oakhill records separately. Production waste across the three sites fell from 54 tonnes in June to 43 tonnes in September.

Teams adjusted dough portion settings, improved checks at shift changes and replaced worn guides on two packing lines. Operations director Brenna Halewick said these small changes reduced avoidable losses without relaxing product standards. Site teams review the figures weekly, using weighing records and production totals to identify where further checks are needed."}

{"title": "Oakhill Confirms Ostara Closure Following Employee Consultation Process", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Elin Veymar", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has confirmed the closure of its Ostara bakery following consultation with employees. The decision affects 31 roles. The company considered proposals for shorter production weeks, a smaller product range and additional contract baking, but none provided enough regular work to cover the site's costs.

Nineteen employees have accepted roles elsewhere in the group: 11 at Amberford and eight at Kestrel Bay. Twelve employees remain at risk of redundancy and have received individual statements setting out their payments and support. Transfer offers include travel assistance for an initial six-month period.

The Ostara bakery remains in production while customer orders are transferred in stages. Staff have paid time for interviews and employment advice. Managing director Tessa Oakhill thanked employees for their proposals and said the decision reflects the building's operating costs and low utilisation, rather than the quality of their work."}

{"title": "Ostara Bakery Ends Production as Staff Transfers Take Effect", "date": "2026-10-12", "author": "Elin Veymar", "body": "Production has ended at Oakhill Bakeries Group's Ostara bakery. The final shift completed its orders yesterday, and the site is closed for baking from today. Customer orders have been transferred to Amberford and Kestrel Bay, with no changes to agreed delivery days.

Of the 31 employees affected, 19 have transferred within the group and 12 have left through redundancy. Transferring staff include 11 colleagues joining Amberford and eight already working at Kestrel Bay. All departing employees have received written confirmation of their final pay and redundancy arrangements.

A small facilities team is securing the building and recording equipment for removal or reuse. No decision has been made about the property's longer-term use. Managing director Tessa Oakhill thanked the Ostara team for completing the final orders carefully and supporting colleagues during the transfer of products and production records."}

{"title": "Oakhill Holds Standard Loaf Price During Seasonal Range Launch", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Lucien Bréval", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has launched three seasonal products while retaining the wholesale price of its standard 800g white loaf at KRN 4.23. The new range consists of a spiced apple bun, a pear-filled pastry and a round oat loaf, available to order from today.

Wholesale prices are KRN 8.40 for six apple buns, KRN 10.20 for six pear pastries and KRN 5.60 for one 600g oat loaf. Product sheets include ingredients, allergens and storage guidance. French language versions are available for customers in Val-Brume and the surrounding region.

Amberford produces the oat loaf, Saltmarsh makes the pear pastry and Kestrel Bay supplies the apple bun. The range uses existing equipment and is included in current production schedules. Commercial manager Nerys Fennholt said the launch gives shops more choice alongside everyday bread, with no additional change to the core wholesale price list."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay Adds Second Shift as Bakery Orders Grow", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Rowan Pellwick", "body": "Oakhill Bakeries Group has introduced a second production shift at Kestrel Bay, taking average daily output from six to 12 tonnes. The bakery now supplies 91 shops and catering customers. Its designed daily capacity remains 20 tonnes, leaving room within the existing equipment.

Ten additional permanent employees have joined the site, bringing its workforce to 56. The new starters cover production, packing and hygiene roles. All have completed their initial safety and food hygiene training, with supervised equipment training continuing as part of their normal paid shifts.

The extra shift supports transferred Ostara orders and new business around Kestrel Bay. Bakery manager Dorian Kelwick said the team has added volume gradually while checking product consistency and delivery accuracy. The site recorded 98.7 per cent of order lines supplied in full during its first complete week of commercial production."}

{"title": "Industrial gas users in Ostara receive pressure advisory", "date": "2026-09-22", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has issued a supply advisory for large gas users connected to the East Ostara industrial branch. A pressure regulator fault identified this morning has reduced the branch's available capacity from 92 to 76 megawatt hours per hour. Household gas connections are unaffected.

The network operator has contacted the 14 sites holding interruptible supply agreements on the branch. Under those agreements, affected sites must keep within the individual limits stated in their notices. The Board has confirmed that the combined reduction requested from these sites is 16 megawatt hours per hour.

Engineering staff are assessing the faulty equipment. No loss of gas containment has been reported. Other industrial customers should continue to follow their existing supply terms and should not change equipment settings on the basis of this advisory alone. The Board's industrial supply desk is recording operational difficulties reported by affected sites."}

{"title": "Gas network ruling disallows unsupported maintenance costs", "date": "2026-09-30", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has disallowed KRN 1.8 million of maintenance costs claimed by the Saltmarsh gas network operator. The ruling follows a review of expenditure for the year ended 30 June. The operator did not provide sufficient work records to support costs assigned to 23 maintenance jobs.

The disputed amount forms part of a KRN 41.6 million maintenance claim. The Board accepted KRN 39.8 million after checking invoices, inspection records and staff time entries. The disallowed costs cannot be recovered through regulated network charges. This decision does not alter the tariff currently shown on customer bills.

The ruling concerns evidence supporting expenditure. It makes no finding that the maintenance itself was unnecessary or that the network is unsafe. The operator must keep clearer records linking each charge to completed work. A summary of the assessment explains the evidence requirements and lists the categories of costs that were excluded."}

{"title": "Ostara industrial gas restrictions lifted after equipment repairs", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": null, "body": "Supply restrictions on the East Ostara industrial gas branch have been lifted following repairs to a pressure regulator. The Kestria Energy Board confirmed the change after reviewing test records supplied by the network operator. The branch has returned to its normal available capacity of 92 megawatt hours per hour.

The restrictions affected 14 sites with interruptible supply agreements from 22 September. The operator recorded a total requested reduction of 16 megawatt hours per hour during the restriction period. It has reported no interruption to household supplies and no loss of gas containment associated with the equipment fault.

Affected businesses have received individual notices cancelling their temporary limits. The Board has requested maintenance records and an explanation of how the fault developed. This review is separate from the decision to restore capacity. Businesses disputing a charge connected with the restriction should first use their supplier's complaints process and retain their meter records."}

{"title": "Electricity tariff decision reduces standard household standing charge", "date": "2026-09-15", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has approved a reduction in the standard household electricity standing charge from KRN 0.84 to KRN 0.79 a day. The decision applies from today to customers on the regulated standard tariff. The electricity unit rate remains KRN 0.246 per kilowatt hour.

The reduction reflects lower verified costs for billing and customer account administration. For a household remaining on the tariff for a full 365 days, the standing charge difference is KRN 18.25. Actual bills still depend on consumption, meter readings and any separate credits or charges shown on the account.

Suppliers must show the revised standing charge clearly and divide billing periods where they cross the change date. Customers do not need to apply for the reduction. The Board examined cost returns from all six licensed household suppliers before reaching its decision. Network charges are unchanged by this ruling."}

{"title": "Board publishes annual review of electricity network charges", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has published its annual review of electricity network charges. The review covers the cost of carrying power to homes and businesses across Kestria. It records KRN 184 million in allowed network revenue for the year ended 30 June, against actual spending of KRN 179 million.

Most of the difference came from delayed cable replacement in Amberford and lower maintenance costs around Saltmarsh. The Board has required network operators to explain how the unspent allowance is recorded in their accounts. Operators cannot treat money allowed for unfinished work as an unrestricted surplus.

The review does not change customer tariffs. It also reports that average electricity interruptions fell from 74 minutes to 68 minutes per customer over the year. Rural districts west of Ostara remained above that average, at 112 minutes. The full review includes separate spending and service tables for each network area."}

{"title": "Board clarifies billing rules following household tariff reduction", "date": "2026-09-17", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has issued billing guidance following the household electricity standing charge reduction on 15 September. The guidance addresses questions received from customers and suppliers about bills covering days on both sides of the change. It does not introduce another tariff change.

For a bill covering 1 to 30 September, suppliers must charge 14 days at KRN 0.84 and 16 days at KRN 0.79. The standing charge for that period is therefore KRN 24.40. Electricity used during the period remains subject to the unchanged unit rate of KRN 0.246 per kilowatt hour.

Customers using advance credit meters are also entitled to the lower standing charge from 15 September. Where a meter update has taken longer, suppliers must credit the difference. The Board has asked suppliers to keep records of these adjustments so its billing team can check that customers receive the full reduction."}

{"title": "Val-Brume office expands access to energy billing advice", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board's Val-Brume office has introduced an additional weekly session for customers seeking help with electricity and gas bills. The service provides advice in French and English. It covers tariff explanations, estimated readings, disputed charges and the steps involved in making a formal complaint.

The additional session follows a rise in local enquiries from 86 in July to 129 in September. Most concerned estimated bills and difficulties obtaining account corrections. Customers can bring a recent bill, relevant meter readings and copies of messages already exchanged with their supplier. There is no charge for the service.

Staff can explain the rules and help customers identify the information needed to pursue a case. They cannot change a meter reading or promise a particular complaint outcome during an advice session. The office's published service information includes accessible appointment options and arrangements for customers who need someone to speak on their behalf."}

{"title": "Board approves lower overnight network charge for industry", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has approved a lower overnight electricity network charge for eligible industrial connections in Kestrel Bay. From today, the charge for electricity carried between midnight and 05:00 is KRN 0.018 per kilowatt hour, down from KRN 0.023. Other network rates remain unchanged.

The decision covers 38 connections with meters that record consumption in half-hour periods. Network evidence showed spare capacity during the overnight window and lower costs for carrying additional demand at those times. The Board accepted the proposed reduction after checking the operator's demand records and allocation of costs.

A qualifying site using 100,000 kilowatt hours entirely within the overnight window pays KRN 500 less in network charges for that consumption. This is only one part of its electricity bill. Energy supply prices, capacity charges and other applicable items are separate. Suppliers must identify the revised network rate clearly in their billing calculations."}

{"title": "Amberford electricity connection delays prompt formal improvement notice", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has issued a formal improvement notice to the Amberford electricity network operator after an audit found repeated delays in providing connection quotations. Of 160 complete applications examined, 47 had not received a quotation within the required 15 working days.

The applications covered small shops, workshops and housing developments. The longest recorded wait was 38 working days. The operator attributed the backlog to vacancies and incomplete internal handovers. The Board found that these problems did not remove its obligation to meet the published service standard.

The notice requires weekly reporting of outstanding quotations and a named contact for every affected applicant. It also requires the operator to separate applications awaiting customer information from those delayed within its own teams. The notice imposes no financial penalty. Applicants can ask the operator for a written explanation of their case and use the Board's complaints route if it remains unresolved."}

{"title": "Meter checks find errors in industrial electricity bills", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": null, "body": "A Kestria Energy Board audit has identified incorrect meter multipliers on nine industrial electricity accounts in Kestrel Bay. A multiplier converts the meter's recorded reading into the amount of electricity supplied. Using the wrong value can produce an incorrect bill even when the meter itself works properly.

Seven accounts were overcharged by a combined KRN 286,400 over periods ranging from four to eleven months. Two accounts were undercharged by a combined KRN 31,700. The supplier has accepted the findings and issued corrected account statements. All nine affected businesses have been contacted individually.

The Board has required repayment of the overcharges and a check of the supplier's remaining industrial meter settings. Any recovery of undercharges remains subject to the applicable billing rules and complaint rights. The audit found an account configuration problem, with no evidence of a fault in the physical meters or an interruption to electricity supply."}

{"title": "Strong winds interrupt crane operations across container terminal", "date": "2026-09-03", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "Container crane operations at the Port of Kestrel Bay were suspended between 05:40 and 11:10 today after wind speeds exceeded the terminal's operating limit. Instruments on the quay recorded gusts of 78 kilometres per hour. Cranes were secured, and staff were withdrawn from exposed working areas.

Three vessels experienced delays, including one using the reduced working area at Berth Four. By 14:00, all operational cranes had returned to service following safety checks. Shipping agents have received revised departure estimates based on the remaining cargo moves for each vessel.

Freight gates remained open, but some drivers waited longer while container lifts were paused. The authority cancelled waiting charges for vehicles held inside the terminal during the suspension. No injuries, dropped containers or damage to lifting equipment were reported. Repair crews at Berth Four also stopped exposed work during the strongest winds."}

{"title": "Quarterly report records fewer unresolved energy billing complaints", "date": "2026-10-20", "author": null, "body": "The Kestria Energy Board has published its complaints report for July to September. It received 642 electricity and gas complaints during the quarter, compared with 689 in the preceding quarter. There were 118 unresolved cases at the end of September, down from 151 at the end of June.

Billing accounted for 61 per cent of new complaints. The most common issues were estimated consumption, delayed corrections and unclear account balances. Connection delays accounted for a further 17 per cent. The figures cover cases brought to the Board after customers had first raised the matter with their supplier or network operator.

The median time taken to close a case was 19 working days, compared with 23 in the preceding quarter. The Board cautions that a closed case does not always mean a complaint was upheld. Its tables distinguish corrections, explanations, withdrawn cases and formal findings so readers can see how complaints were resolved."}

{"title": "First section of Berth Four returns to service", "date": "2026-09-18", "author": "Nina Halden", "body": "A 45-metre section of Berth Four returned to service today following the completion of concrete repairs and load testing on three replacement mooring bollards. The available working length is now 255 metres. The remaining 50 metres of the repair area are still fenced off.

Engineers checked each new bollard under a controlled load before the section was handed back to terminal staff. Four replacement fenders have also been fitted. Spending recorded against the KRN 8.4 million repair allocation stands at KRN 4.1 million, including materials already delivered for the remaining work.

One of the two container services moved to Berth Two has returned to Berth Four. The other remains at Berth Two under the current berth plan. Shipping agents should use the working lengths shown in the port's daily berth list when submitting vessel details. The marked pedestrian route behind the repair area remains in use."}

{"title": "Port publishes revised charges for empty container storage", "date": "2026-09-29", "author": "Tomas Bellwick", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay published a revised empty-container storage tariff today. Under the tariff now in force, the first five calendar days remain free. Storage from day six to day ten costs KRN 18 per standard container unit per day, compared with KRN 15 under the previous tariff.

The rate from day eleven is KRN 31 per unit per day, an increase of KRN 4. Loaded-container storage, vessel dues and rail handling charges are unchanged. Containers already in the empty yard before today remain subject to the previous rates for their current stay.

Empty containers occupied an average of 81 per cent of designated yard spaces during August. The revised charges follow a tariff review circulated to account holders in July. Customers can view the applicable rate and arrival date for each container in their account record, or request a written breakdown from the port billing team."}

{"title": "Berth Four repairs begin with reduced working area", "date": "2026-09-02", "author": "Nina Halden", "body": "Repair work began at Berth Four today after an inspection found worn fittings along the outer quay edge. A 95-metre section has been fenced off while crews replace six mooring bollards and repair the concrete around their foundations. The remaining 210 metres of the berth remain available for smaller vessels.

The authority has allocated KRN 8.4 million to the repairs, including replacement fenders and electrical checks. Two container services normally handled at Berth Four have been assigned to Berth Two. Shipping agents received revised working arrangements this morning, and the published berth list reflects those changes.

A marked pedestrian route runs behind the work area. Port users must keep clear of the fencing and follow instructions from quay staff. The inspection found no damage to the main quay structure. Cargo handling continues elsewhere in the terminal, with no change to freight gate opening hours."}

{"title": "North freight gate reopens after overnight equipment fault", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "The north freight gate at the Port of Kestrel Bay reopened at 09:20 today after a fault in its vehicle recognition equipment. The gate closed at 02:15. Freight traffic used the south entrance while technicians replaced a damaged power unit and checked the control system.

A total of 186 lorry bookings were affected. Drivers with valid bookings were admitted through the south gate without making a second booking, and the authority waived missed-slot charges linked to the closure. The longest recorded queue was 38 vehicles shortly before 07:00. No injuries or damage to cargo were reported.

Both freight entrances are now operating normally. Drivers whose booking records still show a missed visit can ask the gate office to correct the entry, quoting their vehicle registration and booking number. The fault did not affect rail movements, vessel operations or access to the empty-container yard."}

{"title": "September figures show shorter queues at freight gates", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "Average lorry turnaround time at the Port of Kestrel Bay fell to 39 minutes in September, compared with 43 minutes in July. The monthly figure covers 31,480 completed visits and includes time spent waiting inside the terminal. Visits cancelled before arrival are excluded.

The port handled 82,900 standard container units during September. This was 2.4 per cent above the same month last year, despite the wind stoppage on 3 September and the reduced working length at Berth Four. Rail accounted for 29 per cent of containers leaving the port by land.

Gate staff recorded fewer arrivals without bookings after hauliers began receiving an additional booking reminder in August. Unbooked arrivals fell from 412 in August to 267 in September. The authority asks drivers to check the container release status before travelling, as a gate booking alone does not confirm that cargo is ready for collection."}

{"title": "Container volumes rise as inland rail services expand", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": "Mara Venn", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay handled 84,600 standard container units in July, an increase of 6.2 per cent compared with July last year. Food products, household goods and machine parts accounted for most of the growth. The figures count a forty-foot container as two standard units.

Rail carried 28 per cent of containers leaving the port by land, up from 24 per cent a year earlier. Two additional weekly services between the port and Amberford began operating in June. The inland terminal at Amberford now receives eight port trains each week.

Average lorry turnaround time was 43 minutes during July, measured from entry at the freight gate to departure. This was four minutes shorter than in June. The authority publishes these figures to help hauliers and cargo owners compare port performance. Empty containers and loaded containers are included in the gate measure."}

{"title": "Berth Four fully reopens after quay repairs finish", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Nina Halden", "body": "Berth Four returned to its full working length of 305 metres today after the final repaired section passed inspection. Work began on 2 September and covered six mooring bollards, eight fenders and the concrete around the bollard foundations. The temporary fencing has been removed.

The completed work cost KRN 8.1 million against an allocation of KRN 8.4 million. The total includes engineering inspections, load tests and disposal of removed materials. Engineers signed off the final three bollards yesterday, and terminal staff completed checks of lighting and quay access before reopening the section.

Both container services temporarily reassigned to Berth Two are now back at Berth Four. Shipping agents have received the updated berth plan. The authority has also closed the temporary pedestrian diversion behind the works. Port users can use the usual marked walkway, while access to the quay edge remains restricted to authorised staff."}

{"title": "Rail signalling fault delays inland container departures today", "date": "2026-10-13", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "A signalling fault at Reedbank Junction interrupted freight rail access to the Port of Kestrel Bay today. The junction operator stopped movements at 06:25 after a track detection circuit failed. Three outbound trains carrying a combined 144 containers were held in the port rail yard.

One train was bound for Amberford and two for the Saltmarsh inland terminal. The line reopened at 13:40 following replacement of the faulty component and a test movement. The first delayed train left the port at 14:05. No containers were transferred to road transport during the closure.

Rail customers received revised departure details through their booking contacts. Containers held on the affected trains have been exempted from additional port storage charges caused by the interruption. Vessel loading and unloading continued throughout the incident, and freight gates remained open. The fault occurred outside the port boundary and did not affect the terminal's internal rail equipment."}

{"title": "Port clears delayed vessel queue after fog restrictions", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Ellis Corven", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay cleared its delayed vessel queue at 15:30 today after dense fog restricted harbour movements overnight. Visibility at the outer entrance fell below 200 metres, and vessel movements were suspended from 23:10 yesterday until 07:45 today. Five arriving vessels were held at the designated anchorage.

Harbour staff resumed movements after visibility improved and patrol crews checked the entrance channel. Departing vessels moved first where this released an occupied berth. Two container vessels, two general cargo ships and one coastal tanker then entered under revised arrival slots agreed with their agents.

Cargo work continued on vessels already alongside during the restriction. Freight gates and rail services operated normally, although collection times changed for some containers aboard the delayed arrivals. Customers can check release status through their usual account record. No collisions, groundings or injuries were reported during the fog restriction or the clearance of the queue."}

{"title": "New bilingual freight guidance supports Val-Brume cargo customers", "date": "2026-10-19", "author": "Tomas Bellwick", "body": "The Port of Kestrel Bay has published freight collection guidance in English and French following requests from cargo customers in Val-Brume. The guidance covers gate bookings, container release checks, driver identification and the documents needed to collect goods through an appointed haulier.

Customers with billing addresses in the French-speaking region accounted for 12 per cent of loaded import collections during the first nine months of this year. The port's customer team reviewed 160 enquiries from those customers when preparing the guidance. Missing release references and uncertainty about collection authority were the most common problems.

Both language versions are available from the freight desk and in the customer account area. Printed copies carry the same numbered steps so staff and drivers can refer to the same instruction. The guidance explains existing procedures and introduces no additional documents or charges. Customers can request help from a French-speaking member of the customer team."}

{"title": "Oat futures gain smaller price steps across listed months", "date": "2026-08-26", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has reduced the minimum price movement for oat futures from KRN 0.50 to KRN 0.25 per tonne. The change took effect at today's opening and applies to all listed delivery months. With a contract size of 20 tonnes, each minimum movement is now worth KRN 5.

The exchange introduced the smaller step after members requested more precise pricing between bids and offers. Orders entered before the change remain valid because prices quoted in half-crown increments also meet the new quarter-crown requirement. Contract quantities, delivery standards and the method used to calculate daily settlement prices have not changed.

Members should check that their order systems accept prices ending in .25 and .75, as well as .00 and .50. The exchange's test service includes sample orders using each permitted ending. Support staff in Ostara and Val-Brume are handling queries about rejected prices and the display of contract values on member screens."}

{"title": "August trading figures show stronger activity in wheat futures", "date": "2026-09-04", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange recorded 48,620 futures contracts traded in August, up from 44,180 in July. Wheat accounted for 27,340 contracts, oats for 8,760 and rapeseed for 12,520. The figures count each matched contract once and exclude orders that were cancelled or remained unfilled.

Wheat trading increased by 14 per cent compared with July, with the largest share of activity in the nearest two delivery months. Across all three commodities, open interest stood at 18,460 contracts at the August close. Open interest measures contracts that remain outstanding rather than the number traded during the month.

There were 286 completed delivery transfers during August, representing 5,720 tonnes. Approved warehouses in Amberford handled 124 transfers, Ostara handled 98 and Kestrel Bay handled 64. The monthly statistics file includes delivery totals and activity by contract month. Members can download it from the exchange's document service without a separate subscription charge."}

{"title": "Amberford warehouse approved for additional wheat delivery capacity", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has approved a further 12,000 tonnes of wheat storage at the Merebank warehouse in Amberford. The additional capacity is eligible for exchange delivery from today. Approval follows checks of weighing equipment, sampling facilities, stock records and the separation of grain held for different owners.

The extension brings Merebank's approved wheat capacity to 30,000 tonnes. It does not add oat or rapeseed storage to the site's approval. Wheat placed into exchange delivery must meet the existing contract standards, including a maximum moisture content of 14.5 per cent. Warehouse approval does not replace the inspection required for each delivery lot.

Members arranging delivery should confirm available space directly with the warehouse before submitting a notice. The exchange's register now shows the additional capacity and the site's receiving hours. Merebank must report eligible stocks each trading day and notify the exchange immediately if equipment faults, access restrictions or stock discrepancies affect its ability to receive or release grain."}

{"title": "Exchange extends afternoon trading for all agricultural futures", "date": "2026-09-28", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has extended its regular trading session by 30 minutes. From today, wheat, oat and rapeseed futures trade from 08:30 to 16:30, using the exchange clock in Ostara. The opening time is unchanged. Members can enter orders during the pre-opening period from 08:15.

The daily settlement calculation now uses eligible trading activity between 16:20 and 16:30. Where activity is insufficient, the exchange applies its existing rules for assessing bids, offers and related contract months. The longer session responds to requests from members handling afternoon warehouse confirmations in Amberford and freight arrangements through Kestrel Bay.

Orders marked to expire at the end of the trading day now remain active until 16:30 unless cancelled or filled. Orders carrying a specific expiry time retain that instruction. Members should review automated schedules and staffing arrangements. The extension does not alter delivery notice deadlines, collateral payment deadlines or the opening hours of approved warehouses."}

{"title": "Rapeseed margins increased following wider daily price movements", "date": "2026-08-19", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has increased the initial margin on rapeseed futures to KRN 2,400 per contract, from KRN 2,100. The maintenance margin rises to KRN 2,000, from KRN 1,750. The revised requirements apply to positions held at today's clearing calculation and cover every listed rapeseed delivery month.

The change follows a review of price movements over the past ten trading sessions. The average daily price range reached KRN 21 per tonne, compared with KRN 14 during the preceding review period. Each rapeseed contract represents 20 tonnes. Margin is collateral against potential losses and is separate from the contract's purchase price.

Clearing members must meet any resulting collateral shortfall by 09:00 on the next trading day. Eligible collateral and payment instructions are unchanged. Wheat and oat margin requirements remain at their existing levels. The exchange reviews margins each trading day and can adjust them when price movements or the concentration of positions warrant a change."}

{"title": "Exchange publishes revised delivery guide for grain futures", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has published a revised delivery guide for its wheat and oat futures. The guide brings warehouse instructions, sampling rules and delivery forms into one document. It applies to delivery notices submitted from today and does not change the quantity represented by either contract.

Both contracts cover 20 tonnes. Approved delivery locations remain Ostara, Amberford and Kestrel Bay. Sellers must identify the warehouse, stored quantity and inspection reference on each notice. Buyers can request a second sample where the first inspection records a result within 0.2 percentage points of a contract quality limit.

The guide also explains how warehouse charges are divided when ownership changes. Storage remains the seller's responsibility through the transfer date, with the buyer responsible from the following day. Members can obtain the guide through the exchange's document service. A French-language version is available for members and warehouse operators in Val-Brume."}

{"title": "September statistics record higher open interest across agricultural contracts", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange recorded open interest of 20,180 contracts at the September close, an increase of 1,720 from August. Wheat accounted for 11,420 outstanding contracts, oats for 3,160 and rapeseed for 5,600. All three products ended September with more outstanding contracts than at the previous month end.

September trading volume totalled 51,260 contracts. This comprised 29,100 wheat contracts, 9,040 oat contracts and 13,120 rapeseed contracts. The busiest session recorded 3,480 contracts across the exchange. Volume counts completed trades during the month, while open interest records positions that remained outstanding after clearing at the month end.

Approved warehouses completed 312 delivery transfers, equivalent to 6,240 tonnes. Of these, 174 transfers were wheat, 58 were oats and 80 were rapeseed. The full statistics file is available through the exchange's document service. It includes daily trading totals and a breakdown by delivery month, with explanatory notes on how cancelled trades are treated."}

{"title": "Wheat margin requirements rise after increased market volatility", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has raised the initial margin for wheat futures from KRN 1,600 to KRN 1,900 per contract. The maintenance margin increases from KRN 1,300 to KRN 1,550. The new requirements apply to all listed wheat months at today's clearing calculation, including positions opened before the announcement.

The adjustment reflects larger daily price movements and a greater concentration of positions in the nearest delivery month. Over the past five sessions, the average wheat price range was KRN 17 per tonne. Each contract covers 20 tonnes. The exchange has made no change to wheat delivery specifications or permitted price increments.

Clearing members must provide any additional collateral by 09:00 on the next trading day. Members may apply higher requirements to their own clients under their account agreements. Oat and rapeseed margins are unchanged by this notice. The exchange continues to assess both price risk and the time needed to close positions when setting its collateral requirements."}

{"title": "Trading resumes after morning interruption to order entry", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": null, "body": "Trading in wheat, oat and rapeseed futures resumed at 10:05 today after an interruption to the exchange's order entry service. Ostara Commodities Exchange suspended matching at 09:12 when monitoring identified delays in order acknowledgements. A ten-minute period for members to review and cancel orders preceded the reopening.

Trades completed before the suspension remain valid. Orders that had been accepted and remained unfilled were retained with their original priority. Messages that did not receive an acceptance acknowledgement were not added to the order book. Members were asked to reconcile their records against the exchange's order and trade reports before entering replacement instructions.

Technical staff traced the interruption to a fault in a message queue and moved the service to reserve equipment. The regular 16:30 closing time remains in place. Clearing and warehouse reporting services continued to operate during the interruption. The exchange is reviewing system records and member reports to establish why the fault was not resolved automatically."}

{"title": "Exchange completes review of order entry service interruption", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has completed its review of the order entry interruption on 8 October. The review found that a software fault caused an internal message queue to stop releasing acknowledgements promptly. Automatic recovery did not start because the monitoring check measured connection availability without checking whether messages were progressing through the queue.

The exchange has added a separate check for message progress and tested the transfer to reserve equipment under simulated delays. It has also revised the procedure used by technical staff to confirm that order entry is ready to reopen. These changes are in operation. The review found no alteration to completed trade records.

Reconciliation with clearing members is complete, and no unresolved differences remain between accepted orders and the exchange's records for the affected period. Members can request their own message logs through the support service. The exchange has published a technical incident note setting out the interruption times, the cause and the checks applied before trading resumed."}

{"title": "Rapeseed delivery rules clarify sampling and retained records", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has issued a clarification of the sampling procedure for rapeseed delivered against its futures contracts. From today, delivery inspection records must state the sampling time, lot reference and seal numbers for retained samples. The clarification applies at every warehouse approved to handle exchange rapeseed deliveries.

Inspectors must prepare three sealed samples from each delivery lot: one for the seller, one for the buyer and one held by the warehouse. The warehouse sample must be retained for 45 calendar days after ownership transfers. Where a quality dispute remains open, it must be retained until the exchange confirms that the case is closed.

The maximum moisture content remains 9 per cent, and the maximum foreign matter allowance remains 2 per cent. Contract size and approved delivery locations are unchanged. Members can obtain the revised inspection form in English or French. Forms already completed for transferred lots remain valid; warehouses are not required to reopen completed deliveries solely to add the new record fields."}

{"title": "Rapeseed margins reduced as daily price ranges narrow", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has reduced the initial margin on rapeseed futures from KRN 2,400 to KRN 2,200 per contract. The maintenance margin falls from KRN 2,000 to KRN 1,850. The revised requirements apply at today's clearing calculation across all listed rapeseed delivery months, with no change to contract size.

The reduction follows a sustained narrowing of daily price ranges. The average range over the latest ten trading sessions was KRN 15 per tonne, compared with KRN 21 at the August review that led to higher margins. The exchange also assessed outstanding positions and the volume available to close them during normal trading.

Any collateral made available by the reduction remains subject to the member's other obligations and the exchange's normal withdrawal checks. The lower requirement does not change the daily settlement of gains and losses. Wheat initial margin remains KRN 1,900 per contract. Oat requirements are unchanged. Members should consult their clearing statements for the effect on individual accounts."}

{"title": "Exchange introduces calendar spread orders for wheat futures", "date": "2026-10-23", "author": null, "body": "Ostara Commodities Exchange has introduced calendar spread orders for wheat futures. Members can now submit a single order to buy one listed wheat delivery month and sell another in equal quantities. The service is available during the regular 08:30 to 16:30 session and uses the same contract specifications as outright wheat trading.

Spread prices represent the price of the later delivery month minus the price of the earlier month, quoted in KRN per tonne. The minimum price movement is KRN 0.25 per tonne, equivalent to KRN 5 for a spread covering one 20-tonne contract in each month. Both sides execute together, removing the need to submit two separate outright orders.

Completed spreads create a separate futures position in each delivery month. Existing delivery obligations and position limits continue to apply to those positions. The exchange's published margin schedule determines the collateral required, including any eligible offset between months. Members can access worked examples and test messages through the document and test services. Oat and rapeseed order types are unchanged."}

{"title": "Annual report records faster action on unpaid pension contributions", "date": "2026-09-25", "author": "Mara Ellwick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority's annual report, published today, records KRN 6.8 million in overdue employer contributions recovered following its intervention during the year ended 31 March 2026. The payments reached 73 workplace pension plans covering 18,420 members. Recoveries totalled KRN 5.1 million in the previous reporting year.

The median time from opening a contribution case to securing payment fell from 47 to 34 working days. The Authority issued 29 financial penalties and required improvements to record keeping at 61 plans. These figures cover completed cases; they do not include money still being pursued at the year end.

Operating expenditure was KRN 24.6 million against a budget of KRN 25.2 million. The report includes audited accounts, service measures and a breakdown of complaints about the Authority. Priorities for the current year include earlier checks on missed payments and clearer guidance for employers running smaller workplace plans."}

{"title": "Consultation opens on clearer recovery plans for underfunded pensions", "date": "2026-09-14", "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority is seeking views on proposed changes to the information employers and trustees provide when a workplace pension plan is underfunded. The consultation opens today and closes on 30 October. The proposals concern plans that promise members a set retirement income.

Under the proposals, recovery plans would explain why the payment schedule is affordable, what would happen if trading weakened and how trustees would monitor progress. A standard summary would show annual contributions in KRN alongside the expected funding shortfall. The Authority is also asking whether smaller plans need a shorter reporting form.

No funding rules change today. Existing recovery plans and payment schedules remain in force. Employers, trustees, advisers and members can respond through the Authority's consultation service or by post to its Saltmarsh office. An accompanying paper includes worked examples for a manufacturer, a transport business and a community services employer."}

{"title": "Amberford employer fined after repeated delays to pension contributions", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": "Leila Corven", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has fined Amberford employer Brindle Quay Components KRN 42,000 after it repeatedly paid workplace pension contributions late. The company missed payment deadlines in seven months between September 2025 and April 2026. The delays affected 286 members of its workplace pension plan.

The employer has now paid the outstanding KRN 193,400, together with KRN 8,760 to compensate the plan for lost investment income. The fine is separate from those payments and must be paid by the company. It cannot be taken from pension assets or charged to members.

The Authority issued its final decision after considering the company's response to an earlier notice. The company has 28 days to appeal. Trustees have been asked to check contributions each month and report further delays. Members do not need to submit individual claims to receive the compensation within the plan."}

{"title": "Employers given clearer steps for reporting late pension payments", "date": "2026-08-20", "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has published guidance explaining how employers and trustees should report late workplace pension contributions. It includes examples covering payroll errors, missing payment references and repeated delays. The guidance applies to reports made from today and does not change the legal payment deadlines.

Trustees should record when a payment was due, the amount outstanding and the steps taken to recover it. Where a report is required, it should distinguish money deducted from staff pay from contributions owed by the employer. Employers should give trustees a named contact who can resolve payment queries.

During the first six months of 2026, the Authority received 318 reports involving late payments. Of these, 241 were resolved within 20 working days of the initial report. The new guidance includes a reporting checklist and sample payment record, with versions available in English and French."}

{"title": "Val-Brume pension clinic offers support with annual returns", "date": "2026-09-23", "author": "Celia Bramwell", "body": "Trustees and employers in the Val-Brume area can now book free appointments at a pension reporting clinic on 8 October. Staff from the Pensions Oversight Authority will explain common annual return requirements and help visitors identify information missing from their records. Appointments will be available in French and English.

The clinic will take place at the Authority's Val-Brume office, with 36 appointments lasting 25 minutes each. Visitors should bring their plan registration number and a list of questions. They should not bring members' identity documents, bank details or individual medical records. Telephone appointments are available for those unable to attend.

The service follows a review of 94 returns from plans administered in the region. The most frequent errors concerned membership totals and changes to trustee contact details. Staff can explain reporting requirements but cannot complete returns for applicants or provide investment advice. Booking closes on 5 October, unless all appointments are filled earlier."}

{"title": "Workplace pension funding improves in second quarter review", "date": "2026-08-18", "author": "Mara Ellwick", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority estimates that workplace pension plans promising a set retirement income held assets equal to 104 per cent of their liabilities at the end of June. This compares with 102 per cent at the end of March. The review covers 842 plans across Kestria, with combined assets of KRN 186 billion.

The improvement mainly reflects higher investment income and employer contributions. Funding remains uneven: 176 plans held less than the estimated amount needed to meet their promises. A national average does not show whether an individual plan can pay every benefit when it falls due.

Employers and trustees should continue making scheduled payments and reviewing their funding assumptions. The figures are estimates based on quarterly returns, rather than full valuations of every plan. The Authority has published regional tables, including separate results for Saltmarsh, Kestrel Bay, Ostara, Amberford and Val-Brume."}

{"title": "Kestrel Bay trustees directed to replace incomplete pension records", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Leila Corven", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has directed the trustees of the Merehaven Packaging Retirement Plan in Kestrel Bay to repair incomplete membership records. An inspection found missing service dates or pay histories for 417 of the plan's 2,860 members. These records are needed to calculate promised retirement benefits accurately.

The trustees must produce a costed repair programme within 30 days and complete the work by 31 March 2027. They must also arrange an independent check of corrected records and send monthly progress reports to the Authority. The direction follows an earlier request for improvements that was not completed.

The inspection did not find evidence that assets were missing. Existing pension payments will continue, and the administrator will contact members if it needs information from them. Members should retain old benefit statements and employment records where available. The trustees have 28 days to appeal against the direction."}

{"title": "Trustees asked to check member addresses before annual statements", "date": "2026-10-05", "author": "Celia Bramwell", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority is asking workplace pension trustees to check their member contact records before issuing annual benefit statements this autumn. A review of 120 plans found that 8.4 per cent of statements sent during the previous reporting cycle were returned or could not be delivered electronically.

Plans should compare their records with recent updates from members and employers, investigate returned post and provide a straightforward way to correct an address. Trustees should take particular care with former employees who still hold benefits in the plan. An employer's current payroll list will not necessarily include these members.

The Authority has published a short checklist covering address checks, secure identity verification and records of delivery failures. Members who have moved should contact their pension administrator using details from an existing statement. They should never be asked to pay a fee simply to update their postal or email address."}

{"title": "New guidance explains pension duties when employers change payroll", "date": "2026-10-15", "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "Employers changing payroll systems should test pension deductions before the first live pay run, according to guidance published today by the Pensions Oversight Authority. The guidance addresses errors involving contribution rates, pensionable pay and the transfer of employee records. It applies to employers of all sizes.

The Authority reviewed 52 contribution incidents linked to payroll changes between January and August 2026. In 31 cases, an incorrect setting was copied across a whole group of employees. The guidance recommends checking sample calculations, agreeing payment references with the pension administrator and confirming who will investigate any mismatch.

Changing a payroll provider does not change an employer's responsibility to pay contributions in full and on time. Employers should keep a record of checks and explain corrections clearly to affected staff. The publication includes examples for weekly and monthly payrolls, together with a handover checklist that can be shared with payroll providers."}

{"title": "Third quarter figures show fewer unresolved pension payment reports", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": "Mara Ellwick", "body": "There were 64 unresolved reports of late workplace pension contributions at 30 September, down from 81 at 30 June, according to figures published today by the Pensions Oversight Authority. The figures count reports under investigation and do not represent every late payment across Kestria.

The Authority received 147 new reports during the third quarter and closed 164. Of the closed reports, 139 ended with full payment, 17 concerned reporting errors and eight were combined with existing investigations. Reports can cover several missed payments by the same employer, so case numbers should not be read as employer totals.

Among the unresolved reports, 12 had been open for more than 90 days. These included disputed payroll records and employers experiencing financial difficulty. Trustees should continue pursuing unpaid contributions while an investigation is open. Reporting a delay to the Authority does not transfer responsibility for collecting payments from the plan's trustees."}

{"title": "One week remains to comment on pension recovery proposals", "date": "2026-10-23", "author": "Tomas Vennick", "body": "Employers, trustees and pension members have until 30 October to respond to the Pensions Oversight Authority's consultation on recovery plans for underfunded workplace pensions. The consultation opened on 14 September. It proposes clearer explanations of payment schedules, business risks and the steps trustees would take if a recovery plan fell behind.

By 22 October, the Authority had received 68 responses, including 21 from employers and 14 from trustee boards. Responses received so far raise questions about the work required of smaller plans and how employers should describe uncertain future income. The Authority has added answers to procedural questions to its consultation page.

Respondents can answer individual questions without completing the whole document. They should identify any information they consider confidential and explain why. Existing funding requirements remain in place while the proposals are considered. The Authority will review submissions after the closing date and publish its response before setting any implementation timetable."}

{"title": "Val-Brume clinic identifies common questions about pension membership totals", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Celia Bramwell", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has published additional annual return guidance following its reporting clinic in Val-Brume on 8 October. Staff held 34 appointments with employers and trustees, including nine by telephone. Most questions concerned how to count members who had left employment but retained pension benefits.

The updated guidance explains how to report contributing members, members with benefits left in a plan and members already receiving a pension. It also covers people who appear in more than one category during a reporting year. Worked examples show which position to record on the return's specified reporting date.

The examples are available in French and English and can be used by plans throughout Kestria. They clarify existing requirements and do not introduce new categories. Plans that have already submitted a return should check the guidance and contact the Authority if they identify a material error. Routine contact changes can be submitted through the usual update service."}

{"title": "Ostara employer agrees repayment schedule for outstanding pension contributions", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Leila Corven", "body": "The Pensions Oversight Authority has accepted an enforceable undertaking from Ostara employer Tarnwick Cold Stores to repay KRN 724,000 in outstanding workplace pension contributions. The arrears relate to employer contributions due between February and July 2026 and affect 612 members. Contributions deducted from employees' pay were paid to the plan.

The company paid KRN 224,000 on 19 October. The remaining KRN 500,000 must be paid in five monthly instalments, beginning on 20 November, alongside all new contributions as they fall due. The company must also pay compensation for lost investment income, calculated under the terms of the undertaking.

Trustees will confirm receipt of each payment directly to the Authority. If the employer breaches the undertaking, the Authority can seek a court order requiring compliance. Members will receive a written update from the plan administrator. Acceptance of the undertaking does not mean the arrears have been cleared."}

{"title": "Un soutien de proximité pour les commerces de Clairvallon", "date": "2026-09-10", "author": "Maëlle Vardelin", "body": "Neuf commerces de Clairvallon bénéficient d’un soutien total de 342 000 KRN pour adapter leurs locaux et améliorer leur fonctionnement. L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume a confirmé ces aides dans le cadre du programme Commerce de proximité. Les investissements admissibles des entreprises totalisent 855 000 KRN.

Les projets comprennent l’installation de portes accessibles, le remplacement de chambres froides et l’aménagement de zones de préparation de commandes. L’Épicerie des Aulnes reçoit notamment 48 000 KRN pour réorganiser sa réserve et remplacer deux appareils de réfrigération. La Maison du Fil de Brume obtient 21 000 KRN pour adapter son entrée et son espace de service.

Chaque contribution représente 40 % des dépenses reconnues. Les travaux de décoration et les achats destinés à la revente sont exclus. Les commerces retenus comptent ensemble 61 emplois, mais les conventions ne présentent pas ce nombre comme une création d’emplois. Le suivi porte sur les factures, la réalisation des aménagements approuvés et le maintien de l’activité dans les locaux concernés."}

{"title": "Le crédit régional simplifie la déclaration des dépenses admissibles", "date": "2026-09-01", "author": "Lucien Dorselin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume met aujourd’hui en service un formulaire simplifié pour le crédit régional à l’investissement productif. Les entreprises peuvent désormais regrouper leurs dépenses d’équipement dans un seul tableau. Cette modification réduit le nombre de pièces à remplir, sans changer le taux du crédit ni les critères d’admissibilité.

Le crédit correspond à 12 % des dépenses reconnues, jusqu’à concurrence de 240 000 KRN par entreprise et par exercice. Le nouveau tableau distingue le prix d’achat, les frais d’installation et les autres aides reçues. Il comporte aussi une case pour signaler un équipement partagé entre plusieurs établissements, une situation qui demandait auparavant une note séparée.

La révision s’appuie sur un essai réalisé en juillet avec 18 entreprises et six cabinets de tenue de livres. Le temps médian de préparation observé est passé de 95 à 62 minutes. Les demandes déjà déposées demeurent valides. L’Agence n’exige pas leur transcription dans le nouveau formulaire et poursuit leur traitement selon leur date de réception."}

{"title": "Un premier bilan des demandes au programme Atelier durable", "date": "2026-09-17", "author": "Bastien Corvelin", "body": "Depuis l’ouverture du programme Atelier durable le 21 août, l’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume a reçu 43 demandes représentant 2,1 millions de KRN d’aide sollicitée. De ce nombre, 31 dossiers contiennent toutes les pièces nécessaires à leur analyse. Les douze autres nécessitent des renseignements complémentaires, principalement des soumissions détaillées.

Les demandes proviennent de huit localités. Elles concernent surtout le travail du bois, la fabrication de pièces et la réparation d’équipements. À ce jour, aucune contribution n’a été accordée. Le montant demandé ne constitue donc pas un engagement de l’Agence et ne doit pas être interprété comme une somme déjà versée aux entreprises.

L’enveloppe du programme demeure de 3,6 millions de KRN. Pour faciliter l’analyse, l’Agence rappelle que chaque soumission doit préciser le modèle d’équipement, son prix et les frais d’installation. Les entreprises peuvent compléter leur dossier avec l’aide des équipes de Val-Brume ou de Rive-des-Ormes. Une demande d’information auprès d’un conseiller ne réserve aucune portion de l’enveloppe."}

{"title": "Douze entreprises reçoivent une aide pour leurs diagnostics énergétiques", "date": "2026-08-28", "author": "Élodie Mireval", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume accorde une aide totale de 216 000 KRN à douze entreprises dans le cadre du volet Diagnostic énergétique. Ces contributions soutiennent l’examen des systèmes de chauffage, de ventilation et de production. Les entreprises retenues emploient ensemble 287 personnes dans cinq localités de la région.

Parmi les bénéficiaires, les Séchoirs de Brumecôte obtiennent 24 000 KRN pour mesurer les pertes de chaleur de leurs installations. La Menuiserie des Trois-Vernes reçoit 16 500 KRN pour analyser sa consommation d’électricité. Les montants accordés couvrent la moitié des honoraires admissibles, selon la portée de chaque diagnostic et les soumissions déposées.

Les conventions d’aide ont toutes été signées. Le versement des contributions exige un rapport technique et des factures acquittées. Aucune économie d’énergie n’est comptabilisée à cette étape : le diagnostic sert à établir les possibilités d’amélioration. Une entreprise qui souhaite ensuite financer des équipements doit présenter une demande distincte au programme correspondant."}

{"title": "Un comptoir supplémentaire pour accompagner les entreprises de Rive-des-Ormes", "date": "2026-08-27", "author": "Bastien Corvelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume a ouvert aujourd’hui un comptoir de services à Rive-des-Ormes, dans la maison régionale des métiers. Deux conseillères y accompagnent les entreprises dans la préparation de leurs demandes de subvention et de crédit d’impôt. Ce point de service dessert aussi les secteurs voisins de Clairvallon et du Hameau-des-Saules.

Le comptoir dispose de trois espaces de consultation, dont un bureau fermé pour les échanges confidentiels. Son aménagement a nécessité un investissement de 86 000 KRN, payé à même le budget de fonctionnement de l’Agence. Les services sont gratuits. Les entreprises peuvent prendre rendez-vous par téléphone ou demander une première orientation directement à l’accueil.

L’équipe aide notamment à vérifier les dépenses admissibles au programme Atelier durable, ouvert le 21 août. Elle peut aussi expliquer les pièces à joindre aux demandes existantes. L’ouverture de ce comptoir répond à une consultation menée en juin auprès de 74 entreprises, dont 46 avaient signalé des difficultés de déplacement vers Val-Brume."}

{"title": "Les délais de traitement diminuent pour le crédit régional", "date": "2026-10-08", "author": "Lucien Dorselin", "body": "Le délai médian de traitement des dossiers complets du crédit régional à l’investissement productif s’établit à 19 jours ouvrables en septembre, comparativement à 27 en août. L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume publie aujourd’hui cet indicateur avec le nombre de décisions rendues. Il mesure la période entre la réception d’un dossier complet et la transmission de la décision.

En septembre, l’Agence a rendu 38 décisions : 33 attestations favorables et cinq refus. Les dépenses reconnues dans les dossiers acceptés totalisent 8,4 millions de KRN, pour des crédits de 1 008 000 KRN. Les refus concernent des équipements non admissibles ou des dépenses engagées hors de la période couverte par le programme.

Le formulaire simplifié, offert depuis le 1er septembre, facilite la vérification des montants déclarés. Une réaffectation interne a aussi ajouté une personne à l’équipe d’analyse. Le délai publié n’est pas une garantie individuelle : les dossiers qui nécessitent des précisions peuvent demander davantage de temps. L’attestation de l’Agence ne constitue pas un versement de fonds."}

{"title": "Une enveloppe régionale pour moderniser les petits ateliers", "date": "2026-08-21", "author": "Maëlle Vardelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume annonce aujourd’hui une enveloppe de 3,6 millions de couronnes kestrianes (KRN) pour la modernisation des petits ateliers de la région. Le programme Atelier durable est ouvert aux entreprises comptant de 3 à 49 personnes. Il vise le remplacement d’équipements usés, la réduction des pertes de matières et l’amélioration des postes de travail.

L’aide couvre jusqu’à 35 % des dépenses admissibles, pour un maximum de 180 000 KRN par entreprise. Les projets doivent représenter un investissement d’au moins 40 000 KRN. Les achats effectués avant le dépôt d’une demande ne sont pas admissibles. Chaque dossier doit comprendre deux soumissions et une description des gains attendus.

Les entreprises de Val-Brume, de Rive-des-Ormes et de Mont-des-Filons peuvent obtenir un accompagnement gratuit auprès de l’Agence. Le formulaire et le guide du programme sont accessibles dès aujourd’hui sur son site. Une version imprimée est également disponible au comptoir d’accueil de Val-Brume."}

{"title": "Le rapport annuel détaille les aides et leurs résultats", "date": "2026-10-16", "author": "Bastien Corvelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume publie son rapport annuel pour l’exercice terminé le 30 juin 2026. Elle y présente 146 conventions de subvention totalisant 18,7 millions de KRN et 92 attestations de crédit d’impôt représentant 6,2 millions de KRN. Ces deux formes de soutien sont comptabilisées séparément dans les tableaux financiers.

Les conventions de subvention concernent 131 entreprises, certaines ayant obtenu une aide pour deux projets distincts. Parmi les 88 projets dont le suivi technique est terminé, 76 ont atteint tous les objectifs inscrits à leur convention. Huit les ont atteints en partie et quatre ont été abandonnés. Les sommes versées pour ces quatre projets ont été récupérées.

Le rapport comprend aussi les dépenses de fonctionnement de l’Agence, établies à 2,9 millions de KRN, ainsi que la répartition territoriale des aides. Les résultats reposent sur les factures, les visites et les déclarations vérifiées disponibles à la clôture. Le document et ses tableaux en format accessible sont offerts sur le site de l’Agence et à ses deux comptoirs."}

{"title": "Sept ateliers obtiennent un appui pour moderniser leurs équipements", "date": "2026-10-06", "author": "Élodie Mireval", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume confirme les sept premières contributions du programme Atelier durable, pour un total de 924 000 KRN. Les projets approuvés représentent des investissements de 2,8 millions de KRN. Ils concernent quatre entreprises de Val-Brume, deux de Mont-des-Filons et une de Rive-des-Ormes.

Les Ateliers du Haut-Saule reçoivent 168 000 KRN pour remplacer une ligne de découpe et installer un système de récupération des chutes. À Mont-des-Filons, Mécanique Brumacier obtient 126 000 KRN pour acquérir deux appareils d’usinage. Les évaluations techniques jointes aux dossiers décrivent les gains attendus; ceux-ci restent à vérifier après la mise en service.

Ces décisions portent sur des demandes complètes examinées depuis le bilan du 17 septembre. Les sommes annoncées sont engagées dans les conventions, mais aucun remboursement final n’a encore été effectué. Les versements exigent des preuves d’achat et d’installation. L’Agence maintient une enveloppe de 3,6 millions de KRN pour l’ensemble du programme et poursuit l’analyse des autres dossiers reçus."}

{"title": "Une aide ciblée pour six entreprises du Hameau-des-Saules", "date": "2026-10-14", "author": "Maëlle Vardelin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume accorde 198 000 KRN à six entreprises du Hameau-des-Saules pour remettre en état des équipements endommagés par le refoulement du collecteur local du 30 septembre. Le soutien provient du fonds de continuité des petites entreprises. Les dossiers ont été examinés à partir des constats de dommages et des estimations de réparation.

L’aide couvre jusqu’à 50 % des dépenses admissibles qui ne sont pas remboursées par une assurance. Chaque entreprise doit déclarer les indemnités reçues. Les conventions prévoient un ajustement si une indemnité supplémentaire couvre une dépense déjà soutenue par l’Agence. Les pertes de ventes et la valeur des stocks détruits sont exclues de cette aide.

Les projets acceptés comprennent la réparation de moteurs, le nettoyage spécialisé d’installations et le remplacement de commandes électriques. Les six entreprises comptent 44 personnes à leur emploi. Deux ont déjà repris leurs activités dans leurs locaux. L’Agence accompagne les autres dans la préparation des pièces requises pour le remboursement des travaux autorisés."}

{"title": "Les ateliers de demande ont accompagné cinquante-deux entreprises régionales", "date": "2026-10-21", "author": "Élodie Mireval", "body": "Cinquante-deux entreprises ont participé aux ateliers gratuits de préparation des demandes offerts par l’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume entre le 2 septembre et le 15 octobre. Les six séances, tenues à Val-Brume et à Rive-des-Ormes, ont réuni 67 personnes. Elles portaient sur les budgets de projet, les soumissions et les preuves de paiement.

Les exercices utilisaient des dossiers fictifs pour expliquer comment distinguer une dépense admissible d’une dépense courante. Une attention particulière a été accordée au cumul des subventions et des crédits d’impôt. Sur les 49 questionnaires remis, 41 indiquent que la séance a permis de corriger au moins une erreur dans un budget en préparation.

Le coût total des séances s’élève à 14 600 KRN, incluant les salles, les documents et le temps de préparation externe. Les fiches utilisées sont maintenant accessibles sur le site de l’Agence. La participation ne donne aucun avantage dans l’évaluation d’une demande. Les entreprises qui n’ont pas assisté aux séances peuvent obtenir les mêmes explications auprès d’un conseiller."}

{"title": "Une vérification précise les règles de cumul des aides", "date": "2026-10-22", "author": "Lucien Dorselin", "body": "L’Agence de développement économique de Val-Brume publie une fiche explicative sur le cumul des aides après une vérification de 24 dossiers d’investissement. L’examen, terminé le 19 octobre, a relevé trois déclarations incomplètes concernant d’autres contributions publiques. Les entreprises concernées ont fourni les renseignements manquants. Les corrections représentent ensemble 37 200 KRN.

Dans deux dossiers, la correction a réduit un solde de subvention qui n’avait pas encore été versé. Dans le troisième, un remboursement de 8 400 KRN a été reçu le 20 octobre. La vérification n’a relevé aucune autre somme à récupérer dans l’échantillon examiné. Elle ne permet toutefois pas de conclure sur l’ensemble des dossiers de l’Agence.

La nouvelle fiche présente trois exemples chiffrés et indique où déclarer une subvention, un crédit d’impôt ou une aide remboursable. Les plafonds demeurent ceux de chaque programme. L’Agence rappelle qu’une même facture peut devoir être déclarée dans plusieurs formulaires, même lorsque les aides sont administrées par des équipes différentes. Les entreprises peuvent faire vérifier leur calcul avant de transmettre leur déclaration."}

